1
00:00:00,188 --> 00:00:01,898
..aSPens quiver,

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:04,901
Little breezes, dusk and shiver.

3
00:00:05,193 --> 00:00:07,904
Through the wave that runs forever.

4
00:00:08,196 --> 00:00:10,323
By the island in the river.

5
00:00:10,615 --> 00:00:13,910
Flowing down to Camelot.

6
00:00:14,202 --> 00:00:17,914
Four gray walls and four gray towers.

7
00:00:18,206 --> 00:00:19,916
Overlook a space of flowers.

8
00:00:20,208 --> 00:00:22,919
And the silent isle imbowers

9
00:00:23,211 --> 00:00:26,381
"the lady of Shalott."

10
00:00:28,716 --> 00:00:36,716
(Music)

11
00:00:39,227 --> 00:00:41,938
"Piling sheaves In uplands airy,

12
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:45,024
Listening, whispers "tis the fairy".

13
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:50,029
Lady of Shalott:

14
00:00:52,115 --> 00:00:55,952
There she weaves by night and day.

15
00:00:56,244 --> 00:00:59,956
A magic web with colors gay.

16
00:01:00,248 --> 00:01:02,959
She has heard a whisper Say,

17
00:01:03,251 --> 00:01:05,962
A curse is on her if she stay.

18
00:01:06,254 --> 00:01:09,048
To look down on Camelot.

19
00:01:14,137 --> 00:01:15,972
She knows not what the curse may be,

20
00:01:16,264 --> 00:01:18,975
And so she weaveth steadily,

21
00:01:19,267 --> 00:01:22,687
And little other care hath she...

22
00:01:22,979 --> 00:01:25,398
"The lady of Shalott."

23
00:01:26,149 --> 00:01:27,942
Mrs. Hammond: Anne!

24
00:01:28,443 --> 00:01:29,944
Anne!

25
00:01:30,236 --> 00:01:33,072
Coming Mrs. Hammond!

26
00:01:35,116 --> 00:01:36,993
Anne! Anne Shirley! (Baby crying)

27
00:01:37,285 --> 00:01:39,996
Get in here this instant!

28
00:01:40,288 --> 00:01:42,373
It's all right, honey.

29
00:01:43,541 --> 00:01:45,877
Go on, get in, get!

30
00:01:46,794 --> 00:01:48,796
(Baby screaming)

31
00:01:50,173 --> 00:01:52,008
Oh, watch it you sloppy girl!

32
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:55,011
That comes right out of my babies' mouths.

33
00:01:55,303 --> 00:01:56,280
I'm sorry Mrs. Hammond.

34
00:01:56,304 --> 00:01:58,014
But | was rushing so and it's quite heavy.

35
00:01:58,306 --> 00:01:59,283
But that will be my share,

36
00:01:59,307 --> 00:02:00,492
so there won't be any less for the children.

37
00:02:00,516 --> 00:02:03,019
Oh, will you just take him and clean him up.

38
00:02:03,311 --> 00:02:05,021
(Baby crying)

39
00:02:05,313 --> 00:02:08,024
Well, if you'd pay more attention to your chores

40
00:02:08,316 --> 00:02:12,028
instead over pourin' over them fool books of yours.

41
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,030
Please, | won't do it again.

42
00:02:14,322 --> 00:02:17,033
It was just so thrilling | couldn't put it down.

43
00:02:17,325 --> 00:02:20,119
Oh you darn well won't do it again!

44
00:02:24,207 --> 00:02:27,043
And if | catch you readin' any more of them books of yours

45
00:02:27,335 --> 00:02:29,313
while your supposed to be looking after my youngins

46
00:02:29,337 --> 00:02:31,047
they'll feed the fire too, Missy!

47
00:02:31,339 --> 00:02:33,049
Well, don't stand there lookin' daft;

48
00:02:33,341 --> 00:02:36,135
finish changin' Meg n' Peter.

49
00:02:38,221 --> 00:02:39,323
Mr. Hammond and the men been waitin'

50
00:02:39,347 --> 00:02:41,057
well nigh an hour for their lunch,

51
00:02:41,349 --> 00:02:43,142
while you been dawdlin'.

52
00:02:43,893 --> 00:02:45,645
| enjoy babies in moderation Mrs. Hammond,

53
00:02:46,354 --> 00:02:48,981
but twins three times in succession is too much!

54
00:02:49,774 --> 00:02:52,336
- What!
- | simply couldn't live here if | hadn't any imagination.

55
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:54,080
I'll take none of your cheek, Anne Shirley.

56
00:02:54,362 --> 00:02:56,122
Believe you me you'll be out on your backside

57
00:02:56,364 --> 00:02:59,575
if | get another word out of you!

58
00:03:00,368 --> 00:03:01,869
Oh, go on get going to the mill

59
00:03:02,161 --> 00:03:05,248
before Mr. Hammond takes a whippin' to you. Get!

60
00:03:05,707 --> 00:03:08,000
(Babies crying)

61
00:03:09,168 --> 00:03:10,336
Eat!

62
00:03:11,087 --> 00:03:13,631
(Machine chugs)

63
00:03:18,261 --> 00:03:21,180
Not those gull darn planks!

64
00:03:28,646 --> 00:03:32,358
What's the matter? Not that junk, idiot!

65
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:34,444
Cut it out!

66
00:03:41,117 --> 00:03:42,493
Help! Get out here!

67
00:03:44,287 --> 00:03:46,122
What happened, Tom?

68
00:03:46,414 --> 00:03:47,391
He's been in a temper over lunch.

69
00:03:47,415 --> 00:03:49,135
Screamin' and swearin. You know how he gets.

70
00:03:49,417 --> 00:03:51,127
He wouldn't stop.

71
00:03:51,419 --> 00:03:54,547
Someone take the wagon and go for the doctor!

72
00:03:56,299 --> 00:03:59,218
He won't be needin' no doctor.

73
00:04:10,313 --> 00:04:13,357
Katie, | know you understand,

74
00:04:13,649 --> 00:04:17,153
but if | hadn't lost myself in the beauty of the day,

75
00:04:17,445 --> 00:04:21,115
the only beauty which is vouchsafed me...

76
00:04:21,407 --> 00:04:24,243
Poor Mr. Hammond might still be with us.

77
00:04:25,703 --> 00:04:27,622
(Music)

78
00:04:33,336 --> 00:04:35,254
(Weeping)

79
00:04:35,963 --> 00:04:38,257
There, there Norah.

80
00:04:39,342 --> 00:04:41,260
He led a good life.

81
00:04:43,346 --> 00:04:45,546
You have to think about yourself and your young'uns now.

82
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:47,266
(Sobs)

83
00:04:48,559 --> 00:04:52,271
Sell the Mill and come and live with me.

84
00:04:53,731 --> 00:04:56,192
And what about the girl?

85
00:04:56,484 --> 00:04:58,444
She's a home child, isn't she?

86
00:04:58,736 --> 00:05:00,279
Yeah.

87
00:05:01,364 --> 00:05:04,242
She'll have to go back to the orphanage.

88
00:05:09,372 --> 00:05:12,208
Mrs. Hammond you must know how much

89
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:15,211
| want to be of help to you in your time of trial.

90
00:05:15,503 --> 00:05:18,965
| consider it a burden | must bear.

91
00:05:19,507 --> 00:05:22,218
| was daft when | took you in.

92
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:24,220
It's all your doin.

93
00:05:24,512 --> 00:05:27,223
None but yours.

94
00:05:27,515 --> 00:05:29,433
| blame myself entirely, Mrs. Hammond.

95
00:05:29,725 --> 00:05:31,325
To have to wait and extra hour for lunch

96
00:05:31,519 --> 00:05:33,229
is a terrible burden on any man.

97
00:05:33,521 --> 00:05:36,232
And | shall never overcome my grief.

98
00:05:36,524 --> 00:05:39,235
But going back to an orphanage would be more than | could bear.

99
00:05:39,527 --> 00:05:43,197
| beg of you, Mrs. Hammond, please let me stay with you.

100
00:05:43,489 --> 00:05:45,241
Orphan children are all the same.

101
00:05:45,533 --> 00:05:46,576
Trash.

102
00:05:48,411 --> 00:05:51,247
- Trash.
- That's right, Anne Shirley.

103
00:05:51,539 --> 00:05:55,334
Poor miserable trash that don't deserve no better.

104
00:06:00,423 --> 00:06:02,341
(Door creaks shut)

105
00:06:05,970 --> 00:06:13,970
(Music)

106
00:06:18,566 --> 00:06:20,359
(Door opens)

107
00:06:28,451 --> 00:06:31,370
Mrs. Hammond, ma'am.

108
00:06:33,456 --> 00:06:38,294
Mrs. Hammond | sent a reply to your letter just this morning.

109
00:06:38,586 --> 00:06:40,296
I'm afraid we cannot take the girl.

110
00:06:40,588 --> 00:06:43,299
We are overcrowded as it Is.

111
00:06:43,591 --> 00:06:45,551
But I've already had to divide my own sweet babies

112
00:06:45,593 --> 00:06:48,304
among my relatives, Ma'am.

113
00:06:48,596 --> 00:06:50,306
She ain't my responsibility no more.

114
00:06:50,598 --> 00:06:53,309
You have to take her.

115
00:06:53,601 --> 00:06:55,603
Come here, child.

116
00:06:57,480 --> 00:06:59,315
Tell me what you know about yourself.

117
00:06:59,607 --> 00:07:02,318
Well, it really isn't worth telling Mrs. Cadbury.

118
00:07:02,610 --> 00:07:04,770
But if you let me tell you what | imagine about myself,

119
00:07:04,904 --> 00:07:06,384
you'd find it a lot more interesting.

120
00:07:06,614 --> 00:07:10,326
Uh... she was, uh... twelve last March, ma'am.

121
00:07:10,618 --> 00:07:12,328
Uh, born in Halifax.

122
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,331
Both parents died of the fever when she was just three months.

123
00:07:15,623 --> 00:07:17,333
| took her in from a neighbor last year

124
00:07:17,625 --> 00:07:19,335
to help out with the young'uns,

125
00:07:19,627 --> 00:07:21,420
but she's been in and outta orphanages

126
00:07:21,712 --> 00:07:22,606
ever since she was a wee thing

127
00:07:22,630 --> 00:07:24,340
and she's not too proud for here.

128
00:07:24,632 --> 00:07:26,342
And what were your parents names?

129
00:07:26,634 --> 00:07:29,345
Walter and Bertha Shirley.

130
00:07:29,637 --> 00:07:30,614
Aren't they lovely names?

131
00:07:30,638 --> 00:07:32,348
I'm proud they had such nice names.

132
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:35,351
It would be such a disgrace to have a father called

133
00:07:35,643 --> 00:07:36,620
well, HEZEKIAH!

134
00:07:36,644 --> 00:07:38,354
It doesn't matter what a person's name is

135
00:07:38,646 --> 00:07:40,356
as long as they behave themselves.

136
00:07:40,648 --> 00:07:42,358
Well, | don't know.

137
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:44,628
| read in a book once that a rose by any other name

138
00:07:44,652 --> 00:07:47,363
would smell as sweet but I've never been able to believe it.

139
00:07:47,655 --> 00:07:49,633
A rose just couldn't smell as sweet if it were a thistle

140
00:07:49,657 --> 00:07:50,657
or a skunk cabbage.

141
00:07:50,741 --> 00:07:53,369
| don't know where she picks up them fool ideas;

142
00:07:53,661 --> 00:07:56,372
but she's a real bright little thing, ain't she?

143
00:07:56,664 --> 00:07:59,000
And she won't be no trouble to you | can promise that.

144
00:07:59,291 --> 00:08:03,379
Hah. Well, this is a real Christian place you folks is runnin' here

145
00:08:03,671 --> 00:08:06,382
and | sure am grateful to you for helpin' me outta this predicament.

146
00:08:06,674 --> 00:08:08,474
Now Mrs. Hammond, wait a minute. Mrs. Hammond!

147
00:08:08,676 --> 00:08:11,387
We can't take her for at least another month!

148
00:08:11,679 --> 00:08:13,389
There are papers to be signed...

149
00:08:13,681 --> 00:08:16,392
Lady | gota train to catch.

150
00:08:16,684 --> 00:08:18,477
(Children yell and cry)

151
00:08:19,353 --> 00:08:21,272
(Door slams)

152
00:08:23,858 --> 00:08:27,945
(Music)

153
00:08:31,949 --> 00:08:33,492
(Blows whistle)

154
00:08:35,578 --> 00:08:43,578
(Music)

155
00:08:48,716 --> 00:08:52,428
Katie, I'm glad we have each other.

156
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:56,307
It's so difficult finding a kindred spirit these days.

157
00:08:56,599 --> 00:08:59,643
Anne Shirley get undressed at once!

158
00:08:59,935 --> 00:09:02,438
Have you no respect for rules and regulations?

159
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:06,442
I'm sorry Mrs. Cadbury, but | wasn't paying attention.

160
00:09:06,734 --> 00:09:09,445
You haven't been paying attention for the past six months.

161
00:09:09,737 --> 00:09:11,447
Oh, | know I'll improve.

162
00:09:11,739 --> 00:09:14,450
It's just that my life is a perfect graveyard

163
00:09:14,742 --> 00:09:16,452
of buried hopes now.

164
00:09:16,744 --> 00:09:18,454
That's a sentence | read once

165
00:09:18,746 --> 00:09:19,723
and | say it over to comfort myself

166
00:09:19,747 --> 00:09:22,458
in these times that try the soul.

167
00:09:22,750 --> 00:09:24,460
We've had a request for two of our girls

168
00:09:24,752 --> 00:09:28,464
to live with families in Prince Edward Island.

169
00:09:28,756 --> 00:09:31,467
And I've decided that you will be one of them.

170
00:09:31,759 --> 00:09:34,470
Oh, thank you, Mrs. Cadbury.

171
00:09:34,762 --> 00:09:37,473
Thank you with all my heart!

172
00:09:37,765 --> 00:09:39,475
It's not my wish to reward rebelliousness;

173
00:09:39,767 --> 00:09:43,562
but for the good of discipline it seems that | must.

174
00:09:45,648 --> 00:09:47,483
Perhaps this new family of yours

175
00:09:47,775 --> 00:09:50,486
can shatter this dream world that you live in.

176
00:09:50,778 --> 00:09:54,323
Now get into your nightgown and go to bed.

177
00:09:54,782 --> 00:09:56,575
(Train chugs)

178
00:10:06,544 --> 00:10:08,587
(Low hum of chatter)

179
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:19,223
Are you waitin' for Someone, miss?

180
00:10:19,515 --> 00:10:20,516
1am thank you.

181
00:10:20,808 --> 00:10:22,786
Would you prefer to sit in the ladies' waiting room?

182
00:10:22,810 --> 00:10:24,520
No, | prefer to sit here.

183
00:10:24,812 --> 00:10:27,106
There's so much more scope for the imagination.

184
00:10:27,398 --> 00:10:28,524
Thank you just the same.

185
00:10:28,816 --> 00:10:32,611
(Chuckles) As you like, miss.

186
00:10:43,706 --> 00:10:45,624
(Cart rattles, hooves clop)

187
00:10:48,002 --> 00:10:51,630
Thomas! Isn't that Matthew Cuthbert driving that buggy?

188
00:10:54,091 --> 00:10:55,091
Appears to be.

189
00:10:55,843 --> 00:10:57,563
But he never goes to town this time of year

190
00:10:57,845 --> 00:11:00,556
and he never wears his suit except in church!

191
00:11:00,848 --> 00:11:02,766
Maybe he's goin' courtin'.

192
00:11:03,058 --> 00:11:06,562
Don't be so utterly ridiculous, Thomas!

193
00:11:06,854 --> 00:11:08,564
He's not going fast enough for a doctor.

194
00:11:08,856 --> 00:11:11,567
Oh, my afternoon is spoiled!

195
00:11:11,859 --> 00:11:14,570
| won't have a moment's peace until | know what that man iS up to.

196
00:11:14,862 --> 00:11:16,906
Wearing a suit...

197
00:11:19,742 --> 00:11:22,661
Marilla is simply going to have to explain all this...

198
00:11:25,748 --> 00:11:28,667
(Door opens) Yoo hoo, Marilla!

199
00:11:34,757 --> 00:11:37,593
Ah, Rachel, good morning.

200
00:11:37,885 --> 00:11:39,678
And how are all the Lyndes?

201
00:11:40,930 --> 00:11:43,766
Oh we're all right as rain, Marilla.

202
00:11:44,058 --> 00:11:46,602
Uh, but | was kind of worried about you

203
00:11:46,894 --> 00:11:48,604
when | saw your brother drive by just now.

204
00:11:48,896 --> 00:11:51,607
Oh, I'm fine. Just fine.

205
00:11:51,899 --> 00:11:53,609
Appreciate the concern though.

206
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:55,611
But he was in his suit.

207
00:11:55,903 --> 00:11:57,613
And smoking his pipe.

208
00:11:57,905 --> 00:12:00,699
Well, | don't mind so long as he smokes his pipe

209
00:12:01,408 --> 00:12:03,786
in the great outdoors and not in my kitchen.

210
00:12:05,788 --> 00:12:07,623
He was in his suit.

211
00:12:07,915 --> 00:12:09,708
(Sighs) Yes Rachel.

212
00:12:10,793 --> 00:12:13,629
Well, Matthew never goes to town this time of year.

213
00:12:13,921 --> 00:12:16,715
Matthew wasn't going to town.

214
00:12:18,092 --> 00:12:20,886
Oh, don't keep me in such suspense!

215
00:12:23,806 --> 00:12:25,641
He was going to Bright River.

216
00:12:25,933 --> 00:12:27,911
We're getting a little boy from an orphanage in Nova Scotia

217
00:12:27,935 --> 00:12:30,646
and he's coming in on the afternoon train.

218
00:12:30,938 --> 00:12:34,692
A boy! You can't be serious!

219
00:12:34,984 --> 00:12:36,920
Well, you don't know anything about raising children;

220
00:12:36,944 --> 00:12:38,737
what ever put such an idea into your head?

221
00:12:39,029 --> 00:12:42,658
Well... Matthew's getting along in years.

222
00:12:42,950 --> 00:12:44,660
He's not as spry as he once was

223
00:12:44,952 --> 00:12:48,664
and his heart bothers him greatly.

224
00:12:48,956 --> 00:12:50,666
Mrs. Spencer was up here before Christmas

225
00:12:50,958 --> 00:12:52,668
and said she was getting a little gir!

226
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:56,672
From the Hopeton Asylum in the spring.

227
00:12:56,964 --> 00:12:59,675
Matthew and | gave it good consideration

228
00:12:59,967 --> 00:13:03,679
sO we sent word to her by her niece Roberta

229
00:13:03,971 --> 00:13:08,767
to tell her to bring us a boy home while she was at it.

230
00:13:09,852 --> 00:13:12,896
| shall be surprised at nothing after this. Nothing.

231
00:13:13,188 --> 00:13:17,693
We told her to fix us up with a little boy eleven or twelve.

232
00:13:17,985 --> 00:13:19,695
Old enough to do the chores

233
00:13:19,987 --> 00:13:23,699
and young enough to be brought up properly.

234
00:13:23,991 --> 00:13:26,702
You know | pride myself on speaking my mind.

235
00:13:26,994 --> 00:13:31,707
And let me tell you | think you are doing a mighty risky thing.

236
00:13:31,999 --> 00:13:33,709
| wish you'd consulted me first.

237
00:13:34,001 --> 00:13:35,761
Why it was just last week | read in the paper

238
00:13:36,003 --> 00:13:37,843
where a couple took a boy from an orphan asylum

239
00:13:38,005 --> 00:13:40,716
and he set fire to their house at night. On purpose!

240
00:13:41,008 --> 00:13:44,053
Burnt them to a crisp in their beds!

241
00:13:44,345 --> 00:13:47,723
Well, | won't say that | haven't had my qualms, Rachel.

242
00:13:48,015 --> 00:13:49,725
But Matthew was so terrible determined

243
00:13:50,017 --> 00:13:52,728
and it's so seldom that he sets his mind on anything

244
00:13:53,020 --> 00:13:55,773
that | felt | had to give in.

245
00:13:56,065 --> 00:13:57,584
And there was another case six months ago

246
00:13:57,608 --> 00:13:58,734
over in New Brunswick.

247
00:13:59,026 --> 00:14:01,737
Where an asylum child put strychnine in the well

248
00:14:02,029 --> 00:14:04,740
and the entire family died In agony.

249
00:14:05,032 --> 00:14:06,742
Only in was a girl in that instance.

250
00:14:07,034 --> 00:14:10,829
Well, we are not getting a girl.

251
00:14:13,916 --> 00:14:15,834
(Hooves clop)

252
00:14:19,922 --> 00:14:21,840
Whoa.

253
00:14:29,932 --> 00:14:32,768
- Oh, how do, Matthew.
- Hello Angus.

254
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:34,037
Is the afternoon train due soon?

255
00:14:34,061 --> 00:14:36,688
Oh, been and gone a half an hour ago.

256
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:38,774
There was a passenger dropped off for you.

257
00:14:39,066 --> 00:14:40,776
She's waitin' for you on the platform.

258
00:14:41,068 --> 00:14:42,778
She?

259
00:14:43,070 --> 00:14:45,781
Not to worry Matthew. | don't think she bites.

260
00:14:46,073 --> 00:14:47,741
It's a boy I've come for.

261
00:14:48,033 --> 00:14:49,219
Oh, she won't have any trouble explainin.

262
00:14:49,243 --> 00:14:51,870
She has a tongue of her own. (Laughs)

263
00:15:13,016 --> 00:15:15,811
| Suppose youre Mister Matthew Cuthbert.

264
00:15:16,103 --> 00:15:18,814
My name is Anne Shirley;

265
00:15:19,106 --> 00:15:20,816
Ann spelled with an "e".

266
00:15:21,108 --> 00:15:23,336
| was beginning to be afraid you weren't coming for me today

267
00:15:23,360 --> 00:15:26,822
so | made up my mind to climb up that big wild cherry tree

268
00:15:27,114 --> 00:15:28,091
and wait for you 'til morning.

269
00:15:28,115 --> 00:15:29,835
It would be lovely to sleep in a cherry tree

270
00:15:30,117 --> 00:15:32,828
all silvery in the moonshine, don't you think?

271
00:15:33,120 --> 00:15:35,831
Oh yes it would; | mean no

272
00:15:36,123 --> 00:15:38,834
| mean uh... There's been a big mistake.

273
00:15:39,126 --> 00:15:40,166
Oh no. There's no mistake.

274
00:15:40,252 --> 00:15:42,838
Not if you're Mr. Matthew Cuthbert.

275
00:15:43,130 --> 00:15:46,842
You are Mr. Matthew Cuthbert, aren't you?

276
00:15:47,134 --> 00:15:48,278
Mrs. Spencer told me to wait right here for you

277
00:15:48,302 --> 00:15:50,846
and so I've done. Most pleasantly | must say.

278
00:15:51,138 --> 00:15:55,934
Oh, this is beautiful country you have here, Mr. Cuthbert.

279
00:15:57,019 --> 00:15:58,854
I'm sorry I... | was late.

280
00:15:59,146 --> 00:16:00,123
Oh no, that's fine, thank you.

281
00:16:00,147 --> 00:16:01,857
It's very light and thin... like me.

282
00:16:02,149 --> 00:16:03,859
| better hold onto my bag.

283
00:16:04,151 --> 00:16:06,129
If it isn't carried in a certain way the handle falls off.

284
00:16:06,153 --> 00:16:07,863
| mastered the trick of it on my journey.

285
00:16:08,155 --> 00:16:10,908
It's a very old carpetbag.

286
00:16:12,034 --> 00:16:13,869
Not at all the sort of luggage

287
00:16:14,161 --> 00:16:16,041
| imagine the Lady of Shallot would travel with.

288
00:16:16,163 --> 00:16:17,140
But of course hers would be suited

289
00:16:17,164 --> 00:16:20,959
to a horse drawn pavilion and not a train.

290
00:16:25,047 --> 00:16:26,882
I'm very glad you've come.

291
00:16:27,174 --> 00:16:29,885
Even if it would have been nice to sleep in a wild cherry tree.

292
00:16:30,177 --> 00:16:33,889
We've got a long piece to drive yet, haven't we?

293
00:16:34,181 --> 00:16:37,893
Oh, I'm glad because | love driving.

294
00:16:38,185 --> 00:16:40,163
It seems so wonderful that I'm going to live with you

295
00:16:40,187 --> 00:16:41,897
and belong to you.

296
00:16:42,189 --> 00:16:44,900
I've never really belonged to anyone before.

297
00:16:45,192 --> 00:16:47,170
And the asylum was the worst place I've lived in yet.

298
00:16:47,194 --> 00:16:49,172
Mrs. Spencer says it was wicked of me to talk like that,

299
00:16:49,196 --> 00:16:50,906
but | don't mean to be wicked.

300
00:16:51,198 --> 00:16:55,994
It's just So easy to be wicked without knowing it, isn't it?

301
00:16:57,079 --> 00:16:58,914
Am | talking too much?

302
00:16:59,206 --> 00:17:00,183
Oh, people are always telling me | do.

303
00:17:00,207 --> 00:17:02,918
And | can stop if | make my mind up to do it.

304
00:17:03,210 --> 00:17:06,004
You can talk all you like, | don't mind.

305
00:17:07,506 --> 00:17:11,969
Oh, | know you and | are going to get along just fine, Mr. Cuthbert.

306
00:17:29,111 --> 00:17:31,947
| love this place already.

307
00:17:32,239 --> 00:17:33,949
| always heard that Prince Edward Island

308
00:17:34,241 --> 00:17:35,951
was the most beautiful place in Canada

309
00:17:36,243 --> 00:17:39,955
and | used to imagine | was living here.

310
00:17:40,247 --> 00:17:43,041
This is the first dream that has ever come true for me.

311
00:17:48,046 --> 00:17:51,883
It's always been one of my dreams to live by the sea.

312
00:17:52,259 --> 00:17:53,969
These red roads are so peculiar.

313
00:17:54,261 --> 00:17:55,238
When we got into the train at Charlottetown

314
00:17:55,262 --> 00:17:56,972
and the red roads began to flash past,

315
00:17:57,264 --> 00:17:58,974
| asked Mrs. Spencer what made them red

316
00:17:59,266 --> 00:18:00,976
and she said she didn't know,

317
00:18:01,268 --> 00:18:04,062
and for pity's sake not to ask her any more questions.

318
00:18:05,147 --> 00:18:07,983
Dreams don't often come true do they, Mr. Cuthbert?

319
00:18:08,275 --> 00:18:11,987
Just now | feel pretty nearly perfectly happy.

320
00:18:12,279 --> 00:18:15,991
| can't feel exactly perfectly happy because...

321
00:18:16,283 --> 00:18:17,993
what colour would you call this.

322
00:18:18,285 --> 00:18:20,996
- Red?
- Red.

323
00:18:21,288 --> 00:18:25,000
That's why | can't ever be perfectly happy.

324
00:18:25,292 --> 00:18:27,002
| Know I'm skinny and a little freckled

325
00:18:27,294 --> 00:18:29,129
and my eyes are green...

326
00:18:29,421 --> 00:18:32,007
But | can imagine | have a beautiful roseleaf complexion

327
00:18:32,299 --> 00:18:35,010
and lovely starry violet eyes.

328
00:18:35,302 --> 00:18:37,012
But | cannot imagine my red hair away.

329
00:18:37,304 --> 00:18:41,016
It'll be my life long sorrow.

330
00:18:41,308 --> 00:18:43,018
| read of a girl in a novel once

331
00:18:43,310 --> 00:18:45,020
who was divinely beautiful.

332
00:18:45,312 --> 00:18:46,998
Have you ever imagined what it must be like

333
00:18:47,022 --> 00:18:49,232
to be divinely beautiful?

334
00:18:49,524 --> 00:18:51,276
(Crow caws)

335
00:18:51,568 --> 00:18:53,028
Oh, | have often.

336
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:55,030
Which would you rather be

337
00:18:55,322 --> 00:18:58,033
divinely beautiful or dazzlingly clever

338
00:18:58,325 --> 00:19:00,118
or angelically good?

339
00:19:02,162 --> 00:19:04,164
Well... | don't know.

340
00:19:04,456 --> 00:19:06,041
Neither do |.

341
00:19:06,333 --> 00:19:08,710
| Know I'll never be angelically good.

342
00:19:09,002 --> 00:19:12,047
Mrs. Spencer says | talk so much that...

343
00:19:12,339 --> 00:19:15,050
Mr. Cuthbert! Mr. Cuthbert

344
00:19:15,342 --> 00:19:17,052
what is this place called?

345
00:19:17,344 --> 00:19:21,139
The Avenue. Pretty ain't it?

346
00:19:28,230 --> 00:19:30,065
Pretty doesn't seem the right word to use.

347
00:19:30,357 --> 00:19:32,067
Nor beautiful either.

348
00:19:32,359 --> 00:19:35,070
They don't go far enough.

349
00:19:35,362 --> 00:19:37,656
It is wonderful. Wonderful.

350
00:19:39,241 --> 00:19:41,161
They shouldn't call this lovely place the Avenue?

351
00:19:41,368 --> 00:19:43,161
There's no meaning ina name like that.

352
00:19:43,453 --> 00:19:48,125
They should call it White Way Delight.

353
00:19:49,251 --> 00:19:54,172
It's far more glorious than | could ever have imagined.

354
00:19:55,257 --> 00:19:58,093
That's Barry's Pond.

355
00:19:58,385 --> 00:20:02,347
Oh, no... This is the Lake of Shining Waters.

356
00:20:03,265 --> 00:20:05,100
That's its rightful name.

357
00:20:05,392 --> 00:20:08,645
Do things like this ever give you a thrill Mr. Cuthbert?

358
00:20:08,937 --> 00:20:13,358
Well, uh, digging up them ugly white grubs in the cucumber beds.

359
00:20:15,277 --> 00:20:19,114
Yes, | could see how that could be very thrilling.

360
00:20:19,406 --> 00:20:23,994
- Whoa. Green Gables...
- Yonder.

361
00:20:26,288 --> 00:20:28,123
I've pinched myself SO many times today

362
00:20:28,415 --> 00:20:31,126
to make sure that this was real.

363
00:20:31,418 --> 00:20:33,128
But it is real!

364
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:35,213
And we're nearly home.

365
00:20:38,300 --> 00:20:40,218
Giddup.

366
00:20:41,303 --> 00:20:49,303
(Music)

367
00:20:56,443 --> 00:21:04,443
(Music)

368
00:21:11,458 --> 00:21:19,458
(Music)

369
00:21:26,473 --> 00:21:34,473
(Music)

370
00:21:36,942 --> 00:21:38,985
(Sighs) I'm overwhelmed.

371
00:21:45,367 --> 00:21:48,286
Matthew Cuthbert, who is that?

372
00:21:57,379 --> 00:21:59,214
It's a girl.

373
00:21:59,506 --> 00:22:01,216
- | can see that.
- Where's the boy?

374
00:22:01,508 --> 00:22:04,219
There weren't any. Just her.

375
00:22:04,511 --> 00:22:06,221
| figured we just couldn't leave her there

376
00:22:06,513 --> 00:22:09,224
no matter what the mistake was.

377
00:22:09,516 --> 00:22:10,493
You figured!

378
00:22:10,517 --> 00:22:13,228
Well, this is a fine kettle of fish.

379
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:16,147
This is what comes of sending word

380
00:22:16,439 --> 00:22:17,839
instead of going ourselves Matthew.

381
00:22:17,899 --> 00:22:19,234
You don't want me?

382
00:22:19,526 --> 00:22:23,238
You don't want me because I'm not a boy.

383
00:22:23,530 --> 00:22:25,240
Nobody ever did want me.

384
00:22:25,532 --> 00:22:29,244
| might have known this was all too beautiful to be true.

385
00:22:29,536 --> 00:22:32,247
- Come, come now don"t cry.
- It is not your fault.

386
00:22:32,539 --> 00:22:34,332
This is just the most tragical thing

387
00:22:34,624 --> 00:22:38,253
that has ever happened to me.

388
00:22:38,545 --> 00:22:40,255
Well, what's your name?

389
00:22:40,547 --> 00:22:43,341
Would you please call me Cordelia?

390
00:22:45,427 --> 00:22:47,262
Call you Cordelia?

391
00:22:47,554 --> 00:22:48,531
Don't you think it's a pretty name?

392
00:22:48,555 --> 00:22:50,140
Is that your name?

393
00:22:50,432 --> 00:22:51,792
Well, no it's not exactly my name.

394
00:22:51,933 --> 00:22:54,477
But, oh, | would love to be called Cordelia.

395
00:22:54,769 --> 00:22:56,271
| don't understand what you mean.

396
00:22:56,563 --> 00:22:59,274
Cordelia is such a perfectly elegant name.

397
00:22:59,566 --> 00:23:02,319
What is your name child, and no more nonsense.

398
00:23:02,611 --> 00:23:04,279
Anne Shirley.

399
00:23:04,571 --> 00:23:07,282
Plain old unromantic Anne Shirley.

400
00:23:07,574 --> 00:23:09,284
Ann Shirley is a fine sensible name

401
00:23:09,576 --> 00:23:11,286
and hardly one to be shamed of.

402
00:23:11,578 --> 00:23:12,555
Oh, I'm not ashamed;

403
00:23:12,579 --> 00:23:14,289
but if you're going to call me Anne

404
00:23:14,581 --> 00:23:16,501
would you please be sure to spell it with an "e"?

405
00:23:16,708 --> 00:23:19,294
What difference does it make how it is spelled?

406
00:23:19,586 --> 00:23:21,296
It makes a lot of difference.

407
00:23:21,588 --> 00:23:24,299
Print out A-N-N and it looks absolutely dreadful.

408
00:23:24,591 --> 00:23:27,302
But Anne with an "e" is quite distinguished.

409
00:23:27,594 --> 00:23:29,304
So if you'll only call me Anne with an "e"

410
00:23:29,596 --> 00:23:32,307
I'll try and reconcile myself to not being called Cordelia.

411
00:23:32,599 --> 00:23:36,311
Very well then, Anne with an "e".

412
00:23:36,603 --> 00:23:38,581
How is it that you happen to be brought and not a boy?

413
00:23:38,605 --> 00:23:41,399
If | were very beautiful and had nut-brown hair,

414
00:23:41,691 --> 00:23:43,318
would you keep me?

415
00:23:43,610 --> 00:23:46,321
No, we have absolutely no use for a girl.

416
00:23:46,613 --> 00:23:48,323
Well, don't stand there gaping.

417
00:23:48,615 --> 00:23:50,325
Come along. Bring your bag.

418
00:23:50,617 --> 00:23:52,337
Now that you're here | suppose we'll have to

419
00:23:52,619 --> 00:23:54,788
put you somewhere tonight.

420
00:23:55,080 --> 00:23:57,332
Take off your hat.

421
00:23:57,624 --> 00:23:58,625
You must be hungry.

422
00:23:58,917 --> 00:24:00,335
| can't eat.

423
00:24:00,627 --> 00:24:03,338
| can never eat when I'm in the depths of despair.

424
00:24:03,630 --> 00:24:05,340
The depths of despair?

425
00:24:05,632 --> 00:24:06,609
Can you eat when you're that way?

426
00:24:06,633 --> 00:24:08,343
I've never been that way.

427
00:24:08,635 --> 00:24:11,346
Can't you even imagine you're in the depths of despair?

428
00:24:11,638 --> 00:24:12,615
Nol cannot.

429
00:24:12,639 --> 00:24:16,434
To despair is to turn your back on God.

430
00:24:23,525 --> 00:24:24,627
This is your room for the night.

431
00:24:24,651 --> 00:24:28,363
Wash up and then come down for supper.

432
00:24:28,655 --> 00:24:30,448
Yes, Miss Cuthbert.

433
00:24:36,538 --> 00:24:38,456
(Birds chirp)

434
00:24:42,919 --> 00:24:44,629
(Water sloshes)

435
00:24:54,556 --> 00:24:56,266
I'm taking her straight over

436
00:24:56,558 --> 00:24:58,393
to that Spencer woman in the morning.

437
00:24:58,685 --> 00:25:01,396
This girl has to go straight back to the asylum.

438
00:25:01,688 --> 00:25:05,400
- | Suppose.
- You suppose?

439
00:25:05,692 --> 00:25:07,402
Don't you know it?

440
00:25:07,694 --> 00:25:10,405
She's a nice little thing, Marilla.

441
00:25:10,697 --> 00:25:12,407
Seems a pity to send her back.

442
00:25:12,699 --> 00:25:15,660
She's-she's so set on staying.

443
00:25:15,952 --> 00:25:20,415
Matthew Cuthbert, | believe this child has bewitched you.

444
00:25:20,707 --> 00:25:24,419
| can see plain as plain you want to keep her.

445
00:25:24,711 --> 00:25:28,423
We could hire a boy and she could be company for you.

446
00:25:28,715 --> 00:25:30,425
I'm not suffering for company.

447
00:25:30,717 --> 00:25:32,427
Particularly a girl who prattles on

448
00:25:32,719 --> 00:25:34,429
without stopping for breath.

449
00:25:34,721 --> 00:25:36,431
She's no good for us;

450
00:25:36,723 --> 00:25:39,434
she has to go straight back where she came from.

451
00:25:39,726 --> 00:25:42,520
Well, we might be of some good to her.

452
00:25:46,608 --> 00:25:48,526
(Door creaks open)

453
00:25:57,619 --> 00:25:59,537
(Footsteps thud lightly)

454
00:26:03,625 --> 00:26:06,461
Good night Anne with an "e".

455
00:26:06,753 --> 00:26:08,463
It's difficult to say good night

456
00:26:08,755 --> 00:26:12,467
when it's the worst night I've ever known.

457
00:26:12,759 --> 00:26:15,470
Good night just the same, child.

458
00:26:15,762 --> 00:26:17,555
Good night, Miss Cuthbert.

459
00:26:22,769 --> 00:26:26,272
(Music)

460
00:26:37,659 --> 00:26:40,495
Little Jerry Buote from the creek was around.

461
00:26:40,787 --> 00:26:43,581
| told him | guessed that I'd hire him on for the summer.

462
00:26:45,667 --> 00:26:47,502
Hurry up, child!

463
00:26:47,794 --> 00:26:49,504
Just fixing Green Gables in my memory.

464
00:26:49,796 --> 00:26:51,774
In years to come I'm going to look back on Green Gables

465
00:26:51,798 --> 00:26:54,759
as a beautiful dream that will always haunt me.

466
00:26:58,680 --> 00:27:00,390
Don't you think it's romantic.

467
00:27:00,682 --> 00:27:02,517
You can think about it as you drive along.

468
00:27:02,809 --> 00:27:06,604
| shall never forget your kindness Mr. Cuthbert.

469
00:27:13,695 --> 00:27:21,695
(Music)

470
00:27:33,423 --> 00:27:35,341
Marilla... oh... oh...

471
00:27:37,719 --> 00:27:39,554
Marilla dear.

472
00:27:39,846 --> 00:27:42,557
You're the last person | expected to see today.

473
00:27:42,849 --> 00:27:45,101
I'd have imagined you'd be getting Anne settled.

474
00:27:45,393 --> 00:27:46,561
How are you Anne?

475
00:27:46,853 --> 00:27:50,607
As well as a victim of tragic circumstances could be, Mrs. Spencer.

476
00:27:50,899 --> 00:27:54,527
There seems to be some queer mistake, Sarah.

477
00:27:54,819 --> 00:27:57,488
We told Roberta for you to get us a boy.

478
00:27:57,864 --> 00:28:00,658
Oh Marilla! You don't say!

479
00:28:02,744 --> 00:28:06,581
Well, Roberta distinctly said that you wanted a girl.

480
00:28:06,873 --> 00:28:08,583
| knew | should have gone myself.

481
00:28:08,875 --> 00:28:11,586
| am dreadfully sorry, Marilla.

482
00:28:11,878 --> 00:28:15,590
| suppose the asylum will take the child back?

483
00:28:15,882 --> 00:28:20,595
- Well, as a matter of fact, Mrs.
- Blewett was up here yesterday

484
00:28:20,887 --> 00:28:22,607
asking me if | could get her a little girl.

485
00:28:22,889 --> 00:28:24,599
She has such a large family you know

486
00:28:24,891 --> 00:28:27,310
ten children and another one on the way.

487
00:28:27,602 --> 00:28:29,145
She's simply beside herself for help.

488
00:28:29,437 --> 00:28:30,605
Excuse me, Mrs. Spencer.

489
00:28:30,897 --> 00:28:33,608
Would there happen to be any twins among them?

490
00:28:33,900 --> 00:28:36,611
Oh, she has two sets of twins.

491
00:28:36,903 --> 00:28:38,613
How did you know, child?

492
00:28:38,905 --> 00:28:40,531
Twins seem to be my lot in life.

493
00:28:40,823 --> 00:28:42,533
- Mrs. Spencer...
- Mrs. Spencer...

494
00:28:42,909 --> 00:28:44,629
Anne, will be just the girl... and oh, look!

495
00:28:44,911 --> 00:28:46,621
There's Mrs. Blewett this blessed minute.

496
00:28:46,913 --> 00:28:49,624
| call this positively providential.

497
00:28:49,916 --> 00:28:52,627
Yoo hoo, Mrs. Blewett!

498
00:28:52,919 --> 00:28:54,629
| have someone for you to meet.

499
00:28:54,921 --> 00:28:58,633
Mrs. Blewett... Anne Shirley.

500
00:28:58,925 --> 00:29:01,636
She'll be just the thing for you.

501
00:29:01,928 --> 00:29:05,640
- Miss Cuthbert.
- Mrs. Blewett.

502
00:29:05,932 --> 00:29:07,642
How old are you, girl?

503
00:29:07,934 --> 00:29:09,644
Thirteen.

504
00:29:09,936 --> 00:29:12,939
Hmph. Ain't much to you.

505
00:29:13,815 --> 00:29:15,650
But youre wiry and | don't know

506
00:29:15,942 --> 00:29:18,653
but the wiry ones can work the hardest.

507
00:29:18,945 --> 00:29:20,655
I'll expect you to earn your keep.

508
00:29:20,947 --> 00:29:22,615
No mistakin' that.

509
00:29:22,907 --> 00:29:25,660
And | want you to act smart and be respectful.

510
00:29:25,952 --> 00:29:27,662
All right I'll take her.

511
00:29:27,954 --> 00:29:29,714
My twins have been awful fractious these days

512
00:29:29,956 --> 00:29:32,667
and I'm terrible worn out.

513
00:29:32,959 --> 00:29:35,503
Well now, I don't know.

514
00:29:35,795 --> 00:29:36,939
| feel | oughtn't to make a decision

515
00:29:36,963 --> 00:29:40,508
until | speak to Matthew.

516
00:29:40,842 --> 00:29:44,679
I'll just take her home again and talk to him.

517
00:29:44,971 --> 00:29:46,764
Good afternoon, ladies.

518
00:29:52,854 --> 00:29:54,772
Well!

519
00:30:00,862 --> 00:30:02,697
Miss Cuthbert!

520
00:30:02,989 --> 00:30:05,700
Did you really say it or did | only just imagine it?

521
00:30:05,992 --> 00:30:07,702
| haven't said anything yet, young lady.

522
00:30:07,994 --> 00:30:09,704
Except | want to speak to Matthew.

523
00:30:09,996 --> 00:30:11,836
Sending you back to the orphanage is one thing.

524
00:30:12,123 --> 00:30:16,711
Handing you over to the likes of Mathilda Blewett is another.

525
00:30:17,003 --> 00:30:18,981
I'd rather go back to the asylum than live with her.

526
00:30:19,005 --> 00:30:21,716
Two sets of twins. Oh!

527
00:30:22,008 --> 00:30:24,135
Besides she looks exactly like a gimlet.

528
00:30:25,887 --> 00:30:27,722
You should be ashamed of yourself

529
00:30:28,014 --> 00:30:29,294
speaking of a stranger that way.

530
00:30:29,390 --> 00:30:33,728
Hold your tongue and don't criticize your elders.

531
00:30:34,020 --> 00:30:36,731
I'll try and do anything and be anything you want,

532
00:30:37,023 --> 00:30:40,818
if you'll only keep me, Miss Cuthbert.

533
00:30:48,326 --> 00:30:49,702
(Sheep bleat)

534
00:30:52,914 --> 00:30:56,751
Well? Aren't you going to say anything, Matthew?

535
00:30:57,043 --> 00:31:00,129
| wouldn't give a dog | liked to that Blewett woman.

536
00:31:01,923 --> 00:31:04,008
It makes no sense to keep her.

537
00:31:06,928 --> 00:31:09,764
But if we did keep her...

538
00:31:11,933 --> 00:31:15,770
I'd expect you not to interfere with my methods.

539
00:31:16,062 --> 00:31:18,773
An old maid like me may not know much about raising a child

540
00:31:19,065 --> 00:31:23,861
but | know a darn sight more than an old bachelor like you.

541
00:31:27,949 --> 00:31:30,451
(Chuckles)

542
00:31:30,910 --> 00:31:37,041
Oh, she could talk the hind leg off a mule, that's certain.

543
00:31:37,959 --> 00:31:41,879
Oh, wouldn't that be a change around here.

544
00:31:57,603 --> 00:31:59,439
Have you said your prayers?

545
00:32:00,106 --> 00:32:01,816
| never say any prayers.

546
00:32:02,108 --> 00:32:03,085
What do you mean?

547
00:32:03,109 --> 00:32:04,829
Haven't you been taught to say your prayers?

548
00:32:05,111 --> 00:32:07,822
Mrs. Hammond told me that God made my hair red on purpose

549
00:32:08,114 --> 00:32:09,824
and I've never cared for him since.

550
00:32:10,116 --> 00:32:13,828
Well, while you're under my roof you will say your prayers.

551
00:32:14,120 --> 00:32:16,831
- Why of course.
- If you want me to.

552
00:32:17,123 --> 00:32:18,833
How does one do it?

553
00:32:19,125 --> 00:32:22,962
Well, you kneel beside the bed.

554
00:32:23,254 --> 00:32:26,841
That's the part | never really could understand.

555
00:32:27,133 --> 00:32:29,844
Why must people kneel down to pray?

556
00:32:30,136 --> 00:32:32,847
If | really wanted to pray

557
00:32:33,139 --> 00:32:36,851
I'd go out into a great big field, all alone.

558
00:32:37,143 --> 00:32:38,853
I'd look up into the sky.

559
00:32:39,145 --> 00:32:42,857
I'd imagine it was the dome of great cathedral.

560
00:32:43,149 --> 00:32:47,945
Oh, and then I'd close my eyes and just feel a prayer...

561
00:32:49,614 --> 00:32:50,948
What am I to say?

562
00:32:53,034 --> 00:32:56,120
Well, | think you're old enough to think of your own prayer.

563
00:32:56,454 --> 00:32:59,665
You'll thank God for his blessings

564
00:33:00,041 --> 00:33:04,003
and then humbly ask him for the things you want.

565
00:33:04,295 --> 00:33:05,880
I'll do my best.

566
00:33:06,172 --> 00:33:08,132
(Clears throat)

567
00:33:11,052 --> 00:33:13,888
Dear Gracious heavenly father.

568
00:33:14,180 --> 00:33:16,891
| thank you for everything.

569
00:33:17,183 --> 00:33:18,893
As for the things | especially want,

570
00:33:19,185 --> 00:33:21,163
they're so numerous it would take a great deal of time

571
00:33:21,187 --> 00:33:22,164
to mention them all.

572
00:33:22,188 --> 00:33:25,900
So I'll just mention the two most important.

573
00:33:26,192 --> 00:33:29,904
Please let me stay at Green Gables;

574
00:33:30,196 --> 00:33:32,907
please make me beautiful when | grow up.

575
00:33:33,199 --> 00:33:37,995
| remain yours respectfully, Anne Shirley. With an "e".

576
00:33:38,829 --> 00:33:40,748
Did | do all right?

577
00:33:41,040 --> 00:33:44,961
Yes if you were addressing a business letter to the catalogue store.

578
00:33:45,670 --> 00:33:47,296
Get into bed.

579
00:33:59,100 --> 00:34:02,937
| should have said Amen instead of yours respectfully.

580
00:34:03,229 --> 00:34:04,939
Think it'll make any difference?

581
00:34:05,231 --> 00:34:08,025
- | expect God will overlook it.
- This time.

582
00:34:10,111 --> 00:34:12,029
- Good night.
- Good night, Miss Cuthbert.

583
00:34:18,411 --> 00:34:22,039
That girl is next door to a perfect heathen.

584
00:34:31,591 --> 00:34:34,969
Good Morning, Miss Cuthbert.

585
00:34:35,261 --> 00:34:36,971
Where's Matthew?

586
00:34:37,263 --> 00:34:38,973
He had his breakfast hours ago

587
00:34:39,265 --> 00:34:42,602
and been in the fields ever since. Why?

588
00:34:43,603 --> 00:34:45,483
| see I'll have to be up before the break of day

589
00:34:45,771 --> 00:34:49,025
if I'm to say good morning to Matthew.

590
00:34:49,317 --> 00:34:53,070
- That... is... if...
- If what?

591
00:34:54,155 --> 00:34:55,155
Please Miss Cuthbert.

592
00:34:55,281 --> 00:34:56,675
Tell me if you're going to send me back.

593
00:34:56,699 --> 00:34:57,992
| made up my mind to be patient,

594
00:34:58,284 --> 00:35:00,995
but | just can't bear it any longer.

595
00:35:01,287 --> 00:35:03,998
Well, you'll just have to bear it because | simply don't know.

596
00:35:04,290 --> 00:35:07,001
| thought maybe we'd put it on trial for a while.

597
00:35:07,293 --> 00:35:10,004
For all our sakes. Would that suit you?

598
00:35:10,296 --> 00:35:12,016
If you think it's necessary, Miss Cuthbert.

599
00:35:12,298 --> 00:35:14,008
| do.

600
00:35:14,300 --> 00:35:18,095
You may not be happy with two old grumps like us.

601
00:35:19,180 --> 00:35:21,015
| know | would be.

602
00:35:21,307 --> 00:35:26,020
I'd be happier than even | can imagine at this present moment.

603
00:35:26,312 --> 00:35:29,023
Here, while you're eating your breakfast

604
00:35:29,315 --> 00:35:30,292
| want you to learn that.

605
00:35:30,316 --> 00:35:32,026
You need a little religion in your life

606
00:35:32,318 --> 00:35:36,030
as bad as you need fattening up.

607
00:35:36,322 --> 00:35:40,117
"Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name".

608
00:35:41,577 --> 00:35:44,413
That is just like a line of music.

609
00:35:44,955 --> 00:35:48,000
I'm glad you thought of making me learn this, Miss Cuthbert.

610
00:35:48,292 --> 00:35:50,294
Well, learn it then and hold your tongue.

611
00:35:50,753 --> 00:35:52,672
Yes ma'am.

612
00:35:56,217 --> 00:36:00,137
- Oh, Good Lord!
- Here comes Rachel Lynde.

613
00:36:01,222 --> 00:36:04,058
Anne, take that card into the parlor

614
00:36:04,350 --> 00:36:07,061
and then you come back here on your best behavior.

615
00:36:07,353 --> 00:36:09,146
| don't want her knowing you're a heathen.

616
00:36:15,236 --> 00:36:19,073
- Good morning, Marilla.
- Come in, Rachel.

617
00:36:19,365 --> 00:36:22,076
1am shocked at this horrendous mistake I've heard about.

618
00:36:22,368 --> 00:36:25,246
I've gotten over the shock myself.

619
00:36:31,252 --> 00:36:33,087
Couldn't you send her back?

620
00:36:33,379 --> 00:36:36,090
Well, we're still considering on it.

621
00:36:36,382 --> 00:36:38,302
- Considering on it?
- What is there to consider?

622
00:36:38,384 --> 00:36:40,264
| mean, a boy would have been bad enough, but...

623
00:36:40,386 --> 00:36:43,264
This is a friend and neighbor of mine, Mrs. Rachel Lynde.

624
00:36:43,556 --> 00:36:45,099
Anne Shirley.

625
00:36:45,391 --> 00:36:48,102
How do you do, Mrs. Lynde?

626
00:36:48,394 --> 00:36:50,354
Well, her looks are certainly nothing to consider.

627
00:36:50,396 --> 00:36:54,108
| mean, she's terribly skinny and homely, Marilla.

628
00:36:54,400 --> 00:36:56,193
Come over here, child.

629
00:37:02,283 --> 00:37:06,120
Lawful heart, her hair is as red as carrots.

630
00:37:06,412 --> 00:37:11,333
How dare you say I'm skinny and... Carrots!

631
00:37:12,293 --> 00:37:16,130
You're a rude, impolite, unfeeling woman and | hate you!

632
00:37:16,422 --> 00:37:17,965
Anne Shirley!

633
00:37:18,257 --> 00:37:20,402
How would you like to have nasty things said about you!

634
00:37:20,426 --> 00:37:24,138
How would you like to be told that you're fat and ugly

635
00:37:24,430 --> 00:37:26,223
and a sour old gossip!

636
00:37:28,309 --> 00:37:30,144
Anne Shirley!

637
00:37:30,436 --> 00:37:34,815
Anne Shirley, you come back at once and apologize!

638
00:37:35,191 --> 00:37:36,192
Mark my words, Marilla.

639
00:37:36,484 --> 00:37:39,153
That's the kind puts strychnine in the well.

640
00:37:39,445 --> 00:37:42,156
You shouldn't have twitted her about her looks.

641
00:37:42,448 --> 00:37:43,908
Marilla Cuthbert!

642
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:46,160
I'm not making excuses for her.

643
00:37:46,452 --> 00:37:48,172
Perhaps she was never taught what was right.

644
00:37:48,454 --> 00:37:50,164
But you were too hard on her, Rachel!

645
00:37:50,456 --> 00:37:53,167
| see I'll have to be very careful what | say from now on!

646
00:37:53,459 --> 00:37:55,169
Oh, I'm not vexed, Marilla.

647
00:37:55,461 --> 00:37:59,173
I'm too sorry for you to leave any room for anger in my mind.

648
00:37:59,465 --> 00:38:02,176
It's obvious to me that the good sense | admired in you

649
00:38:02,468 --> 00:38:05,262
left you when that child walked in your door.

650
00:38:07,348 --> 00:38:08,348
Good bye, Marilla.

651
00:38:08,474 --> 00:38:10,184
Come down and see me when you can,

652
00:38:10,476 --> 00:38:12,186
but don't expect me to visit here again

653
00:38:12,478 --> 00:38:14,271
if |am to be treated in such a fashion.

654
00:38:18,359 --> 00:38:20,236
- (Slams door)
- Good bye Rachel.

655
00:38:20,528 --> 00:38:22,279
(Crying)

656
00:38:36,377 --> 00:38:40,214
When | said trial | had no idea you'd take me literally.

657
00:38:40,506 --> 00:38:44,218
Of all the people you would pick on Rachel Lynde.

658
00:38:44,510 --> 00:38:48,222
She hadn't any right to say what she did!

659
00:38:48,514 --> 00:38:51,225
Rachel is too outspoken.

660
00:38:51,517 --> 00:38:55,229
But she is your elder, a stranger, and my guest;

661
00:38:55,521 --> 00:38:57,773
not to mention my friend.

662
00:38:58,065 --> 00:39:02,236
All of them very good reasons for you to have bit your tongue.

663
00:39:02,528 --> 00:39:04,248
She deserves an apology, you will go to her

664
00:39:04,530 --> 00:39:06,240
and you will give it.

665
00:39:06,532 --> 00:39:09,243
| can never do that!

666
00:39:09,535 --> 00:39:11,245
You can punish me any way you like.

667
00:39:11,537 --> 00:39:14,248
You can lock me up in a dark dungeon

668
00:39:14,540 --> 00:39:17,251
inhabited by snakes and toads

669
00:39:17,543 --> 00:39:20,254
and feed me on bread and water | won't complain.

670
00:39:20,546 --> 00:39:24,258
But | cannot ask Mrs. Lynde to forgive me.

671
00:39:24,550 --> 00:39:26,260
If you expect to remain under my roof

672
00:39:26,552 --> 00:39:29,346
you will apologize to Mrs. Lynde.

673
00:39:30,431 --> 00:39:33,225
Then you'll have to send me back!

674
00:39:40,441 --> 00:39:43,277
Rachel Lynde deserves what she gets...

675
00:39:43,569 --> 00:39:46,280
Matthew Cuthbert, don't form opinions for me.

676
00:39:46,572 --> 00:39:50,367
Next you'll be saying she oughtn't to be punished at all.

677
00:39:51,702 --> 00:39:52,703
(Sighs)

678
00:39:57,458 --> 00:40:05,458
(Music)

679
00:40:12,598 --> 00:40:20,598
(Music)

680
00:40:21,607 --> 00:40:23,609
(Door creaks open)

681
00:40:31,492 --> 00:40:33,661
(Anne sniffling)

682
00:40:40,501 --> 00:40:42,419
(Door creaks open)

683
00:40:56,684 --> 00:41:01,438
| haven't been upstairs in this house in four years.

684
00:41:02,731 --> 00:41:05,359
| guess you're leavin' then, huh?

685
00:41:05,651 --> 00:41:09,363
Oh, Matthew I'd rather die than apologize to Mrs. Lynde.

686
00:41:09,655 --> 00:41:11,448
It would be so humiliating.

687
00:41:13,534 --> 00:41:16,453
Well, Marilla's a terrible determined woman.

688
00:41:17,663 --> 00:41:20,374
You don't have to be exactly sorry, you know.

689
00:41:20,666 --> 00:41:23,377
You can just be sorta sorry.

690
00:41:23,669 --> 00:41:25,462
I'm not sorry at all.

691
00:41:29,550 --> 00:41:32,803
| hear Mrs. Blewett's an awful workhorse.

692
00:41:34,555 --> 00:41:38,851
And it'll be terrible lonesome around here without you.

693
00:41:40,227 --> 00:41:44,398
Couldn't you just kind of smooth it over?

694
00:41:44,690 --> 00:41:48,402
You really don't want me to go, do you?

695
00:41:48,694 --> 00:41:50,404
I'd do anything for you, Matthew;

696
00:41:50,696 --> 00:41:52,823
if you really wanted me to.

697
00:41:53,115 --> 00:41:56,827
Of course | do.

698
00:41:57,119 --> 00:42:00,414
We can't let Mrs. Lynde be the cause of our parting.

699
00:42:00,706 --> 00:42:04,209
| don't have to be really sorry.

700
00:42:04,501 --> 00:42:06,420
| just have to remove the disgrace

701
00:42:06,712 --> 00:42:10,424
I've brought upon Marilla's good name.

702
00:42:10,716 --> 00:42:12,426
Don't tell Marilla that | said anythin.

703
00:42:12,718 --> 00:42:15,679
She'll say I'm interferin.

704
00:42:17,598 --> 00:42:20,684
Wild horses couldn't drag it from me.

705
00:42:42,623 --> 00:42:44,541
(Door shuts)

706
00:43:01,642 --> 00:43:03,560
Miss Cuthbert?

707
00:43:09,650 --> 00:43:10,692
What is it?

708
00:43:10,984 --> 00:43:13,487
I'm sorry | lost my temper and said those rude things;

709
00:43:13,779 --> 00:43:16,490
and I'm willing to go and tell Mrs. Lynde so.

710
00:43:16,782 --> 00:43:18,492
| think that's a wise decision.

711
00:43:18,784 --> 00:43:20,577
I'll take you over first thing.

712
00:43:20,869 --> 00:43:24,498
Now get up to bed and don't forget to say your prayers.

713
00:43:24,790 --> 00:43:25,790
Yes ma'am.

714
00:43:30,671 --> 00:43:34,591
| knew that if we left her alone she'd come to her senses.

715
00:43:45,686 --> 00:43:47,604
(Muttering)

716
00:43:49,606 --> 00:43:52,526
(Door creaks open) Hurry up, Anne.

717
00:43:52,818 --> 00:43:54,528
What are you muttering about?

718
00:43:54,820 --> 00:43:58,782
| was just imagining out what | must say to Mrs. Lynde.

719
00:43:59,074 --> 00:44:02,619
Oh, Miss Cuthbert, you look so elegant.

720
00:44:03,787 --> 00:44:06,707
You don't make an important visit in kitchen clothes.

721
00:44:10,711 --> 00:44:12,546
| think amethysts are lovely.

722
00:44:12,838 --> 00:44:14,758
That's what | used to imagine diamonds were like.

723
00:44:14,840 --> 00:44:15,817
And then | saw a real diamond and...

724
00:44:15,841 --> 00:44:17,885
Oh, for goodness sakes child, bite your tongue

725
00:44:18,260 --> 00:44:20,637
and come along!

726
00:44:30,731 --> 00:44:32,566
Good Morning, Rachel.

727
00:44:32,858 --> 00:44:35,819
Anne has something to say to you.

728
00:44:36,111 --> 00:44:40,574
Mrs. Lynde, |am extremely sorry | behaved so terribly.

729
00:44:40,866 --> 00:44:42,576
I've disgraced my good friends

730
00:44:42,868 --> 00:44:44,668
who have let me stay at Green Gables on trial,

731
00:44:44,870 --> 00:44:45,847
even though I'm not a boy.

732
00:44:45,871 --> 00:44:48,582
Lam wicked and ungrateful

733
00:44:48,874 --> 00:44:50,584
and | deserve to be cast out forever.

734
00:44:50,876 --> 00:44:52,586
What you said was true.

735
00:44:52,878 --> 00:44:56,590
Lam skinny and ugly and my hair is red.

736
00:44:56,882 --> 00:44:58,592
What | said about you was true too,

737
00:44:58,884 --> 00:45:00,594
only | shouldn't have said it.

738
00:45:00,886 --> 00:45:03,597
Please Mrs. Lynde, forgive me.

739
00:45:03,889 --> 00:45:09,603
You wouldn't be so cruel as to inflict a life long sorrow On a poor orphan.

740
00:45:09,895 --> 00:45:12,064
Please, please forgive me!

741
00:45:13,774 --> 00:45:16,610
- There, there child...
- of course | forgive you.

742
00:45:16,902 --> 00:45:19,613
| guess | was a bit hard.

743
00:45:19,905 --> 00:45:20,882
But you mustn't mind me.

744
00:45:20,906 --> 00:45:24,618
I'm known throughout these parts as a woman who speaks her mind.

745
00:45:24,910 --> 00:45:26,620
And don't worry about your hair.

746
00:45:26,912 --> 00:45:29,623
| knew a girl once who had hair every bit as red as yours,

747
00:45:29,915 --> 00:45:34,086
but when she grew up it darkened into a real handsome auburn.

748
00:45:35,796 --> 00:45:38,632
You have given me hope, Mrs. Lynde.

749
00:45:38,924 --> 00:45:42,636
| shall always think of you as a benefactress.

750
00:45:42,928 --> 00:45:46,640
Marilla, what this child needs is discipline

751
00:45:46,932 --> 00:45:48,642
and a proper education.

752
00:45:48,934 --> 00:45:51,645
The Sunday school picnic is scheduled this week for Barry's field,

753
00:45:51,937 --> 00:45:53,647
| want you to take Anne

754
00:45:53,939 --> 00:45:56,650
so she can meet some civilized children her own age.

755
00:45:56,942 --> 00:45:58,044
Her tongue appears to be hinged in the middle

756
00:45:58,068 --> 00:45:59,653
but she may turn out all right.

757
00:45:59,945 --> 00:46:02,656
I'm sure you're right, Rachel.

758
00:46:02,948 --> 00:46:04,658
And trial or no trial,

759
00:46:04,950 --> 00:46:08,704
you ought to put the gir! Into school.

760
00:46:09,830 --> 00:46:12,666
Putting you in school doesn't mean a decision.

761
00:46:12,958 --> 00:46:15,669
It's just as easy to take you out as put you in.

762
00:46:15,961 --> 00:46:17,671
| understand, Miss Cuthbert,

763
00:46:17,963 --> 00:46:20,674
but it does give a person reason to hope.

764
00:46:20,966 --> 00:46:22,944
I've seen some shocking behavior from you, Anne Shirley.

765
00:46:22,968 --> 00:46:26,680
And it does give a person reason to doubt. Understand?

766
00:46:26,972 --> 00:46:29,683
My temper will never get the better of me again.

767
00:46:29,975 --> 00:46:31,095
Even though | am red haired.

768
00:46:31,351 --> 00:46:32,686
| hope not.

769
00:46:32,978 --> 00:46:34,938
Good behavior in the first place is more important

770
00:46:34,980 --> 00:46:38,692
than theatrical apologies afterwards.

771
00:46:38,984 --> 00:46:40,694
Since | had to do it,

772
00:46:40,986 --> 00:46:42,706
| thought | might as well do it thoroughly.

773
00:46:42,988 --> 00:46:46,700
Ah... save your thoroughness for prayer.

774
00:46:46,992 --> 00:46:49,786
And the praying that counts is the praying that's sincere.

775
00:46:50,078 --> 00:46:52,873
God does not want you for a fair weather friend.

776
00:46:54,875 --> 00:46:57,711
The only real friend | ever had was Katie Maurice

777
00:46:58,003 --> 00:46:59,713
and she was only my window friend.

778
00:47:00,005 --> 00:47:02,716
Window friend?

779
00:47:03,008 --> 00:47:04,986
| discovered her in the window of Mrs. Thomas' bookcase,

780
00:47:05,010 --> 00:47:06,850
It was the only window that hadn't been smashed

781
00:47:07,012 --> 00:47:08,722
by her intoxicated husband.

782
00:47:09,014 --> 00:47:11,725
| lived with them before the Hammonds.

783
00:47:12,017 --> 00:47:14,436
| used to wish | knew the spell to step through the glass

784
00:47:14,728 --> 00:47:17,814
into Katie's world, which was so beautiful.

785
00:47:19,900 --> 00:47:23,779
| don't think you should have window friends anymore.

786
00:47:24,071 --> 00:47:27,741
My greatest wish apart from staying at Green Gables

787
00:47:28,033 --> 00:47:30,744
would be to have a bosom friend.

788
00:47:31,036 --> 00:47:32,013
A what kind of friend?

789
00:47:32,037 --> 00:47:35,749
- A bosom friend.
- A really kindred spirit.

790
00:47:36,041 --> 00:47:39,753
I've dreamt of meeting her all my life.

791
00:47:40,045 --> 00:47:42,756
Diana Barry lives over there on Orchard slope.

792
00:47:43,048 --> 00:47:45,759
She's about your age.

793
00:47:46,051 --> 00:47:48,762
Her parents are sponsoring the picnic next Sunday

794
00:47:49,054 --> 00:47:50,764
and you can meet her.

795
00:47:51,056 --> 00:47:55,227
Diana of the Lake of Shining Waters.

796
00:47:55,936 --> 00:47:58,772
For mercy's sake child.

797
00:47:59,064 --> 00:48:01,775
You set your heart too much on silly names.

798
00:48:02,067 --> 00:48:03,044
What should | call you?

799
00:48:03,068 --> 00:48:05,779
May | call you Aunt Marilla?

800
00:48:06,071 --> 00:48:08,782
No. You can call me just plain Marilla.

801
00:48:09,074 --> 00:48:11,785
| don't believe in calling people names that are not their own.

802
00:48:12,077 --> 00:48:14,788
But you could imagine you were my aunt.

803
00:48:15,080 --> 00:48:17,791
No, | could not.

804
00:48:18,083 --> 00:48:20,061
Don't you ever imagine things differently from what they are?

805
00:48:20,085 --> 00:48:22,796
No.

806
00:48:23,088 --> 00:48:26,883
Oh, Marilla, how much you miss.

807
00:48:39,354 --> 00:48:40,814
How do you like them?

808
00:48:41,106 --> 00:48:42,941
| can imagine | like them.

809
00:48:44,985 --> 00:48:46,820
What's the matter with them?

810
00:48:47,112 --> 00:48:50,824
- They're not... not very...
- pretty.

811
00:48:51,116 --> 00:48:52,826
Lam not going to pamper your vanity.

812
00:48:53,118 --> 00:48:55,829
These are good and sensible dresses.

813
00:48:56,121 --> 00:48:58,832
This one is for Sunday and the others you can wear to school.

814
00:48:59,124 --> 00:49:00,834
Lam grateful...

815
00:49:01,126 --> 00:49:04,006
But I'd be even more grateful if you'd made this one with puffed sleeves.

816
00:49:04,129 --> 00:49:08,842
| cannot waste material on ridiculous looking frills and furbelows.

817
00:49:09,134 --> 00:49:11,344
Plain and sensible is best.

818
00:49:13,138 --> 00:49:15,458
I've always dreamed of going to a picnic in puffed sleeves.

819
00:49:16,141 --> 00:49:17,901
I'd rather look ridiculous with everyone else

820
00:49:18,143 --> 00:49:19,853
than plain and sensible all by myself.

821
00:49:20,145 --> 00:49:21,938
Trust you for that.

822
00:49:27,402 --> 00:49:29,863
Have you seen my amethyst brooch?

823
00:49:30,155 --> 00:49:31,865
- Yes...
- Did you touch it?

824
00:49:32,157 --> 00:49:33,134
| pinned it on yesterday,

825
00:49:33,158 --> 00:49:34,868
but just to see what it looked like.

826
00:49:35,160 --> 00:49:37,871
You had no business to meddle with my brooch.

827
00:49:38,163 --> 00:49:39,873
Where did you put it?

828
00:49:40,165 --> 00:49:41,875
Back on the pincushion.

829
00:49:42,167 --> 00:49:43,877
Honestly, Marilla, | didn't mean to meddle

830
00:49:44,169 --> 00:49:46,880
and | promise I'll never do it again.

831
00:49:47,172 --> 00:49:48,149
That's the one good thing about me.

832
00:49:48,173 --> 00:49:49,883
| never do the same wrong thing twice.

833
00:49:50,175 --> 00:49:53,970
The brooch is gone and you were the last one to handle it.

834
00:49:57,057 --> 00:49:59,976
Did you take it out and lose it?

835
00:50:01,061 --> 00:50:02,896
| didn't.

836
00:50:03,188 --> 00:50:07,901
Anne Shirley, you are telling me a falsehood.

837
00:50:09,069 --> 00:50:10,987
Go to your room!

838
00:50:14,074 --> 00:50:16,910
And you will stay in your room until you confess,

839
00:50:17,202 --> 00:50:18,912
even if it takes a month of Sundays.

840
00:50:19,204 --> 00:50:20,914
If you let me out for the picnic,

841
00:50:21,206 --> 00:50:22,916
I'll stay in my room as long as you like.

842
00:50:23,208 --> 00:50:25,919
| just have to go to the picnic.

843
00:50:26,211 --> 00:50:28,051
You're not going to the picnic or anywhere else

844
00:50:28,213 --> 00:50:29,923
until you tell me the truth.

845
00:50:30,215 --> 00:50:31,925
If |don't go to the picnic,

846
00:50:32,217 --> 00:50:33,927
how willl ever make a bosom friend?

847
00:50:34,219 --> 00:50:35,196
Or any friend at all?

848
00:50:35,220 --> 00:50:37,931
That brooch meant a great deal to me,

849
00:50:38,223 --> 00:50:39,933
more than any picnic.

850
00:50:40,225 --> 00:50:42,018
Now you go to your room.

851
00:50:52,153 --> 00:50:54,948
I've looked in every crack and cranny.

852
00:50:55,240 --> 00:50:56,950
We might as well face it, Matthew.

853
00:50:57,242 --> 00:50:59,953
She's taken that brooch and lied about it.

854
00:51:00,245 --> 00:51:03,957
| feel worse about that than the brooch.

855
00:51:04,249 --> 00:51:06,049
Are you sure it didn't fall behind the bureau?

856
00:51:06,251 --> 00:51:07,961
| moved the bureau.

857
00:51:08,253 --> 00:51:10,964
| even checked the cracks in the floor.

858
00:51:11,256 --> 00:51:12,966
| know how you feel, Matthew,

859
00:51:13,258 --> 00:51:15,458
and in my heart | was prepared to let you have your way.

860
00:51:15,594 --> 00:51:20,974
But now | realize that | was right not to be too hasty.

861
00:51:21,266 --> 00:51:23,977
We can't keep a liar and a thief, Matthew,

862
00:51:24,269 --> 00:51:27,063
and you know it.

863
00:51:44,164 --> 00:51:46,082
(Approaching footsteps)

864
00:51:46,833 --> 00:51:50,837
Marilla, I'm ready to confess.

865
00:51:51,171 --> 00:51:53,131
What have you to say for yourself?

866
00:51:53,423 --> 00:51:56,009
| took the brooch because | was too overcome

867
00:51:56,301 --> 00:51:58,178
with irresistible temptation.

868
00:51:58,470 --> 00:52:01,014
| was imagining | was Lady Cordelia Fitzgerald

869
00:52:01,306 --> 00:52:02,283
and | just had to wear the brooch

870
00:52:02,307 --> 00:52:05,018
over the footbridge on the Lake of Shining Waters

871
00:52:05,310 --> 00:52:09,022
with the wind blowing my auburn hair over to Camelot.

872
00:52:09,314 --> 00:52:12,025
| thought | could put it back before you came home.

873
00:52:12,317 --> 00:52:15,028
But as | leaned over to look at my reflection in the Lake,

874
00:52:15,320 --> 00:52:17,947
it slipped from my fingers

875
00:52:18,239 --> 00:52:21,034
and sank beneath the rippling waves.

876
00:52:21,326 --> 00:52:23,036
That's the best | can do at confessing.

877
00:52:23,328 --> 00:52:27,040
- Now may | go to the picnic?
- NO!

878
00:52:27,332 --> 00:52:30,043
| realize the importance of the brooch Marilla.

879
00:52:30,335 --> 00:52:33,046
Was it a keepsake from a tragic romance?

880
00:52:33,338 --> 00:52:35,048
You did say | could go if | confessed.

881
00:52:35,340 --> 00:52:39,052
What you can do is pack your bags

882
00:52:39,344 --> 00:52:43,139
and start imaging your life with Mrs. Blewett!

883
00:52:50,230 --> 00:52:51,230
Rachel Lynde was right.

884
00:52:51,356 --> 00:52:53,066
| can't imagine how | let that child

885
00:52:53,358 --> 00:52:55,068
worm her way into my affections.

886
00:52:55,360 --> 00:52:57,320
(Sighs)

887
00:52:57,612 --> 00:53:01,491
I'm furious at myself for having let this happen.

888
00:53:01,783 --> 00:53:04,160
Marilla!

889
00:53:31,271 --> 00:53:36,109
What ever made you say that you took it and lost it?

890
00:53:36,401 --> 00:53:39,112
You said you'd keep me In my room until | confessed,

891
00:53:39,404 --> 00:53:41,114
so | just thought up a good confession

892
00:53:41,406 --> 00:53:43,199
and made it as interesting as | could.

893
00:53:48,288 --> 00:53:51,124
But it was still a lie.

894
00:53:51,416 --> 00:53:55,128
You wouldn't believe the truth.

895
00:53:55,420 --> 00:53:58,214
You do beat all child.

896
00:53:59,299 --> 00:54:03,678
But, I'll forgive you if you'll forgive me.

897
00:54:05,305 --> 00:54:09,225
Now you get dressed for service.

898
00:54:15,315 --> 00:54:23,315
(Music)

899
00:54:24,199 --> 00:54:25,199
Hello Miss Cuthbert.

900
00:54:25,450 --> 00:54:28,161
Good afternoon Mr. Barry.

901
00:54:28,453 --> 00:54:30,163
I'd like you to meet Anne Shirley.

902
00:54:30,455 --> 00:54:31,432
- Hello.
- How do you do Mr. Barry?

903
00:54:31,456 --> 00:54:32,725
You should meet my daughter. Diana.

904
00:54:32,749 --> 00:54:35,168
She's over there in the garden. Matthew...

905
00:54:35,460 --> 00:54:37,170
For pity's sake calm down, Anne.

906
00:54:37,462 --> 00:54:40,173
And don't make any of your startling speeches.

907
00:54:40,465 --> 00:54:44,177
Goodness knows what Rachel has told them already about you.

908
00:54:44,469 --> 00:54:46,389
Ah, you'd be excited too if you were going to eat

909
00:54:46,471 --> 00:54:49,182
ice cream for the first time In your life.

910
00:54:49,474 --> 00:54:51,184
Ah, Marilla! Anne!

911
00:54:51,476 --> 00:54:55,188
Rachel; Rev. Allan, Mrs. Allan.

912
00:54:55,480 --> 00:54:58,191
Ah, this is the orphan girl that the Cuthberts are looking after.

913
00:54:58,483 --> 00:55:01,194
Anne Shirley, this is the Rev. And Mrs. Allan.

914
00:55:01,486 --> 00:55:03,446
How are you Anne?

915
00:55:03,738 --> 00:55:06,298
Well in body, although considerably rumpled in spirit, thank you.

916
00:55:06,491 --> 00:55:10,286
There wasn't anything shocking in that, was there, Marilla?

917
00:55:10,578 --> 00:55:13,206
We must try our best to relieve your jitters.

918
00:55:13,498 --> 00:55:17,293
Won't you and Anne join us for tea, Miss Cuthbert?

919
00:55:21,381 --> 00:55:25,218
I've been counting on you coming to Green Gables,

920
00:55:25,510 --> 00:55:27,554
now that you've moved into the manse.

921
00:55:27,846 --> 00:55:29,222
Hmm-mmm.

922
00:55:29,514 --> 00:55:33,226
I've given Elsbeth tremendous reports about your home baking

923
00:55:33,518 --> 00:55:36,229
and your red currant wine, Miss Cuthbert;

924
00:55:36,521 --> 00:55:38,231
she's anxious to learn your secrets.

925
00:55:38,523 --> 00:55:41,234
Marilla, I'm so pleased you could come,

926
00:55:41,526 --> 00:55:44,237
this must be the Anne we've heard so much about.

927
00:55:44,529 --> 00:55:46,239
This is my Diana.

928
00:55:46,531 --> 00:55:49,325
Perhaps Anne would like some ice cream and lemonade Diana.

929
00:55:58,418 --> 00:56:00,253
| think she's enchanting.

930
00:56:00,545 --> 00:56:01,647
Will you keep her then, Marilla?

931
00:56:01,671 --> 00:56:05,258
Well, if she can avoid catastrophe two days in a row

932
00:56:05,550 --> 00:56:08,344
| might have a chance to make up my mind.

933
00:56:17,437 --> 00:56:19,272
Marilla's given me strict instructions

934
00:56:19,564 --> 00:56:20,541
not to talk your head off.

935
00:56:20,565 --> 00:56:22,317
| do have a habit of chattering on So.

936
00:56:22,609 --> 00:56:25,278
Why if | could imagine myself as a bird,

937
00:56:25,570 --> 00:56:28,281
a magpie would probably be the closest thing | could resemble.

938
00:56:28,573 --> 00:56:31,284
(Diana laughs)

939
00:56:31,576 --> 00:56:35,288
Oh, Diana, I've always dreamed

940
00:56:35,580 --> 00:56:38,499
of being in a three-legged race at a picnic.

941
00:56:40,460 --> 00:56:44,297
Would you do me the honor of being my partner?

942
00:56:44,589 --> 00:56:47,300
But there aren't any other girls in it.

943
00:56:47,592 --> 00:56:49,302
You're a sturdy looking girl and I'm fast.

944
00:56:49,594 --> 00:56:52,305
| know we'd stand a good chance.

945
00:56:52,597 --> 00:56:54,307
- | guess so.
- Come on!

946
00:56:54,599 --> 00:57:02,599
(Music)

947
00:57:05,610 --> 00:57:07,320
Hey Diana, who's your friend?

948
00:57:07,612 --> 00:57:09,489
Anne Shirley.

949
00:57:12,492 --> 00:57:15,328
(Low hum of excited chatter)

950
00:57:15,620 --> 00:57:17,580
On your marks, get set...

951
00:57:17,872 --> 00:57:20,333
(Pistol fires)

952
00:57:20,625 --> 00:57:22,418
(Cheering on)

953
00:57:27,507 --> 00:57:30,343
(Whistling, lively chatter)

954
00:57:30,635 --> 00:57:31,635
Great!

955
00:57:31,928 --> 00:57:34,088
| never expected a daughter of mine to outrun the boys.

956
00:57:36,516 --> 00:57:39,435
I'm very proud of you, Diana.

957
00:57:48,736 --> 00:57:52,365
| think we were heroic winners Diana, don't you?

958
00:57:52,657 --> 00:57:54,635
Well, | think it's a shame that Gilbert had to lose

959
00:57:54,659 --> 00:57:56,369
on account of Moody.

960
00:57:56,661 --> 00:57:59,372
Don't you think Gilbert's handsome?

961
00:57:59,664 --> 00:58:00,641
He is handsome,

962
00:58:00,665 --> 00:58:02,375
but | think your Gilbert is awfully bold

963
00:58:02,667 --> 00:58:04,377
to wink at a strange girl.

964
00:58:04,669 --> 00:58:06,379
| wish he'd wink at me.

965
00:58:06,671 --> 00:58:08,381
He's sixteen, but he's in our class.

966
00:58:08,673 --> 00:58:11,384
His father's been ill and he's been away for two years.

967
00:58:11,676 --> 00:58:16,389
Good! | mean, | don't want to be the only one whose behind in school.

968
00:58:16,681 --> 00:58:19,392
That's Mr. Phillips, our schoolteacher.

969
00:58:19,684 --> 00:58:21,394
He's dead gone on Prissy Andrews,

970
00:58:21,686 --> 00:58:22,663
and Prissy thinks she's Queen Bee

971
00:58:22,687 --> 00:58:25,398
just because she's studying her entrance to Queen's.

972
00:58:25,690 --> 00:58:29,402
He moons over her something terrible.

973
00:58:29,694 --> 00:58:31,487
(Girls giggle)

974
00:58:32,572 --> 00:58:35,408
That's Josie Pye, and she moons over Gilbert.

975
00:58:35,700 --> 00:58:37,410
(Girls scream, splash!)

976
00:58:37,702 --> 00:58:39,412
(Diana laughs)

977
00:58:39,704 --> 00:58:41,414
Josie just wants attention.

978
00:58:41,706 --> 00:58:44,417
| hope she nearly drowns.

979
00:58:44,709 --> 00:58:46,419
| wish it had been me.

980
00:58:46,711 --> 00:58:50,423
It would be such a romantic experience nearly to drown.

981
00:58:50,715 --> 00:58:54,427
| heard before that you were kind of a strange girl, Anne Shirley,

982
00:58:54,719 --> 00:58:59,432
but | have a feeling we're going to get along really well.

983
00:58:59,724 --> 00:59:01,684
(Giggling, splashing)

984
00:59:04,604 --> 00:59:06,397
(Laughter and chatter)

985
00:59:08,608 --> 00:59:10,485
(Knocking, students quiet down)

986
00:59:10,777 --> 00:59:12,445
What is your name?

987
00:59:12,737 --> 00:59:15,448
- Anne Shirley.
- Anne spelled with an "e".

988
00:59:15,740 --> 00:59:18,451
We pride ourselves on our scholastic record...

989
00:59:18,743 --> 00:59:20,536
(Students snicker)

990
00:59:22,622 --> 00:59:25,458
And we hope that you will strive to meet our standards.

991
00:59:25,750 --> 00:59:27,460
Oh, I'm sure | will, Mr. Phillips.

992
00:59:27,752 --> 00:59:29,730
I've taught children younger than myself to read before

993
00:59:29,754 --> 00:59:31,464
and both my parents were teachers.

994
00:59:31,756 --> 00:59:35,468
I'm positive we'll have alot in common.

995
00:59:35,760 --> 00:59:37,553
You will share a seat with Diana Barry.

996
00:59:39,639 --> 00:59:44,477
- Oh, thank you, Mr. Phillips!
- Diana Barry is my bosom friend.

997
00:59:44,769 --> 00:59:47,480
Please take your seat and read your lesson.

998
00:59:47,772 --> 00:59:50,483
| must work with my Queen's student now.

999
00:59:50,775 --> 00:59:52,568
(Low hum of chatter)

1000
01:00:02,662 --> 01:00:04,580
Didn't I tell you?

1001
01:00:06,791 --> 01:00:08,501
(Giggling)

1002
01:00:08,793 --> 01:00:11,504
(Clears his throat, students quiet down)

1003
01:00:11,796 --> 01:00:14,507
All right, class, take out your notebooks.

1004
01:00:14,799 --> 01:00:17,593
Memorize the dictation from yesterday.

1005
01:00:24,225 --> 01:00:25,518
(Loud gasp)

1006
01:00:25,810 --> 01:00:27,603
(Students laugh)

1007
01:00:30,398 --> 01:00:31,816
(Students quiet down)

1008
01:00:39,699 --> 01:00:41,534
Hey Carrots!

1009
01:00:41,826 --> 01:00:43,619
Carrots!

1010
01:00:46,706 --> 01:00:48,541
How dare you!

1011
01:00:48,833 --> 01:00:50,543
(Tablet cracks, shocked gasps)

1012
01:00:50,835 --> 01:00:53,546
Anne Shirley, what is the meaning of this?

1013
01:00:53,838 --> 01:00:56,549
- It was my fault, sir.
- |... was teasing her.

1014
01:00:56,841 --> 01:01:00,553
You will stand at the blackboard for the rest of the day.

1015
01:01:00,845 --> 01:01:04,390
| will not tolerate this kind of vindictive temperament in my class.

1016
01:01:06,726 --> 01:01:10,938
Anne Shirley

1017
01:01:11,314 --> 01:01:17,153
has a very... bad... temper.

1018
01:01:19,739 --> 01:01:21,059
And she will learn to control it.

1019
01:01:21,115 --> 01:01:24,660
You will write this 100 times before leaving today.

1020
01:01:34,378 --> 01:01:35,838
(Playful giggling)

1021
01:01:37,757 --> 01:01:39,592
Hey, Anne wait!

1022
01:01:39,884 --> 01:01:41,594
I'm sorry for teasing you about your hair.

1023
01:01:41,886 --> 01:01:44,680
Don't be mad at me for keeps.

1024
01:01:46,766 --> 01:01:49,602
Oh Anne, how could you?

1025
01:01:49,894 --> 01:01:52,605
Gilbert always makes fun of the girls.

1026
01:01:52,897 --> 01:01:54,607
He calls me crow-head all the time

1027
01:01:54,899 --> 01:01:57,151
and I've never heard him apologize before.

1028
01:01:57,443 --> 01:01:59,803
There's a world of difference between being called crow-head

1029
01:01:59,862 --> 01:02:01,572
and being called carrots.

1030
01:02:01,864 --> 01:02:03,616
| shall never forgive Gilbert Blythe.

1031
01:02:03,908 --> 01:02:05,618
An iron has entered my soul, Diana.

1032
01:02:05,910 --> 01:02:07,620
My mind is made up.

1033
01:02:07,912 --> 01:02:10,623
My red hair is a curse.

1034
01:02:10,915 --> 01:02:13,626
Anne Shirley, I've heard all about it!

1035
01:02:13,918 --> 01:02:15,628
Now you open your door at once!

1036
01:02:15,920 --> 01:02:16,897
Please go away Marilla.

1037
01:02:16,921 --> 01:02:18,756
Lam in the depths of despair.

1038
01:02:19,048 --> 01:02:20,633
Oh fiddlesticks!

1039
01:02:20,925 --> 01:02:23,636
Now you open this door at once!

1040
01:02:23,928 --> 01:02:25,721
(Latch clicks open)

1041
01:02:29,558 --> 01:02:30,558
Are you sick?

1042
01:02:30,935 --> 01:02:32,645
Go away. Don't look at me.

1043
01:02:32,937 --> 01:02:35,648
Oh, don't play innocent with me.

1044
01:02:35,940 --> 01:02:37,692
I'm so ashamed | don't know where to begin.

1045
01:02:37,984 --> 01:02:39,652
What do you mean by breaking your slate

1046
01:02:39,944 --> 01:02:41,654
over some boy's head?

1047
01:02:41,946 --> 01:02:43,656
He called me carrots!

1048
01:02:43,948 --> 01:02:45,658
| don't care what he called you.

1049
01:02:45,950 --> 01:02:49,745
You have no reason to lose your temper.

1050
01:02:50,162 --> 01:02:51,872
Anne Shirley

1051
01:02:54,834 --> 01:02:56,961
what have you done to your hair?

1052
01:02:57,253 --> 01:03:01,674
Marilla | thought nothing could be as bad as red hair.

1053
01:03:01,966 --> 01:03:04,677
Green is ten times worse!

1054
01:03:04,969 --> 01:03:06,679
You little know how utterly wretched | am.

1055
01:03:06,971 --> 01:03:09,682
| little know how you got into this fix,

1056
01:03:09,974 --> 01:03:11,684
but | demand that you tell me.

1057
01:03:11,976 --> 01:03:14,687
- | dyed it.
- You dyed it?

1058
01:03:14,979 --> 01:03:17,690
For mercy's sake child.

1059
01:03:17,982 --> 01:03:18,959
He positively assured me

1060
01:03:18,983 --> 01:03:21,694
it would turn my hair a beautiful raven black.

1061
01:03:21,986 --> 01:03:24,697
Who did? Who are you talking about?

1062
01:03:24,989 --> 01:03:28,701
The peddler we met on the road today.

1063
01:03:28,993 --> 01:03:30,703
| absolutely forbid you to...

1064
01:03:30,995 --> 01:03:32,038
(Sobbing)

1065
01:03:36,334 --> 01:03:38,544
Oh what's the use.

1066
01:03:40,004 --> 01:03:42,715
Well, | hope that this has Opened your eyes

1067
01:03:43,007 --> 01:03:45,718
to see where your vanity has taken you.

1068
01:03:46,010 --> 01:03:49,180
Marilla what shall | do?

1069
01:03:49,889 --> 01:03:53,726
I'll never be able to live this down.

1070
01:03:54,018 --> 01:03:56,729
| can't face him again.

1071
01:03:57,021 --> 01:04:00,816
Gilbert Blythe had no right to call me carrots!

1072
01:04:01,901 --> 01:04:06,822
You really smashed your slate over that boy's head?

1073
01:04:07,907 --> 01:04:08,907
Yes.

1074
01:04:09,200 --> 01:04:10,743
Hard?

1075
01:04:11,035 --> 01:04:13,746
Very hard, I'm afraid.

1076
01:04:14,038 --> 01:04:16,749
| know | should be angry, | should be furious.

1077
01:04:17,041 --> 01:04:19,752
What a way to behave your first day at school.

1078
01:04:20,044 --> 01:04:23,756
But if you promise me that nothing of this sort will happen again,

1079
01:04:24,048 --> 01:04:25,966
| won't say another word about it.

1080
01:04:27,927 --> 01:04:29,762
You're not going to send me back?

1081
01:04:30,054 --> 01:04:32,765
- L've come to a decision.
- The trial is over.

1082
01:04:33,057 --> 01:04:36,310
You will stay at Green Gables.

1083
01:04:36,602 --> 01:04:38,854
Oh, Marilla!

1084
01:04:47,405 --> 01:04:51,325
| think you may be a kindred spirit after all.

1085
01:04:58,958 --> 01:05:00,876
(Content sigh)

1086
01:05:05,631 --> 01:05:08,884
| shall never, ever look at myself again.

1087
01:05:12,972 --> 01:05:14,807
Well, you're our girl now.

1088
01:05:15,099 --> 01:05:17,893
And the prettiest one this side of Halifax.

1089
01:05:20,980 --> 01:05:24,900
All right now, stop this nonsense.

1090
01:05:28,988 --> 01:05:30,906
Some girls in books lose their hair in fevers,

1091
01:05:31,198 --> 01:05:33,826
or sell it for money for some good deed.

1092
01:05:34,118 --> 01:05:37,830
I'm sure | wouldn't have minded losing my hair like that.

1093
01:05:38,122 --> 01:05:40,100
There's nothing comforting In having your hair cut off

1094
01:05:40,124 --> 01:05:41,959
because you dyed it.

1095
01:05:42,251 --> 01:05:44,837
This is the very last of the Queen Anne's Lace

1096
01:05:45,129 --> 01:05:46,106
for the summer.

1097
01:05:46,130 --> 01:05:48,841
Don't worry about your hair.

1098
01:05:49,133 --> 01:05:50,843
No one even notices it anymore.

1099
01:05:51,135 --> 01:05:52,845
Every time | look at myself | do penance

1100
01:05:53,137 --> 01:05:54,847
by seeing how ugly | am.

1101
01:05:55,139 --> 01:05:58,100
| don't even try to imagine it away.

1102
01:06:09,028 --> 01:06:10,946
Meet... at...

1103
01:06:13,115 --> 01:06:14,950
Dryad's... Bubble.

1104
01:06:18,037 --> 01:06:19,955
(Shade rolls)

1105
01:06:37,056 --> 01:06:39,892
Diana, aren't you supposed to be studying?

1106
01:06:40,184 --> 01:06:41,904
| know, but! Had to talk to you right away.

1107
01:06:42,061 --> 01:06:43,896
That's why | used the white flags.

1108
01:06:44,188 --> 01:06:45,165
Well?

1109
01:06:45,189 --> 01:06:47,900
Just let me catch my breath.

1110
01:06:48,192 --> 01:06:49,902
Mother thought | was upstairs studying,

1111
01:06:50,194 --> 01:06:51,954
but | was in the pantry getting some cookies.

1112
01:06:52,196 --> 01:06:54,907
And | overheard her talking with Mrs. Blair.

1113
01:06:55,199 --> 01:06:57,910
They were talking about what happened with you and Gilbert Blythe.

1114
01:06:58,202 --> 01:07:00,996
And mother says you have a disposition just like Marilla's.

1115
01:07:01,288 --> 01:07:03,916
She said something about Marilla having been betrothed once,

1116
01:07:04,208 --> 01:07:05,918
many years ago,

1117
01:07:06,210 --> 01:07:08,921
but because of a quarrel she never married.

1118
01:07:09,213 --> 01:07:11,924
And she's had to live with her brother ever since.

1119
01:07:12,216 --> 01:07:14,468
- So that's it!
- What?

1120
01:07:16,095 --> 01:07:19,932
Poor Marilla's been thwarted in love.

1121
01:07:20,224 --> 01:07:23,686
Must have been a supremely tragic romance.

1122
01:07:25,104 --> 01:07:26,939
Did they say anything else?

1123
01:07:27,231 --> 01:07:29,942
No but I'll keep my ears open.

1124
01:07:30,234 --> 01:07:33,028
| have to go now, mother doesn't know I'm gone.

1125
01:07:35,114 --> 01:07:36,949
Good luck on the exam tomorrow.

1126
01:07:37,241 --> 01:07:39,952
- You too.
- | hope you stand first.

1127
01:07:40,244 --> 01:07:43,038
1am indebted to you for life.

1128
01:07:48,669 --> 01:07:56,669
(Music)

1129
01:08:04,268 --> 01:08:05,245
(Rings bell)

1130
01:08:05,269 --> 01:08:07,980
All right class, time's up.

1131
01:08:08,272 --> 01:08:11,984
Place your pencils beside your papers.

1132
01:08:12,276 --> 01:08:14,987
I'll collect your papers once everyone has left.

1133
01:08:15,279 --> 01:08:17,990
However, before everyone leaves for lunch,

1134
01:08:18,282 --> 01:08:22,995
| would like to announce the mathematics half term results.

1135
01:08:23,287 --> 01:08:25,998
The three best standings are as follows:

1136
01:08:26,290 --> 01:08:31,795
First... Gilbert Blythe. Second... Anne Shirley.

1137
01:08:34,173 --> 01:08:37,009
Third... Prissy Andrews.

1138
01:08:37,301 --> 01:08:39,011
(Students murmur)

1139
01:08:39,303 --> 01:08:41,223
| think Miss Andrews has shown excellent progress

1140
01:08:41,305 --> 01:08:43,015
under my tutelage.

1141
01:08:43,307 --> 01:08:45,017
(Giggles)

1142
01:08:45,309 --> 01:08:47,102
Class dismissed.

1143
01:08:57,196 --> 01:09:00,032
He was only smiling to congratulate you, Anne!

1144
01:09:00,324 --> 01:09:03,118
| think he was trying to rub it in.

1145
01:09:07,206 --> 01:09:09,124
(Low buzz of chatter)

1146
01:09:12,211 --> 01:09:15,130
- They won't come.
- They're chicken.

1147
01:09:17,216 --> 01:09:19,136
Can we take a shortcut through the pasture, Anne,

1148
01:09:19,343 --> 01:09:20,719
or we'll be late.

1149
01:09:21,011 --> 01:09:22,322
Don't be afraid of the bullies, Diana.

1150
01:09:22,346 --> 01:09:24,226
We'll be completely civilized and take the road.

1151
01:09:24,348 --> 01:09:26,108
| have no intention of arriving out of breath

1152
01:09:26,350 --> 01:09:29,061
- for our examination.
- But -

1153
01:09:29,353 --> 01:09:31,146
(Boys yelling)

1154
01:09:32,815 --> 01:09:33,899
(Diana screams)

1155
01:09:34,358 --> 01:09:36,068
Charlie Sloane you measly, little bully.

1156
01:09:36,360 --> 01:09:38,070
You ought to be horsewhipped!

1157
01:09:38,362 --> 01:09:39,362
Run!

1158
01:09:48,247 --> 01:09:50,040
This will be the last time

1159
01:09:50,332 --> 01:09:52,172
| catch you little troublemakers in my pasture.

1160
01:09:52,376 --> 01:09:54,086
These fields are not a free-for-all.

1161
01:09:54,378 --> 01:09:57,089
You frighten my cattle to death and they won't be milked.

1162
01:09:57,381 --> 01:10:00,092
|'ve never even set a toenail on your pasture before, Mr. Sadler.

1163
01:10:00,384 --> 01:10:02,594
| was really attempting to avenge my bosom friend Diana

1164
01:10:02,886 --> 01:10:04,096
from being tortured.

1165
01:10:04,388 --> 01:10:06,241
Your cattle are such mournful looking creatures.

1166
01:10:06,265 --> 01:10:07,367
You can't know how utterly wretched | feel

1167
01:10:07,391 --> 01:10:08,535
to have you think I'd frighten them.

1168
01:10:08,559 --> 01:10:10,102
You'll feel wretched all right missy,

1169
01:10:10,394 --> 01:10:13,105
if | ever catch any of you on my land again.

1170
01:10:13,397 --> 01:10:16,108
Now, hop to it before | tan your backside.

1171
01:10:16,400 --> 01:10:21,113
| intend to put a stop to this, once and for all.

1172
01:10:21,405 --> 01:10:25,534
| don't know what education on this island is coming to, Phillips.

1173
01:10:25,826 --> 01:10:28,120
You're the worst teacher this school has ever had.

1174
01:10:28,412 --> 01:10:31,123
The order you keep is scandalous.

1175
01:10:31,415 --> 01:10:33,125
You're not worth half of what we pay you.

1176
01:10:33,417 --> 01:10:35,457
And | know for a fact that you'd never got this post

1177
01:10:35,711 --> 01:10:38,130
if your uncle hadn't been the trustee.

1178
01:10:38,422 --> 01:10:41,133
| suggest if you value your job at all

1179
01:10:41,425 --> 01:10:44,136
you discipline your students a little better

1180
01:10:44,428 --> 01:10:47,139
and keep them out of trouble

1181
01:10:47,431 --> 01:10:49,141
and out of my fields.

1182
01:10:49,433 --> 01:10:51,226
- But... Sir!
- Good day to you.

1183
01:10:59,318 --> 01:11:02,196
Since you seem to be so fond of the boy's company,

1184
01:11:02,488 --> 01:11:06,158
we shall indulge your taste for it this afternoon.

1185
01:11:06,450 --> 01:11:09,161
Take your seat over there, next to Gilbert Blythe.

1186
01:11:09,453 --> 01:11:12,164
(Shocked murmurs)

1187
01:11:12,456 --> 01:11:14,166
Did you hear what | said?

1188
01:11:14,458 --> 01:11:16,168
Yes si.

1189
01:11:16,460 --> 01:11:18,170
But | didn't suppose you really meant it.

1190
01:11:18,462 --> 01:11:20,172
| assure you, | did.

1191
01:11:20,464 --> 01:11:23,258
Obey me at once.

1192
01:11:35,354 --> 01:11:39,191
All right, let's begin the spelling bee.

1193
01:11:39,483 --> 01:11:46,281
Miss Andrews could you give us the spelling of the word chrysanthemum.

1194
01:11:51,370 --> 01:11:59,294
No, r-i-s

1195
01:12:00,671 --> 01:12:02,214
ansemu

1196
01:12:02,506 --> 01:12:08,428
a-n-s-m-u-m!

1197
01:12:09,388 --> 01:12:11,468
Perhaps we'll turn our attention to your spelling now

1198
01:12:11,515 --> 01:12:15,227
that your mathematics is well in hand.

1199
01:12:15,519 --> 01:12:18,313
Gilbert? Chrysanthemum?

1200
01:12:22,401 --> 01:12:23,401
Chrysanthemum.

1201
01:12:23,527 --> 01:12:29,199
C-h-r-y-s-a-n-t-h-a-m-u-m.

1202
01:12:31,410 --> 01:12:33,245
Hmmm.

1203
01:12:33,537 --> 01:12:35,330
Anne?

1204
01:12:37,416 --> 01:12:38,416
Chrysanthemum.

1205
01:12:38,542 --> 01:12:43,672
C-h-r-y-s-a-n-t-h-e-m-u-m.

1206
01:12:44,423 --> 01:12:46,675
Correct.

1207
01:12:52,055 --> 01:12:54,266
Hey Anne! How do you spell "freckles"?

1208
01:12:54,558 --> 01:12:57,352
Hey Josie, how do you spell.

1209
01:13:06,445 --> 01:13:10,282
Congratulations on the spelling test, Anne.

1210
01:13:10,574 --> 01:13:12,374
Oh well, at least you're acknowledging me now.

1211
01:13:12,576 --> 01:13:15,287
That's an improvement.

1212
01:13:15,579 --> 01:13:18,290
It is impolite to pass a person without at least nodding.

1213
01:13:18,582 --> 01:13:20,342
And so | nod out of elementary good breeding.

1214
01:13:20,584 --> 01:13:21,585
Nothing more.

1215
01:13:21,877 --> 01:13:24,296
Oh, why don't you get off your high horse?

1216
01:13:24,588 --> 01:13:27,299
Thank you for your heart-felt congratulations Mr. Blythe.

1217
01:13:27,591 --> 01:13:29,301
But allow me to inform you that next time,

1218
01:13:29,593 --> 01:13:33,305
| shall be first in every subject.

1219
01:13:33,597 --> 01:13:37,309
Anne, you've got more nerve than a fox in a henhouse.

1220
01:13:37,601 --> 01:13:39,401
| don't see any need in being civil to someone

1221
01:13:39,603 --> 01:13:41,855
who chooses to associate with the likes of Josie Pye.

1222
01:13:42,147 --> 01:13:43,315
You're just jealous.

1223
01:13:43,607 --> 01:13:45,317
- Lam not.
- Take that back Diana Barry!

1224
01:13:45,609 --> 01:13:47,319
She's jealous of you.

1225
01:13:47,611 --> 01:13:48,588
Gilbert told Charlie Sloane

1226
01:13:48,612 --> 01:13:50,322
that you were the smartest girl in school.

1227
01:13:50,614 --> 01:13:52,324
Right in front of Josie.

1228
01:13:52,616 --> 01:13:53,616
He did?

1229
01:13:53,700 --> 01:13:55,327
He told Charlie that being smart

1230
01:13:55,619 --> 01:13:57,412
was better than being good looking.

1231
01:14:03,502 --> 01:14:05,337
| might have known he meant to insult me!

1232
01:14:05,629 --> 01:14:06,606
No he didn't.

1233
01:14:06,630 --> 01:14:08,173
It isn't better!

1234
01:14:08,465 --> 01:14:10,050
I'd much rather be pretty than smart;

1235
01:14:10,342 --> 01:14:11,694
but at least | don't have to cheat like Josie does.

1236
01:14:11,718 --> 01:14:13,345
She doesn't have to cheat.

1237
01:14:13,637 --> 01:14:16,431
She just does it because she's a Pye.

1238
01:14:18,517 --> 01:14:26,517
(Music)

1239
01:14:34,199 --> 01:14:35,784
Hey Anne, wait!

1240
01:14:36,118 --> 01:14:38,286
Hurry up, Diana!

1241
01:14:38,912 --> 01:14:46,912
(Music)

1242
01:14:52,676 --> 01:14:56,388
- Well, | think he does.
- Well | think he doesn't.

1243
01:14:56,680 --> 01:14:59,391
Then Bernard came to their native village

1244
01:14:59,683 --> 01:15:02,394
and fell in love with the fair Geraldine.

1245
01:15:02,686 --> 01:15:05,480
(Laughing)

1246
01:15:24,583 --> 01:15:26,418
First lam pleased to announce

1247
01:15:26,710 --> 01:15:28,420
that Anne Shirley and Gilbert Blythe

1248
01:15:28,712 --> 01:15:31,423
have tied for first place honors in the term finals.

1249
01:15:31,715 --> 01:15:33,508
(Students holler)

1250
01:15:41,600 --> 01:15:44,895
And now... the sad news.

1251
01:15:47,606 --> 01:15:50,442
I'm leaving Avonlea.

1252
01:15:50,734 --> 01:15:52,444
| shall not be with you in the Fall,

1253
01:15:52,736 --> 01:15:57,449
to guide your progress to even greater heights of scholastic achievement.

1254
01:15:57,741 --> 01:15:58,718
Let us not have tears.

1255
01:15:58,742 --> 01:16:01,536
Partings are a natural part of life.

1256
01:16:04,623 --> 01:16:08,460
To ease the pain of this news | have glad tidings.

1257
01:16:08,752 --> 01:16:10,462
We shall adjourn early this afternoon

1258
01:16:10,754 --> 01:16:13,465
and make our way to the Spurgeon farm

1259
01:16:13,757 --> 01:16:16,468
where Moody's parents have consented to host a celebration

1260
01:16:16,760 --> 01:16:18,553
in honor of my departure.

1261
01:16:19,638 --> 01:16:21,556
Nobody told me.

1262
01:16:27,646 --> 01:16:31,566
Bye. Students: Bye. Good bye, sir. Bye. I'll miss you.

1263
01:16:33,652 --> 01:16:34,652
(Low hum of chatter)

1264
01:16:38,657 --> 01:16:40,492
Father told mother that Mr. Sadler

1265
01:16:40,784 --> 01:16:43,495
was going to get rid of Mr. Phillips, no matter what.

1266
01:16:43,787 --> 01:16:48,500
And apparently the trustees are forcing him to leave because of Prissy.

1267
01:16:48,792 --> 01:16:50,502
| can't help feeling sorry for him,

1268
01:16:50,794 --> 01:16:52,674
even though he did spell my name without an "e".

1269
01:16:52,796 --> 01:16:54,506
| wouldn't feel too sorry for him.

1270
01:16:54,798 --> 01:16:58,593
He's got a position as a private tutor over in Charlottetown.

1271
01:17:09,437 --> 01:17:11,481
| Suppose some people consider it an accomplishment

1272
01:17:11,815 --> 01:17:15,527
to walk a little picket fence, Diana.

1273
01:17:15,819 --> 01:17:16,796
| knew a girl in Marysville

1274
01:17:16,820 --> 01:17:19,531
who could walk the ridgepole of a roof!

1275
01:17:19,823 --> 01:17:20,800
| don't believe it!

1276
01:17:20,824 --> 01:17:23,535
You sure couldn't, little Miss Bookworm!

1277
01:17:23,827 --> 01:17:24,804
Oh couldn't I?

1278
01:17:24,828 --> 01:17:27,539
Oh, it's a little risky, don't you think Anne?

1279
01:17:27,831 --> 01:17:29,541
Is it indeed, Mr. Blythe?

1280
01:17:29,833 --> 01:17:30,833
| dare you!

1281
01:17:30,959 --> 01:17:35,630
| dare you to walk the ridgepole of Moody's kitchen roof.

1282
01:17:38,717 --> 01:17:40,552
(Murmurs) She's gonna do it!

1283
01:17:40,844 --> 01:17:42,554
- Don't do it, Anne.
- Nevermind her.

1284
01:17:42,846 --> 01:17:44,639
It's not a fair dare.

1285
01:17:46,725 --> 01:17:50,645
| shall walk that ridgepole or perish.

1286
01:17:52,105 --> 01:17:53,648
(Nervous murmurs)

1287
01:18:01,740 --> 01:18:03,658
She's gonna fall!

1288
01:18:16,755 --> 01:18:24,755
(Music)

1289
01:18:27,224 --> 01:18:29,059
(Screams!)

1290
01:18:29,434 --> 01:18:31,144
(Landing thud, concerned gasps)

1291
01:18:31,436 --> 01:18:33,605
Oh, Anne... Oh, Anne...

1292
01:18:33,897 --> 01:18:35,607
Oh... Are you killed?

1293
01:18:35,899 --> 01:18:37,739
Just say one word and tell me if you're killed.

1294
01:18:37,901 --> 01:18:41,696
No, but | think I've been rendered unconscious.

1295
01:18:43,365 --> 01:18:44,699
(Diana sobs)

1296
01:18:50,080 --> 01:18:52,165
Thank you, Mr. Blythe.

1297
01:18:52,499 --> 01:18:54,417
| think she sprained her ankle.

1298
01:18:54,918 --> 01:18:56,758
Anne, I'll borrow a carriage and help you home.

1299
01:18:56,920 --> 01:18:57,920
That won't be necessary.

1300
01:18:58,046 --> 01:19:00,090
Lam quite capable of getting there on my own.

1301
01:19:00,382 --> 01:19:01,633
I'm going your way.

1302
01:19:01,925 --> 01:19:03,635
At least let me give you a hand.

1303
01:19:03,927 --> 01:19:04,970
Thank you, Mr. Blythe,

1304
01:19:05,262 --> 01:19:07,639
but lam going in the opposite direction.

1305
01:19:07,931 --> 01:19:09,724
Come along, Diana.

1306
01:19:11,518 --> 01:19:13,645
Anne, you should have let him help you.

1307
01:19:13,937 --> 01:19:15,730
You're in no condition to walk home.

1308
01:19:26,741 --> 01:19:27,927
Of course, you would take the long route

1309
01:19:27,951 --> 01:19:29,661
when you've sprained your ankle.

1310
01:19:29,953 --> 01:19:30,930
| wouldn't think of giving Gilbert Blythe

1311
01:19:30,954 --> 01:19:34,666
the satisfaction of helping me.

1312
01:19:34,958 --> 01:19:37,669
Why don't we cut through here. It's a lot shorter.

1313
01:19:37,961 --> 01:19:40,672
But you told me this forest was haunted.

1314
01:19:40,964 --> 01:19:42,674
| don't think it's haunted In daylight.

1315
01:19:42,966 --> 01:19:44,676
That doesn't matter.

1316
01:19:44,968 --> 01:19:46,678
It's always dark in the forest.

1317
01:19:46,970 --> 01:19:48,763
Don't be afraid, Diana.

1318
01:19:50,849 --> 01:19:51,850
Oh Anne.

1319
01:19:52,142 --> 01:19:53,768
(Crow caws)

1320
01:19:56,855 --> 01:19:58,773
(Bird chirp)

1321
01:20:00,859 --> 01:20:03,695
What kind of ghosts would you Say live in here?

1322
01:20:03,987 --> 01:20:05,697
I'm not sure there are any.

1323
01:20:05,989 --> 01:20:06,966
| only imagined it was haunted

1324
01:20:06,990 --> 01:20:09,701
because it seemed so romantic at the time.

1325
01:20:09,993 --> 01:20:11,703
- (Branch snaps)
- Ahhhh! What is it?

1326
01:20:11,995 --> 01:20:13,705
Did you see a ghost?

1327
01:20:13,997 --> 01:20:16,708
My foot caught on something.

1328
01:20:17,000 --> 01:20:19,711
Charlie Sloane says that his grandmother saw his grandfather

1329
01:20:20,003 --> 01:20:21,713
driving the cows home last year.

1330
01:20:22,005 --> 01:20:23,715
So?

1331
01:20:24,007 --> 01:20:26,718
His grandfather died two years ago.

1332
01:20:27,010 --> 01:20:28,154
There was supposed to be a white lady

1333
01:20:28,178 --> 01:20:30,722
who walked along the river bank by Mr. Hammond's saw miill,

1334
01:20:31,014 --> 01:20:32,724
wringing her hands and wailing

1335
01:20:33,016 --> 01:20:34,726
only | never actually saw her.

1336
01:20:35,018 --> 01:20:36,728
Oh, Anne, don't.

1337
01:20:37,020 --> 01:20:38,740
Perhaps she's now accompanied by Mr. Hammond

1338
01:20:39,022 --> 01:20:40,732
whose looking for his lunch.

1339
01:20:41,024 --> 01:20:42,734
Oh, | shouldn't have said that.

1340
01:20:43,026 --> 01:20:44,736
He may follow me here.

1341
01:20:45,028 --> 01:20:47,489
- (whimper) I'm scared.
- Soaml.

1342
01:20:47,781 --> 01:20:49,741
Deliciously scared.

1343
01:20:50,033 --> 01:20:52,261
Mrs. Hammond said she once felt the ghost of a murdered child

1344
01:20:52,285 --> 01:20:56,831
creep up behind her and lay Its icy fingers on her hand.

1345
01:20:59,209 --> 01:21:02,754
Charlie's grandmother is a very religious woman

1346
01:21:03,046 --> 01:21:04,964
and | don't think she would lie.

1347
01:21:07,926 --> 01:21:09,926
Do you think there might be ghosts living in there?

1348
01:21:11,930 --> 01:21:13,848
It's all right, Diana.

1349
01:21:15,934 --> 01:21:18,853
(Wood cracks, Diana screams shrilly)

1350
01:21:20,939 --> 01:21:23,858
Stop it, Diana, and help me out!

1351
01:21:24,943 --> 01:21:26,903
(Effort grunts)

1352
01:21:31,950 --> 01:21:34,786
Why did we ever come in here Anne?

1353
01:21:35,078 --> 01:21:36,788
Are you all right?

1354
01:21:37,080 --> 01:21:39,791
| think I've twisted my other ankle.

1355
01:21:40,083 --> 01:21:42,001
What are we going to do?

1356
01:21:43,962 --> 01:21:45,255
You mustn't be afraid, Diana.

1357
01:21:45,547 --> 01:21:46,798
I'll be all right here.

1358
01:21:47,090 --> 01:21:48,800
Run home find your father and ask him

1359
01:21:49,092 --> 01:21:50,292
if he'll come back and get me.

1360
01:21:50,427 --> 01:21:52,804
What if you get carried off by a ghost?

1361
01:21:53,096 --> 01:21:54,806
I'd never forgive myself!

1362
01:21:55,098 --> 01:21:56,975
Be brave Diana! Go!

1363
01:21:57,267 --> 01:21:59,811
| shall endure until your return,

1364
01:22:00,103 --> 01:22:01,813
although | may be forced to faint

1365
01:22:02,105 --> 01:22:04,816
if my imagination gets the better of me.

1366
01:22:05,108 --> 01:22:08,820
| could never be as brave as you are, Anne.

1367
01:22:09,112 --> 01:22:10,947
Good bye.

1368
01:22:12,490 --> 01:22:15,160
(Sobbing)

1369
01:22:22,000 --> 01:22:23,918
(Woodpecker pecks)

1370
01:22:30,008 --> 01:22:31,634
(Owl hoots)

1371
01:22:31,968 --> 01:22:34,345
(Branch snaps, wings beating)

1372
01:22:34,637 --> 01:22:36,931
(Sighs, thuds lightly)

1373
01:22:43,021 --> 01:22:44,397
What's happened to her?

1374
01:22:44,689 --> 01:22:47,150
- Don't fear, Marilla.
- She's all right.

1375
01:22:50,028 --> 01:22:51,130
Don't be very frightened, Marilla.

1376
01:22:51,154 --> 01:22:53,865
| fell off the ridgepole at Moody Spurgeon's

1377
01:22:54,157 --> 01:22:57,285
and then | twisted my other ankle falling into an old well.

1378
01:22:57,577 --> 01:22:59,871
| should have known that you'd start the summer this way.

1379
01:23:00,163 --> 01:23:01,873
Barely the last day of school.

1380
01:23:02,165 --> 01:23:03,875
Marilla, look on the bright side.

1381
01:23:04,167 --> 01:23:05,877
| might have broken my neck.

1382
01:23:06,169 --> 01:23:08,929
And what would you have done if Someone dared you to walk a ridgepole?

1383
01:23:09,172 --> 01:23:11,883
| would have stayed on firm ground and let them dare away.

1384
01:23:12,175 --> 01:23:14,886
Oh now, Marilla, don't be too hard on her.

1385
01:23:15,178 --> 01:23:17,889
| think she's doing a pretty good job being hard on herself.

1386
01:23:18,181 --> 01:23:20,061
Especially considering that she took first place

1387
01:23:20,183 --> 01:23:21,893
in the term results.

1388
01:23:22,185 --> 01:23:23,895
Tied for first.

1389
01:23:24,187 --> 01:23:26,981
Tied for first place, why Anne.

1390
01:23:28,942 --> 01:23:32,862
Well, John Barry, it's certain that she didn't injure her tongue.

1391
01:23:33,196 --> 01:23:34,906
Come on.

1392
01:23:35,198 --> 01:23:42,372
(Music)

1393
01:23:42,664 --> 01:23:45,041
You clutter up the house too much with outside things.

1394
01:23:45,333 --> 01:23:48,920
Don't we have enough flowers right outside our door?

1395
01:23:49,212 --> 01:23:51,132
| want the house to look flowery to impress Diana

1396
01:23:51,214 --> 01:23:52,924
when she arrives for tea.

1397
01:23:53,216 --> 01:23:55,260
May | use the rosebud spray tea set, Marilla?

1398
01:23:55,552 --> 01:23:58,930
No, the everyday set will do for your company.

1399
01:23:59,222 --> 01:24:02,934
You may have the fruitcake and the cherry preserves.

1400
01:24:03,226 --> 01:24:04,936
And there's a bottle of raspberry cordial

1401
01:24:05,228 --> 01:24:06,938
on the shelf in the kitchen.

1402
01:24:07,230 --> 01:24:09,941
Now, tell Matthew that Mirs. Allan will drive me back.

1403
01:24:10,233 --> 01:24:12,944
But I'll be late coming back from the Ladies' Aid Society

1404
01:24:13,236 --> 01:24:16,948
so you must see that Matthew and Jerry's supper

1405
01:24:17,240 --> 01:24:20,952
is laid out for them. (Knock on door)

1406
01:24:21,244 --> 01:24:22,954
Good afternoon, Miss Cuthbert.

1407
01:24:23,246 --> 01:24:24,956
Good afternoon, Diana.

1408
01:24:25,248 --> 01:24:29,961
Now Anne | trust that you will be responsible for your guest.

1409
01:24:30,253 --> 01:24:32,964
I'll be the perfect hostess.

1410
01:24:33,256 --> 01:24:36,968
Have a lovely afternoon, ladies.

1411
01:24:37,260 --> 01:24:40,054
Can | take your parasol?

1412
01:24:43,141 --> 01:24:46,144
So good of you to invite me to tea this afternoon.

1413
01:24:46,436 --> 01:24:49,981
Please come in and make yourself comfortable.

1414
01:24:50,273 --> 01:24:52,066
Why, thank you.

1415
01:24:57,155 --> 01:24:58,990
How is your mother?

1416
01:24:59,282 --> 01:25:00,992
Very well, thank you.

1417
01:25:01,284 --> 01:25:05,079
| saw Mr. Cuthbert hauling potatoes to the "Lily Sands" boat this afternoon.

1418
01:25:05,371 --> 01:25:07,999
Yes. Our crop is quite good

1419
01:25:08,291 --> 01:25:12,003
we were fortunate to have hired Jerry Buote to help us with the harvest.

1420
01:25:12,295 --> 01:25:14,005
Have you picked any apples yet?

1421
01:25:14,297 --> 01:25:15,274
Ever so many.

1422
01:25:15,298 --> 01:25:18,009
Marilla has been cooking and baking endlessly.

1423
01:25:18,301 --> 01:25:21,012
We even have pies and cakes and preserves to last us for years.

1424
01:25:21,304 --> 01:25:24,182
Oh, it isn't good manners to tell your guest what you're serving,

1425
01:25:24,474 --> 01:25:27,101
so | won't tell you what she said we could have to drink.

1426
01:25:54,671 --> 01:25:58,424
Oh! Raspberry cordial, right?

1427
01:25:59,217 --> 01:26:00,217
Oh, that's my favorite.

1428
01:26:00,343 --> 01:26:02,053
You mean you've had it before?

1429
01:26:02,345 --> 01:26:05,056
Lots of times. Haven't you?

1430
01:26:05,348 --> 01:26:07,058
| must admit | have never tasted it.

1431
01:26:07,350 --> 01:26:10,061
But you can have as much as you like.

1432
01:26:10,353 --> 01:26:12,063
| have to stir up the fire.

1433
01:26:12,355 --> 01:26:14,065
There are so many responsibilities

1434
01:26:14,357 --> 01:26:17,151
on a person's mind when they're housekeeping.

1435
01:26:19,237 --> 01:26:27,237
(Music)

1436
01:26:34,377 --> 01:26:42,377
(Music)

1437
01:26:48,391 --> 01:26:51,102
It's awfully nice, Anne.

1438
01:26:51,394 --> 01:26:53,104
It's much better than Mrs. Lynde's.

1439
01:26:53,396 --> 01:26:56,107
She brags about hers all the time.

1440
01:26:56,399 --> 01:26:57,376
I'm not surprised it's better.

1441
01:26:57,400 --> 01:27:00,111
Of course Marilla is a famous cook.

1442
01:27:00,403 --> 01:27:03,197
It doesn't taste a bit like it.

1443
01:27:05,283 --> 01:27:07,118
She's trying to teach me how to cook

1444
01:27:07,410 --> 01:27:11,122
but | assure you Diana, lam a dismal failure.

1445
01:27:11,414 --> 01:27:14,125
There's so little scope for imagination in cookery.

1446
01:27:14,417 --> 01:27:17,211
You simply have to go by the rules.

1447
01:27:19,297 --> 01:27:22,216
(Liquid pours, bottle clinks)

1448
01:27:23,301 --> 01:27:25,136
Last time | made a cake

1449
01:27:25,428 --> 01:27:29,140
| forgot to put the flour in it.

1450
01:27:29,432 --> 01:27:33,144
| was thinking up the loveliest story about us Diana.

1451
01:27:33,436 --> 01:27:37,148
| imagined you were desperately ill with smallpox

1452
01:27:37,440 --> 01:27:40,151
and when everyone deserted you | went boldly to your bedside

1453
01:27:40,443 --> 01:27:43,154
and nursed you back to life.

1454
01:27:43,446 --> 01:27:46,157
And then | took smallpox and died

1455
01:27:46,449 --> 01:27:49,160
and you planted a rosebush by my grave

1456
01:27:49,452 --> 01:27:52,163
and watered it with your tears

1457
01:27:52,455 --> 01:27:56,167
because you never, ever forgot the friend of your youth

1458
01:27:56,459 --> 01:27:59,170
who sacrificed her life for yours.

1459
01:27:59,462 --> 01:28:01,255
(Giggling)

1460
01:28:04,342 --> 01:28:08,179
(Gulps)

1461
01:28:08,471 --> 01:28:11,182
lt was such a pathetic story and | was crying so

1462
01:28:11,474 --> 01:28:14,185
that | forgot to put the flour in the cake.

1463
01:28:14,477 --> 01:28:16,187
The cake was a dismal failure.

1464
01:28:16,479 --> 01:28:19,190
Flour is so essential to cakes, you know.

1465
01:28:19,482 --> 01:28:23,277
There were bubbles all over the inside of the stove.

1466
01:28:30,368 --> 01:28:32,203
It was amess.

1467
01:28:32,495 --> 01:28:34,205
Marilla was furious.

1468
01:28:34,497 --> 01:28:36,207
(Groans)

1469
01:28:36,499 --> 01:28:39,210
| don't wonder I'm such a trial to her.

1470
01:28:39,502 --> 01:28:43,256
Oh, | feel sick.

1471
01:28:44,382 --> 01:28:46,217
Oh, | gotta go home.

1472
01:28:46,509 --> 01:28:48,219
Diana, you haven't eaten yet.

1473
01:28:48,511 --> 01:28:50,231
A piece of cake and another glass of cordial

1474
01:28:50,513 --> 01:28:52,056
will be just the thing.

1475
01:28:52,348 --> 01:28:53,348
Please have some.

1476
01:28:56,394 --> 01:28:58,312
Oh...

1477
01:28:59,397 --> 01:29:01,190
Oh...

1478
01:29:05,403 --> 01:29:07,321
Here.

1479
01:29:13,411 --> 01:29:16,330
(Diana moans)

1480
01:29:19,417 --> 01:29:21,252
Diana, you can't be sick.

1481
01:29:21,544 --> 01:29:22,545
Wake up.

1482
01:29:22,837 --> 01:29:25,256
| gotta go home.

1483
01:29:25,548 --> 01:29:27,258
No, lie down, you'll feel better.

1484
01:29:27,550 --> 01:29:29,260
Now tell me, where does it hurt?

1485
01:29:29,552 --> 01:29:32,263
| gotta go home.

1486
01:29:32,555 --> 01:29:34,265
(Moans)

1487
01:29:34,557 --> 01:29:36,434
Oh, I'm awfully dizzy.

1488
01:29:36,726 --> 01:29:38,269
Oh, Oh, Oww.

1489
01:29:38,561 --> 01:29:39,538
It's probably the smallpox epidemic.

1490
01:29:39,562 --> 01:29:42,273
Don't worry, Diana! I'll never forsake you.

1491
01:29:42,565 --> 01:29:44,275
I'll nurse you back to health!

1492
01:29:44,567 --> 01:29:47,361
But please stay until after tea.

1493
01:30:03,586 --> 01:30:05,212
(Grunts)

1494
01:30:19,477 --> 01:30:21,395
(Chair scrapes)

1495
01:30:26,484 --> 01:30:30,321
Oh... What on earth's the matter, Diana?

1496
01:30:30,613 --> 01:30:32,323
(Burps)

1497
01:30:32,615 --> 01:30:35,326
She's drunk!

1498
01:30:35,618 --> 01:30:39,330
Anne Shirley, what did you give my Diana to drink?

1499
01:30:39,622 --> 01:30:42,333
Only raspberry cordial, Mrs. Barry.

1500
01:30:42,625 --> 01:30:43,602
Cordial my foot!

1501
01:30:43,626 --> 01:30:47,338
The girl smells like Jake Griffith's distillery!

1502
01:30:47,630 --> 01:30:49,340
(Vomits)

1503
01:30:49,632 --> 01:30:52,343
Drunk! My daughter is drunk!

1504
01:30:52,635 --> 01:30:56,347
And Mrs. Lynde the chairwoman of the temperance society!

1505
01:30:56,639 --> 01:30:59,350
You are a wicked, wicked girl, Anne Shirley!

1506
01:30:59,642 --> 01:31:01,352
It was against my better judgment

1507
01:31:01,644 --> 01:31:03,354
to let Diana associate with an orphan

1508
01:31:03,646 --> 01:31:04,623
and | have been proven right!

1509
01:31:04,647 --> 01:31:07,441
Diana will never see you again.

1510
01:31:10,528 --> 01:31:12,655
Leave our property at once.

1511
01:31:21,497 --> 01:31:24,375
Drunk! What on earth did you give her?

1512
01:31:24,667 --> 01:31:26,377
Only raspberry cordial.

1513
01:31:26,669 --> 01:31:28,379
She had three glasses of it

1514
01:31:28,671 --> 01:31:30,381
but | didn't know it would set her drunk.

1515
01:31:30,673 --> 01:31:32,383
You certainly have a genius for trouble.

1516
01:31:32,675 --> 01:31:35,386
- This is currant wine.
- Can't you tell the difference?

1517
01:31:35,678 --> 01:31:38,389
I've never tasted either.

1518
01:31:38,681 --> 01:31:41,392
Stop crying. It wasn't your fault.

1519
01:31:41,684 --> 01:31:45,271
| probably put the cordial in the cellar instead of the pantry.

1520
01:31:45,563 --> 01:31:46,689
I'll go over and explain.

1521
01:31:59,577 --> 01:32:01,662
Marilla, | don't believe a word of it.

1522
01:32:01,954 --> 01:32:05,416
Anne Shirley is a conniving, manipulative child

1523
01:32:05,708 --> 01:32:07,418
and she's pulled the wool over your eyes.

1524
01:32:07,710 --> 01:32:10,421
I've always warned you about making that currant wine, Marilla.

1525
01:32:10,713 --> 01:32:12,793
You said it wouldn't have the least effect on anyone.

1526
01:32:13,007 --> 01:32:15,426
Well, | ask you!

1527
01:32:15,718 --> 01:32:19,430
It isn't meant to be drunk three tumblerfuls at a time.

1528
01:32:19,722 --> 01:32:22,433
And if | had a child that was so greedy

1529
01:32:22,725 --> 01:32:25,436
I'd sober her up with a darn good spanking!

1530
01:32:25,728 --> 01:32:28,439
Oh, so it's my Diana's fault, Is it?

1531
01:32:28,731 --> 01:32:30,441
It's the demon liquor's fault

1532
01:32:30,733 --> 01:32:32,443
and as I've told you for years,

1533
01:32:32,735 --> 01:32:34,713
if you didn't insist on making that currant wine...

1534
01:32:34,737 --> 01:32:38,449
My currant wine is famous all over the island, Rachel Lynde,

1535
01:32:38,741 --> 01:32:42,453
as you well know. And the Rev. Allan himself

1536
01:32:42,745 --> 01:32:45,456
is not opposed to taking a bit when he comes calling.

1537
01:32:45,748 --> 01:32:47,541
And as for Christian virtue,

1538
01:32:47,833 --> 01:32:50,461
making a little wine for refreshment

1539
01:32:50,753 --> 01:32:55,466
is far less sinful than meddling in other people's affairs!

1540
01:32:55,758 --> 01:32:57,551
Oh!

1541
01:33:00,638 --> 01:33:02,473
Of all the unreasonable, pig-headed,

1542
01:33:02,765 --> 01:33:04,475
self-important women that | have ever met,

1543
01:33:04,767 --> 01:33:06,477
she is the worst!

1544
01:33:06,769 --> 01:33:08,569
| don't think Mrs. Barry is a well-bred woman.

1545
01:33:08,771 --> 01:33:10,481
| don't believe God himself

1546
01:33:10,773 --> 01:33:11,750
would entirely meet with her approval.

1547
01:33:11,774 --> 01:33:14,485
Anne, You mustn't say things like that.

1548
01:33:14,777 --> 01:33:17,488
Especially in front of the minister's wife.

1549
01:33:17,780 --> 01:33:20,491
But if you left God out of it,

1550
01:33:20,783 --> 01:33:23,577
you'd have it just about right.

1551
01:33:39,677 --> 01:33:42,513
(Brush rasps)

1552
01:33:42,805 --> 01:33:44,682
This story will make a fine handle

1553
01:33:44,974 --> 01:33:47,518
for all those folks who have always been down

1554
01:33:47,810 --> 01:33:50,521
on my making currant wine.

1555
01:33:50,813 --> 01:33:54,525
| haven't even attempted it in the past three years.

1556
01:33:54,817 --> 01:33:58,529
That bottle was only for sickness.

1557
01:33:58,821 --> 01:34:00,531
Oh, don't cry.

1558
01:34:00,823 --> 01:34:02,783
| don't see it as being your fault.

1559
01:34:03,075 --> 01:34:06,453
I'm just sorry it happened at all.

1560
01:34:06,745 --> 01:34:10,541
- | must cry.
- My heart is broken.

1561
01:34:10,833 --> 01:34:13,544
The stars in their courses fight against me.

1562
01:34:13,836 --> 01:34:16,630
Don't talk such foolishness, child.

1563
01:34:29,727 --> 01:34:31,645
Excuse me, Marilla!

1564
01:34:38,736 --> 01:34:40,571
Your mother hasn't relented?

1565
01:34:40,863 --> 01:34:42,573
| told her it wasn't your fault.

1566
01:34:42,865 --> 01:34:46,577
And | cried and cried; But it's no use Anne,

1567
01:34:46,869 --> 01:34:49,914
we can't ever be friends again.

1568
01:34:54,752 --> 01:34:59,465
Diana. Will you promise never to forget me,

1569
01:34:59,757 --> 01:35:03,594
no matter what other friends come into your life?

1570
01:35:03,886 --> 01:35:06,597
| could never love anybody as much as | love you, Anne.

1571
01:35:06,889 --> 01:35:11,602
- Do you really love me?
- Of course | do.

1572
01:35:11,894 --> 01:35:14,605
Nobody has ever loved me for as long as | can remember.

1573
01:35:14,897 --> 01:35:18,609
Except for Matthew and Marilla.

1574
01:35:18,901 --> 01:35:22,613
Will you swear to be my secret bosom friend?

1575
01:35:22,905 --> 01:35:24,615
But isn't it wicked to swear?

1576
01:35:24,907 --> 01:35:26,617
We're in enough trouble already.

1577
01:35:26,909 --> 01:35:30,621
Not when you're swearing a VOW.

1578
01:35:30,913 --> 01:35:33,624
| solemnly swear to remain faithful

1579
01:35:33,916 --> 01:35:36,585
to my bosom friend, Diana Barry,

1580
01:35:36,877 --> 01:35:40,631
for as long as the sun and the moon shall endure.

1581
01:35:40,923 --> 01:35:42,633
Now you Say it.

1582
01:35:42,925 --> 01:35:45,386
| solemnly swear to be...

1583
01:35:45,803 --> 01:35:51,392
remain faithful to my bosom friend, Anne Shirley,

1584
01:35:52,810 --> 01:35:55,729
for as long as the sun and moon...

1585
01:35:56,814 --> 01:35:59,650
- Shall endure.
- Shall endure.

1586
01:35:59,942 --> 01:36:01,652
And as long as my mother doesn't find out.

1587
01:36:01,944 --> 01:36:03,028
Oh, she mustn't.

1588
01:36:03,320 --> 01:36:05,656
She can't ever!

1589
01:36:05,948 --> 01:36:08,659
| have to get back. She'll be suspicious.

1590
01:36:08,951 --> 01:36:12,454
Wilt thou give me a lock of thy jet-black tresses?

1591
01:36:12,746 --> 01:36:14,665
But | don't have any black dresses.

1592
01:36:14,957 --> 01:36:17,751
- Your hair.
- All right.

1593
01:36:19,712 --> 01:36:21,630
(Sniffling)

1594
01:36:25,843 --> 01:36:27,761
(Emotional exhale)

1595
01:36:29,847 --> 01:36:31,307
| have to go. (Kiss)

1596
01:36:32,349 --> 01:36:40,349
(Music)

1597
01:36:41,984 --> 01:36:44,695
Farewell my beloved friend.

1598
01:36:44,987 --> 01:36:48,782
Henceforth we must be strangers living side by side;

1599
01:36:50,617 --> 01:36:54,788
but my heart will be ever faithful to thee...

1600
01:37:03,840 --> 01:37:06,509
(Girls laughing, whispering)

1601
01:37:15,143 --> 01:37:18,187
- (Bell Rings)
- Girls, would you join us in the classroom?

1602
01:37:19,856 --> 01:37:21,816
And how about you? You gonna join our class?

1603
01:37:37,165 --> 01:37:38,165
(Chalk rasps)

1604
01:37:40,001 --> 01:37:41,836
Good morning, class.

1605
01:37:42,420 --> 01:37:44,213
Please sit down.

1606
01:37:46,841 --> 01:37:49,677
Lam your new teacher, Miss Stacey.

1607
01:37:50,052 --> 01:37:52,764
| want to begin by saying that I-| think it's most unfair

1608
01:37:53,431 --> 01:37:56,142
that the teacher should always have to ask all the questions.

1609
01:37:56,434 --> 01:38:00,146
And I'm hoping that you'll be enthusiastic enough about my classes

1610
01:38:00,438 --> 01:38:02,148
that you will pepper me questions.

1611
01:38:02,440 --> 01:38:05,610
| shall do my very best to live up to the standards

1612
01:38:05,902 --> 01:38:07,904
you were used to under Mr. Phillips.

1613
01:38:08,279 --> 01:38:09,906
(Students laugh)

1614
01:38:10,448 --> 01:38:13,034
But | caution you,

1615
01:38:13,367 --> 01:38:16,162
lam unfailingly strict about punctuality

1616
01:38:17,330 --> 01:38:20,166
and attention in class.

1617
01:38:20,458 --> 01:38:25,171
However, | do believe that the best teacher serves as a guide.

1618
01:38:26,339 --> 01:38:28,382
And | promise you

1619
01:38:28,674 --> 01:38:33,888
that if you are willing to put yourself under my guidance,

1620
01:38:34,305 --> 01:38:40,019
| shall do my utmost to help you form strong ideals,

1621
01:38:40,353 --> 01:38:44,065
ideals which will be the foundation of your future lives.

1622
01:38:44,482 --> 01:38:49,862
| want to look back on this class as being the brightest,

1623
01:38:50,154 --> 01:38:53,199
the most imaginative,

1624
01:38:53,491 --> 01:38:57,995
the most committed students on Prince Edward Island.

1625
01:39:01,999 --> 01:39:03,918
Let's go.

1626
01:39:05,169 --> 01:39:07,289
Come on, we're going to go over and look at that tree.

1627
01:39:07,338 --> 01:39:08,923
Come on, here we go.

1628
01:39:10,341 --> 01:39:12,134
This way.

1629
01:39:18,474 --> 01:39:20,393
Can you see? Can everyone see?

1630
01:39:20,768 --> 01:39:22,687
Would you like to have?

1631
01:39:41,539 --> 01:39:43,374
Jennifer, Andrea, join the group.

1632
01:39:44,542 --> 01:39:48,045
One two, up here, lean forward, and touch your toes,

1633
01:39:48,337 --> 01:39:50,590
touch your toes, touch your toes, touch your toes.

1634
01:39:54,093 --> 01:40:00,933
(Music)

1635
01:40:11,777 --> 01:40:13,487
Please remain after class, Anne.

1636
01:40:14,363 --> 01:40:17,158
I'd like to have a few words with you.

1637
01:40:26,292 --> 01:40:28,920
I'm disappointed in you, Anne.

1638
01:40:29,253 --> 01:40:32,882
Reading novels during geometry class is a misuse of your time.

1639
01:40:33,174 --> 01:40:35,885
Moreover, it is a deception.

1640
01:40:36,218 --> 01:40:38,888
Can you ever forgive me, Miss Stacey?

1641
01:40:39,180 --> 01:40:42,058
| promise | won't even look at Ben Hur for a whole week as penance,

1642
01:40:42,350 --> 01:40:45,770
not even to see how the chariot race turned out.

1643
01:40:46,187 --> 01:40:48,387
I'm returning this to you because | know | can trust you

1644
01:40:48,898 --> 01:40:51,400
not to let it happen again.

1645
01:40:52,151 --> 01:40:55,863
Oh, Anne, you know | want to encourage you to read literature,

1646
01:40:56,155 --> 01:40:58,115
to... develop your imagination.

1647
01:40:58,574 --> 01:41:00,493
It's a precious gift.

1648
01:41:00,952 --> 01:41:03,412
But not during geometry class.

1649
01:41:03,704 --> 01:41:07,249
Miss Stacey, | knew you were sympathetic to the human plight

1650
01:41:07,625 --> 01:41:09,835
the minute we met.

1651
01:41:12,046 --> 01:41:14,799
| understand you have a plight of your own.

1652
01:41:15,216 --> 01:41:17,927
- Diana Barry.
- We were bosom friends,

1653
01:41:18,386 --> 01:41:21,180
but, alas, her mother's refused to even let her speak with me.

1654
01:41:21,639 --> 01:41:25,434
Yes, I've had a visit from Mrs. Barry.

1655
01:41:27,895 --> 01:41:30,731
| can withstand the social persecution of being an orphan.

1656
01:41:31,357 --> 01:41:35,152
But it is a terrible injustice to be falsely accused.

1657
01:41:39,991 --> 01:41:42,827
Sometimes people don't want to hear the truth, Anne.

1658
01:41:43,285 --> 01:41:44,620
You see, it frightens them,

1659
01:41:44,912 --> 01:41:47,832
so they put up walls to protect themselves from it.

1660
01:41:48,374 --> 01:41:51,085
But what we must bear in mind is that all these trials

1661
01:41:51,669 --> 01:41:54,380
and tribulations that pop up in our lives,

1662
01:41:54,672 --> 01:41:56,966
well, they serve a very useful purpose.

1663
01:41:57,258 --> 01:41:58,801
They build character.

1664
01:41:59,093 --> 01:42:00,654
As long as we can hold on to the lessons

1665
01:42:00,678 --> 01:42:03,389
we have learned from our mistakes.

1666
01:42:03,681 --> 01:42:08,227
Remember, you can always start everything afresh tomorrow.

1667
01:42:08,561 --> 01:42:12,398
That is a tremendous consolation, Miss Stacey.

1668
01:42:12,690 --> 01:42:15,151
Tomorrow is always fresh, with no mistakes in it.

1669
01:42:15,443 --> 01:42:19,196
Well, with no mistakes in it yet. (Chuckles)

1670
01:42:19,739 --> 01:42:23,325
As far as the truth goes, don't lose heart.

1671
01:42:23,701 --> 01:42:25,911
Diana will always be your friend

1672
01:42:26,203 --> 01:42:27,747
no matter what anyone accuses you of.

1673
01:42:28,039 --> 01:42:31,959
In the end, the truth will set you free.

1674
01:42:32,293 --> 01:42:35,463
The truth will set you free.

1675
01:42:38,924 --> 01:42:44,305
(Birds Chirp)

1676
01:42:59,111 --> 01:43:03,532
(Music)

1677
01:43:09,747 --> 01:43:12,958
(Music)

1678
01:43:22,635 --> 01:43:24,553
(Paper rustles lightly)

1679
01:43:31,769 --> 01:43:33,938
Did you bring the pudding?

1680
01:43:34,230 --> 01:43:35,606
Excuse me for a minute, Marilla.

1681
01:43:35,898 --> 01:43:38,138
There's something I've got to check outside for a moment.

1682
01:43:38,275 --> 01:43:40,069
What do you have to check?

1683
01:43:42,655 --> 01:43:46,075
Oh, that girl.

1684
01:44:07,096 --> 01:44:10,891
Perhaps we should have the plum pudding without the sauce.

1685
01:44:11,183 --> 01:44:14,728
Whatever for? I've never served it without the sauce.

1686
01:44:15,020 --> 01:44:18,107
| forgot to put the cheesecloth over it last night.

1687
01:44:18,399 --> 01:44:20,109
| was imagining | was a nun

1688
01:44:20,401 --> 01:44:22,486
on my way to the altar to take the vows...

1689
01:44:22,820 --> 01:44:25,531
Well, then you were lucky that the mice had sense enough

1690
01:44:25,823 --> 01:44:27,449
to stay away from it.

1691
01:44:27,741 --> 01:44:32,329
(Knocking) Ohh, goodness gracious.

1692
01:44:32,621 --> 01:44:34,331
Who could that be now at this hour?

1693
01:44:34,623 --> 01:44:36,792
Just before supper.

1694
01:44:39,712 --> 01:44:43,424
- Oh, Miss Stacey.
- Good evening, Miss Cuthbert.

1695
01:44:43,716 --> 01:44:46,427
Well, | was just over at the Barry's and,

1696
01:44:46,719 --> 01:44:49,013
well, | thought I'd take the opportunity to stop by.

1697
01:44:49,305 --> 01:44:50,681
What has she done now?

1698
01:44:50,973 --> 01:44:52,641
| hope this kafuffle with Diana Barry

1699
01:44:52,933 --> 01:44:55,144
hasn't made her neglect her studies.

1700
01:44:55,436 --> 01:44:58,480
No, no, no, not at all, quite the contrary.

1701
01:44:58,772 --> 01:45:02,401
Anne is doing simply excellent work, which is why I'm here.

1702
01:45:02,776 --> 01:45:07,531
| wondered, would you permit her to join a special class?

1703
01:45:07,990 --> 01:45:10,534
You see, | intend to give extra classes after school

1704
01:45:10,826 --> 01:45:14,496
for those students who intend to take the entrance exams at Queens.

1705
01:45:14,788 --> 01:45:17,666
The college in Charlottetown?

1706
01:45:17,958 --> 01:45:19,418
Our Anne?

1707
01:45:19,710 --> 01:45:23,756
She's bright, and energetic, and, well, very determined.

1708
01:45:24,048 --> 01:45:25,567
| think that she could pass for a teacher

1709
01:45:25,591 --> 01:45:28,177
or even go on to the university.

1710
01:45:28,636 --> 01:45:32,139
Well, I've-I've always thought that a girl

1711
01:45:32,431 --> 01:45:34,099
should learn to make a living.

1712
01:45:34,683 --> 01:45:37,728
It's a very insecure world.

1713
01:45:38,103 --> 01:45:40,397
Well, of course she can join the class if she wants to.

1714
01:45:40,689 --> 01:45:43,234
Oh, that is wonderful.

1715
01:45:43,525 --> 01:45:46,237
Why don't you stay for supper?

1716
01:45:46,528 --> 01:45:49,240
I'm just about to set the table.

1717
01:45:49,531 --> 01:45:51,742
Oh, no, really, | couldn't impose.

1718
01:45:52,034 --> 01:45:53,244
Oh, stuff and nonsense.

1719
01:45:53,535 --> 01:45:55,246
You wouldn't be imposing at all.

1720
01:45:55,537 --> 01:45:58,249
And then you could tell Anne all about it yourself.

1721
01:45:58,540 --> 01:46:00,000
Well, all right.

1722
01:46:04,838 --> 01:46:07,466
That was a delicious dinner, Miss Cuthbert.

1723
01:46:07,758 --> 01:46:10,469
Ah, thank you, Miss Stacey.

1724
01:46:10,761 --> 01:46:14,473
Anne actually made this plum pudding herself.

1725
01:46:14,765 --> 01:46:16,433
Oh, really?

1726
01:46:16,725 --> 01:46:18,394
Well, | can hardly wait to taste it.

1727
01:46:18,686 --> 01:46:20,479
Oh, please do.

1728
01:46:24,650 --> 01:46:27,569
Don't eat it, Miss Stacey!

1729
01:46:28,779 --> 01:46:31,573
Anne, what is wrong with you?

1730
01:46:34,827 --> 01:46:38,497
A mouse drowned in the sauce, Marilla.

1731
01:46:38,789 --> 01:46:40,389
| was working up the courage to tell you

1732
01:46:40,666 --> 01:46:43,502
when Miss Stacey came in.

1733
01:46:43,961 --> 01:46:46,588
Anne, if you intend to go for a teacher,

1734
01:46:46,880 --> 01:46:49,550
you're going to have to give your feather-brained ways.

1735
01:46:49,842 --> 01:46:51,162
You're not interested in anything

1736
01:46:51,427 --> 01:46:54,388
but your silly daydreams, and nothing else.

1737
01:46:54,680 --> 01:46:56,640
| really am trying to overcome my faults, Marilla.

1738
01:46:56,890 --> 01:46:59,143
| know | chatter on far too much,

1739
01:46:59,435 --> 01:47:01,770
but if you knew how many things | want to say and don't,

1740
01:47:02,062 --> 01:47:04,606
you would give me some credit.

1741
01:47:09,361 --> 01:47:11,280
(Snickering)

1742
01:47:14,325 --> 01:47:16,076
(Laughing)

1743
01:47:24,543 --> 01:47:29,381
Well, | suppose, in the end, it was a romantic way to perish.

1744
01:47:29,673 --> 01:47:31,383
For a mouse.

1745
01:47:31,675 --> 01:47:34,470
(Laughing)

1746
01:47:36,221 --> 01:47:37,806
- Here you are, Sara.
- Thank you.

1747
01:47:38,098 --> 01:47:39,534
- Good afternoon. Diana.
- Thank you, Miss Stacey.

1748
01:47:39,558 --> 01:47:42,061
You're welcome.

1749
01:47:42,353 --> 01:47:44,073
Aren't you going to be in the Queens class?

1750
01:47:44,355 --> 01:47:48,192
Mother says | should concentrate on learning to run a household

1751
01:47:48,484 --> 01:47:51,570
instead of poring over books sO much.

1752
01:47:52,029 --> 01:47:58,327
Oh, Diana, | feel as though you've tasted the bitterness of death.

1753
01:48:09,922 --> 01:48:11,757
(claps) All right, class.

1754
01:48:12,049 --> 01:48:13,675
Let's start with the Latin verbs.

1755
01:48:13,967 --> 01:48:15,761
We'll move on to the algebra after that.

1756
01:48:16,053 --> 01:48:17,846
Please open your books at page three.

1757
01:48:19,932 --> 01:48:27,932
(Music)

1758
01:48:34,196 --> 01:48:39,368
(Music)

1759
01:48:39,868 --> 01:48:42,454
(Bells jingle, low chatter)

1760
01:48:45,958 --> 01:48:49,336
Now, be sure you get Matthew's meals on time.

1761
01:48:49,628 --> 01:48:51,755
And | shall be home tomorrow before supper.

1762
01:48:52,047 --> 01:48:53,715
Have a lovely time.

1763
01:48:54,007 --> 01:48:55,727
Do you think you'll meet the prime minister?

1764
01:48:55,968 --> 01:48:57,153
Oh, if Rachel has anything to do with it,

1765
01:48:57,177 --> 01:48:58,679
we'll more than meet him.

1766
01:48:58,971 --> 01:49:00,681
He shall be subjected to a lecture

1767
01:49:00,973 --> 01:49:02,641
on the ills of Prince Edward Island,

1768
01:49:02,933 --> 01:49:05,727
the country and the world, in that order.

1769
01:49:07,813 --> 01:49:09,731
- Bea good girl.
- Bye.

1770
01:49:12,985 --> 01:49:20,985
(Music)

1771
01:49:31,712 --> 01:49:33,505
(Sleigh bell jingle)

1772
01:49:36,383 --> 01:49:38,111
Mrs. Lynde says the country's going to the dogs

1773
01:49:38,135 --> 01:49:39,845
the way the government runs things.

1774
01:49:40,137 --> 01:49:41,722
Do you think that's true, Matthew?

1775
01:49:42,014 --> 01:49:43,432
Rachel Lynde is a grit.

1776
01:49:43,724 --> 01:49:45,684
She says, "if women were allowed to vote, '"

1777
01:49:46,018 --> 01:49:48,479
"we would soon see a blessed change.'

1778
01:49:49,146 --> 01:49:50,856
(Chuckles)

1779
01:49:51,148 --> 01:49:53,275
Which way do you vote, Matthew?

1780
01:49:53,567 --> 01:49:55,360
| vote conservative.

1781
01:49:55,652 --> 01:49:56,629
Then I'm a conservative too.

1782
01:49:56,653 --> 01:50:01,366
I'm glad, because Gil, | mean some of the boys at school are grits.

1783
01:50:01,783 --> 01:50:03,494
Ruby Gillis says, when a man is courting,

1784
01:50:03,785 --> 01:50:05,763
he always has to agree with the girl's mother in religion

1785
01:50:05,787 --> 01:50:07,498
and her father in politics.

1786
01:50:07,789 --> 01:50:09,500
Ruby Gillis knows all about courting

1787
01:50:09,791 --> 01:50:11,502
because she has three older sisters.

1788
01:50:11,793 --> 01:50:14,505
Did you ever go courting, Matthew?

1789
01:50:14,796 --> 01:50:16,548
Uh, don't know as | have.

1790
01:50:16,840 --> 01:50:19,009
Never, ever, ever?

1791
01:50:22,095 --> 01:50:24,598
Why ever not?

1792
01:50:24,890 --> 01:50:29,228
Well, | couldn't do it without talking to a girl.

1793
01:50:29,770 --> 01:50:34,566
Well, I'm sure there were many broken hearts as a result.

1794
01:50:35,692 --> 01:50:38,070
Oh, go on.

1795
01:50:39,404 --> 01:50:41,073
Ruby Gillis says, when she grows up,

1796
01:50:41,365 --> 01:50:43,125
she wants to have a line of beaux on a string

1797
01:50:43,283 --> 01:50:44,743
and make them crazy for her.

1798
01:50:45,035 --> 01:50:48,080
I'd rather have one in his rightful mind.

1799
01:50:48,372 --> 01:50:50,082
There are some things In this world

1800
01:50:50,374 --> 01:50:53,085
that even | cannot hope to understand.

1801
01:50:53,377 --> 01:50:58,173
Well, | don't know if | could comprehend all of them either.

1802
01:51:02,761 --> 01:51:04,680
(Door opens, Diana pants)

1803
01:51:06,598 --> 01:51:07,408
Diana!

1804
01:51:07,432 --> 01:51:09,192
My little sister's awful sick with the croup,

1805
01:51:09,393 --> 01:51:10,894
and Mary Jo's baby-sitting,

1806
01:51:11,186 --> 01:51:12,372
and she doesn't know what to do.

1807
01:51:12,396 --> 01:51:14,064
We can't get word to mother and father

1808
01:51:14,356 --> 01:51:16,191
because they're at the rally still.

1809
01:51:19,611 --> 01:51:21,780
- Don't worry, Diana.
- Matthew will get the doctor.

1810
01:51:22,072 --> 01:51:24,533
We're such kindred spirits, | can read his thoughts.

1811
01:51:26,451 --> 01:51:28,120
Dr. Blair is at the rally too.

1812
01:51:28,412 --> 01:51:31,123
Oh, Anne, I'm awful scared.

1813
01:51:31,415 --> 01:51:33,125
The baby can't breathe.

1814
01:51:33,417 --> 01:51:35,127
Get my coat, Diana.

1815
01:51:35,419 --> 01:51:37,212
(Sobbing)

1816
01:51:44,303 --> 01:51:47,139
Stop crying, Diana, | know exactly what to do for the croup.

1817
01:51:47,431 --> 01:51:50,017
Ipecac is an expectorant.

1818
01:51:56,273 --> 01:51:58,609
(Coughing, crying)

1819
01:52:00,986 --> 01:52:02,346
Mrs. Hammond's three sets of twins

1820
01:52:02,446 --> 01:52:04,156
all had croup regularly, Diana,

1821
01:52:04,448 --> 01:52:06,241
and it was me that treated them.

1822
01:52:12,164 --> 01:52:13,624
(Crying)

1823
01:52:17,336 --> 01:52:20,297
She's pretty bad, but I've seen worse.

1824
01:52:20,589 --> 01:52:21,649
Put some wood in the stove, Mary Jo,

1825
01:52:21,673 --> 01:52:22,673
and boil some water.

1826
01:52:22,883 --> 01:52:24,283
| don't mean to hurt your feelings,

1827
01:52:24,384 --> 01:52:27,971
but you might have thought of that before if you had any imagination.

1828
01:52:28,263 --> 01:52:30,390
Okay. Come here.

1829
01:52:30,682 --> 01:52:31,682
(Crying)

1830
01:52:36,647 --> 01:52:38,273
Diana, get a fresh change of clothes,

1831
01:52:38,565 --> 01:52:40,484
and I'll keep administering the ipecac.

1832
01:52:40,776 --> 01:52:41,776
(Crying)

1833
01:52:44,738 --> 01:52:48,033
- (Kiss)
- (Crying) Mommy!

1834
01:52:51,203 --> 01:52:53,747
(Gulps, crying)

1835
01:52:56,166 --> 01:52:59,878
(Hooves clop)

1836
01:53:02,589 --> 01:53:04,508
(Panting)

1837
01:53:10,222 --> 01:53:12,808
I've just given her the last of the ipecac.

1838
01:53:13,100 --> 01:53:15,519
Mary Jo, look after the water.

1839
01:53:15,811 --> 01:53:17,131
We'll change the mustard plaster.

1840
01:53:17,354 --> 01:53:19,147
(Crying)

1841
01:53:25,445 --> 01:53:26,988
(Flames whoosh)

1842
01:53:29,241 --> 01:53:31,159
(Crying)

1843
01:53:40,377 --> 01:53:42,003
(Sniffles)

1844
01:53:49,428 --> 01:53:51,346
(Sniffling)

1845
01:53:55,851 --> 01:53:57,769
(Hooves clop)

1846
01:54:03,275 --> 01:54:06,194
(Footsteps crunch in the snow)

1847
01:54:18,290 --> 01:54:20,333
| gave her every last drop of ipecac,

1848
01:54:20,625 --> 01:54:22,345
but it wasn't till she coughed up the phlegm

1849
01:54:22,419 --> 01:54:25,213
that she really began to improve.

1850
01:54:26,047 --> 01:54:27,966
You must imagine my relief, Doctor,

1851
01:54:28,258 --> 01:54:31,178
Some things cannot be expressed in words.

1852
01:54:31,470 --> 01:54:33,470
It would have been too late by the time | got here.

1853
01:54:33,638 --> 01:54:36,224
You saved this little baby's life.

1854
01:54:48,361 --> 01:54:51,615
(Music)

1855
01:54:52,199 --> 01:54:53,909
| can't go to school.

1856
01:54:54,242 --> 01:54:57,954
(Yawns) | just couldn't keep my eyes open.

1857
01:54:58,455 --> 01:55:00,707
But | hate to stay at home.

1858
01:55:00,999 --> 01:55:04,127
Gil will get ahead.

1859
01:55:05,337 --> 01:55:07,255
Giddup.

1860
01:55:19,351 --> 01:55:20,727
'Morning.

1861
01:55:21,019 --> 01:55:22,479
Afternoon's more like it, Anne.

1862
01:55:22,771 --> 01:55:24,272
You've slept the day away,

1863
01:55:24,564 --> 01:55:28,777
though no one's ever been more entitled to it, | hear.

1864
01:55:29,069 --> 01:55:30,779
Did you meet the prime minister?

1865
01:55:31,071 --> 01:55:33,782
What does he look like?

1866
01:55:34,074 --> 01:55:35,914
Well, he certainly didn't become prime minister

1867
01:55:36,076 --> 01:55:39,788
on account of his looks, but he's a fine speaker.

1868
01:55:40,205 --> 01:55:42,207
Shook my hand.

1869
01:55:42,499 --> 01:55:44,292
How exciting.

1870
01:55:45,627 --> 01:55:47,230
| can just imagine the thrill of the rally,

1871
01:55:47,254 --> 01:55:49,756
with all those people.

1872
01:55:50,298 --> 01:55:52,759
Mrs. Barry was over here before,

1873
01:55:53,051 --> 01:55:54,427
begging to see you,

1874
01:55:54,719 --> 01:55:56,096
but | wasn't about to wake you.

1875
01:55:56,388 --> 01:55:59,975
You're invited to dinner.

1876
01:56:00,267 --> 01:56:03,937
| should imagine humble pie is on the menu.

1877
01:56:04,521 --> 01:56:08,233
Marilla, may | go right now?

1878
01:56:08,525 --> 01:56:11,319
I'm aching to see Diana.

1879
01:56:13,071 --> 01:56:15,991
(Chuckles)

1880
01:56:18,285 --> 01:56:20,203
(Laughs)

1881
01:56:22,414 --> 01:56:24,624
I'm so ashamed, Anne.

1882
01:56:24,916 --> 01:56:27,252
You saved my baby's life.

1883
01:56:27,544 --> 01:56:30,255
| harbor no hard feelings towards you, Mrs. Barry,

1884
01:56:30,547 --> 01:56:32,267
but | hope you believe me, once and for all,

1885
01:56:32,340 --> 01:56:34,968
that | never meant to intoxicate Diana.

1886
01:56:35,260 --> 01:56:36,970
Of course, | believe you, child.

1887
01:56:37,345 --> 01:56:39,139
I'm so sorry | ever doubted you.

1888
01:56:39,556 --> 01:56:42,517
Mother says you can come with us to the Christmas ball at Carmody.

1889
01:56:42,809 --> 01:56:46,271
And we'd be honored if you'd stay the night with Diana as well.

1890
01:56:46,563 --> 01:56:48,273
It's a very special occasion,

1891
01:56:48,565 --> 01:56:51,359
and you would be our guest of honor.

1892
01:56:53,236 --> 01:56:56,406
You can calm down because you're not going.

1893
01:56:56,698 --> 01:56:59,451
For a woman so adamantly against currant wine,

1894
01:56:59,784 --> 01:57:02,704
I'm surprised she's allowing Diana to go.

1895
01:57:02,996 --> 01:57:05,540
The ball is for adults, not children.

1896
01:57:05,832 --> 01:57:07,375
But, Marilla, it's Christmas.

1897
01:57:07,667 --> 01:57:08,867
The minister's gonna be there.

1898
01:57:09,085 --> 01:57:10,170
He's giving an address,

1899
01:57:10,462 --> 01:57:11,942
and that's almost the same asasermon.

1900
01:57:12,255 --> 01:57:15,550
You heard what | said and you know what | meant by it.

1901
01:57:15,842 --> 01:57:17,802
There will be plenty of balls when you're older.

1902
01:57:18,094 --> 01:57:19,804
But | was invited to spend the night.

1903
01:57:20,096 --> 01:57:22,807
I'm to be the guest of honor.

1904
01:57:23,266 --> 01:57:26,811
Ah, well, it's just an honor you'll have to forego, eh.

1905
01:57:27,103 --> 01:57:28,813
Now off to bed.

1906
01:57:29,105 --> 01:57:32,525
This is a wound | shall bear forever.

1907
01:57:35,362 --> 01:57:37,280
Good night.

1908
01:57:48,917 --> 01:57:50,794
You'd have been proud of her presence of mind,

1909
01:57:51,086 --> 01:57:55,757
the way she saved that Barry baby.

1910
01:57:56,049 --> 01:57:57,842
Why don't you let her go?

1911
01:58:00,512 --> 01:58:04,349
You remember, Matthew, who we agreed would be raising her?

1912
01:58:04,641 --> 01:58:05,618
Ah.

1913
01:58:05,642 --> 01:58:07,402
Mrs. Barry just wants to ease her conscience,

1914
01:58:07,644 --> 01:58:09,354
and I'm not going to allow it

1915
01:58:09,646 --> 01:58:12,357
and no amount of huffing and puffing from you

1916
01:58:12,649 --> 01:58:15,193
is going to change my mind.

1917
01:58:15,485 --> 01:58:19,030
You'd let her go to the moon if she had the notion.

1918
01:58:19,406 --> 01:58:22,242
Well, | don't approve of balls.

1919
01:58:22,534 --> 01:58:25,078
Just fill her head with nonsense.

1920
01:58:25,370 --> 01:58:27,090
Fact is, Marilla, you never went to a ball.

1921
01:58:27,539 --> 01:58:32,127
Fact is, this whole idea has got you scared to death.

1922
01:58:32,502 --> 01:58:35,922
That little girl ought to have all the kindness we can give her.

1923
01:58:36,214 --> 01:58:38,967
We got no call to raise her as cheerless as we was.

1924
01:58:42,012 --> 01:58:45,682
And it ain't interfering to have an opinion.

1925
01:58:48,560 --> 01:58:51,396
Besides, it's Christmas.

1926
01:58:51,688 --> 01:58:54,274
You ought to let her go.

1927
01:59:20,592 --> 01:59:22,510
All right, you can go.

1928
01:59:25,597 --> 01:59:27,432
This is all Matthew's doing, though.

1929
01:59:27,724 --> 01:59:29,350
| wash my hands of it.

1930
01:59:29,642 --> 01:59:31,352
If you get overheated and catch pneumonia,

1931
01:59:31,728 --> 01:59:33,313
blame Matthew.

1932
01:59:33,646 --> 01:59:35,356
Oh, Marilla, | dreamt last night

1933
01:59:35,732 --> 01:59:37,452
that | arrived at the ball In puffed sleeves

1934
01:59:37,734 --> 01:59:39,454
and everyone was overcome by my regal entry.

1935
01:59:39,736 --> 01:59:40,945
Regal, my eye!

1936
01:59:41,488 --> 01:59:45,200
You're dripping dirty, greasy water all over my clean floor.

1937
01:59:46,493 --> 01:59:49,204
If | have to listen to any more of this,

1938
01:59:49,496 --> 01:59:53,291
I'll just change my mind, that's what I'll do.

1939
02:00:03,635 --> 02:00:06,554
(Chatter, sleigh bells jingle)

1940
02:00:15,939 --> 02:00:17,565
(Bell jingles)

1941
02:00:25,657 --> 02:00:29,160
Well, Mr. Cuthbert, what can | do for you today?

1942
02:00:29,452 --> 02:00:31,579
Hm. Well, now, uh...

1943
02:00:33,665 --> 02:00:37,043
I'd like, uh... Have you got any, uh...

1944
02:00:37,335 --> 02:00:39,045
Do you have any garden rakes?

1945
02:00:39,337 --> 02:00:42,507
Well, now we don't carry garden rakes in the store in December

1946
02:00:42,799 --> 02:00:44,175
but I'll check upstairs.

1947
02:00:44,467 --> 02:00:46,594
We may have one or two in storage.

1948
02:00:47,220 --> 02:00:49,597
(Bell ringing)

1949
02:00:56,688 --> 02:01:00,150
The very last one.

1950
02:01:00,441 --> 02:01:02,235
Oh, that's, that's nice.

1951
02:01:02,569 --> 02:01:05,280
Will there be anything else, Mr. Cuthbert?

1952
02:01:05,572 --> 02:01:08,366
Well, since you suggest it, uh...

1953
02:01:10,702 --> 02:01:14,622
Uh...| might want to, uh, look at some hayseed.

1954
02:01:15,623 --> 02:01:18,459
Oh, we don't carry hayseed till soring, Mr. Cuthbert.

1955
02:01:19,252 --> 02:01:20,962
Oh, certainly. Of course.

1956
02:01:21,838 --> 02:01:26,634
That will be 75 cents for the rake, Mr. Cuthbert.

1957
02:01:27,719 --> 02:01:30,638
Uh... While I'm here, uh

1958
02:01:32,223 --> 02:01:34,559
| might want to look at, uh...

1959
02:01:35,059 --> 02:01:37,353
If it wouldn't be too much trouble... Uh...

1960
02:01:38,354 --> 02:01:40,064
Yes?

1961
02:01:40,857 --> 02:01:43,193
- Sugar.
- Sugar.

1962
02:01:43,651 --> 02:01:46,446
- Some sugar.
- Oh, white or brown?

1963
02:01:48,031 --> 02:01:51,910
Oh, uh... what would you say?

1964
02:01:52,202 --> 02:01:55,580
Well, we have some nice brown sugar in stock, Mr. Cuthbert.

1965
02:01:55,872 --> 02:01:58,583
How much would you like?

1966
02:01:58,875 --> 02:02:01,920
Uh... would 20 pounds be enough.

1967
02:02:02,212 --> 02:02:04,839
Yes, I'm sure 20 pounds would be enough.

1968
02:02:05,215 --> 02:02:06,382
Mm-hmm.

1969
02:02:15,767 --> 02:02:18,645
That will be one dollar.

1970
02:02:28,780 --> 02:02:31,449
| need adress.

1971
02:02:32,283 --> 02:02:34,202
With puffed sleeves.

1972
02:02:35,161 --> 02:02:36,996
Puffed sleeves?

1973
02:02:37,288 --> 02:02:39,707
For Anne.

1974
02:02:39,999 --> 02:02:41,793
Land sakes, Mr. Cuthbert,

1975
02:02:42,126 --> 02:02:44,629
why didn't you say so in the first place,

1976
02:02:44,921 --> 02:02:47,715
now you just come with me to the window.

1977
02:02:55,807 --> 02:02:58,851
Oh, it's so beautiful.

1978
02:03:04,482 --> 02:03:06,317
Brown sugar, indeed.

1979
02:03:06,609 --> 02:03:08,319
| knew Matthew was up to some foolishness.

1980
02:03:08,945 --> 02:03:11,531
Marilla, look at the puffs.

1981
02:03:11,823 --> 02:03:13,157
They're ridiculous.

1982
02:03:13,491 --> 02:03:16,536
You'll have to turn sideways to get through the doors.

1983
02:03:16,953 --> 02:03:18,579
This can't be real.

1984
02:03:18,871 --> 02:03:20,581
| hope you're satisfied, young lady.

1985
02:03:20,873 --> 02:03:23,418
| don't want you strutting around here vain as a peacock.

1986
02:03:23,710 --> 02:03:25,145
So, Now you go upstairs and take that off.

1987
02:03:25,169 --> 02:03:27,672
| have to thank Matthew.

1988
02:03:27,964 --> 02:03:29,757
(Door opens, then shuts)

1989
02:03:32,885 --> 02:03:37,181
20 pounds of brown sugar.

1990
02:03:52,405 --> 02:03:54,157
| should have waited till Christmas,

1991
02:03:54,449 --> 02:03:58,244
but | thought you might want to wear it to the ball.

1992
02:04:05,043 --> 02:04:06,169
Don't you like it?

1993
02:04:07,003 --> 02:04:08,713
Like it?

1994
02:04:09,005 --> 02:04:09,982
It's more exquisite than any dress

1995
02:04:10,006 --> 02:04:12,800
| could ever have imagined.

1996
02:04:14,886 --> 02:04:16,721
Puffed sleeves.

1997
02:04:17,013 --> 02:04:19,807
Puffiest in the world.

1998
02:04:22,101 --> 02:04:25,229
You are aman of impeccable taste, Matthew.

1999
02:04:28,775 --> 02:04:32,403
| don't want to get your dress dirty.

2000
02:04:33,780 --> 02:04:39,243
(Music)

2001
02:04:40,203 --> 02:04:42,663
(Sleigh bells jingle)

2002
02:04:58,930 --> 02:05:01,849
(Low hum of chatter)

2003
02:05:03,935 --> 02:05:11,935
(Music)

2004
02:05:21,077 --> 02:05:24,789
Diana, enjoy yourself today, all right?

2005
02:05:25,081 --> 02:05:27,792
- Have a good time, girls.
- Look, John, it's Mrs. Cooper.

2006
02:05:28,084 --> 02:05:29,794
Martha, Elizabeth, how are you?

2007
02:05:30,086 --> 02:05:33,798
I'm positively certain this will spoil everyday life forever.

2008
02:05:34,090 --> 02:05:37,885
In three years, I'm going to wear my hair up like Alice Bell.

2009
02:05:40,263 --> 02:05:43,099
She's only 17, and | think she looks ridiculous.

2010
02:05:43,391 --> 02:05:46,811
I'm going to wait until I'm 18.

2011
02:05:49,188 --> 02:05:52,066
My, my, doesn't Gilbert look dashing tonight?

2012
02:05:52,483 --> 02:05:55,069
Gilbert? | hadn't noticed him.

2013
02:05:55,361 --> 02:05:57,071
It's too bad you've been SO awful to him.

2014
02:05:57,780 --> 02:06:01,075
He might have asked you to dance.

2015
02:06:01,576 --> 02:06:03,744
If |wanted him to ask me, which | don't,

2016
02:06:04,036 --> 02:06:05,079
he certainly would.

2017
02:06:05,621 --> 02:06:09,083
Gilbert Blythe would stand on his head for me if | asked him to.

2018
02:06:09,542 --> 02:06:11,102
Oh, he looked right at you again, Anne.

2019
02:06:11,377 --> 02:06:15,089
| bet you couldn't get him to dance with you.

2020
02:06:15,381 --> 02:06:18,176
All right, Diana, If you insist.

2021
02:06:20,011 --> 02:06:22,555
(Music ends, clapping)

2022
02:06:23,014 --> 02:06:28,478
(Music)

2023
02:06:28,769 --> 02:06:29,854
Good to see you, Rebecca.

2024
02:06:30,146 --> 02:06:31,346
| haven't seen your brother...

2025
02:06:31,606 --> 02:06:33,858
Good evening, Gilbert Blythe.

2026
02:06:34,150 --> 02:06:36,944
Tell your brother I'll be seeing him at the tobogganing party.

2027
02:06:41,032 --> 02:06:42,867
A glass of punch, miss?

2028
02:06:43,159 --> 02:06:44,952
Thank you.

2029
02:06:52,043 --> 02:06:54,962
Diana, you look wonderful tonight.

2030
02:06:56,047 --> 02:07:00,968
- Merry Christmas.
- Merry Christmas to you too, Gilbert.

2031
02:07:02,637 --> 02:07:07,475
How could you wish that person a Merry Christmas?

2032
02:07:07,808 --> 02:07:12,396
| gather that person didn't ask you to dance after all.

2033
02:07:14,065 --> 02:07:17,985
No. Would you give me the pleasure instead?

2034
02:07:20,071 --> 02:07:21,989
Thank you. | believe | will.

2035
02:07:23,074 --> 02:07:31,074
(Music)

2036
02:07:38,422 --> 02:07:46,422
(Music)

2037
02:07:56,399 --> 02:07:59,193
(Laughing)

2038
02:08:02,405 --> 02:08:05,449
| bet Gilbert took your dance card.

2039
02:08:05,741 --> 02:08:09,287
Such a romantic gesture would be utterly beyond his imagination.

2040
02:08:09,579 --> 02:08:12,290
Well, then who? Josie Pye?

2041
02:08:12,582 --> 02:08:14,959
A secret admirer obviously.

2042
02:08:15,251 --> 02:08:18,963
(Laughing) (Record scratching)

2043
02:08:19,255 --> 02:08:23,342
(Thumping) -We should get to bed before mother comes down.

2044
02:08:23,634 --> 02:08:25,970
She said we could sleep in the spare bedroom.

2045
02:08:26,262 --> 02:08:27,262
Isn't that exciting?

2046
02:08:27,305 --> 02:08:28,282
All right, then.

2047
02:08:28,306 --> 02:08:30,933
I'll race you to see who gets the warm side of the bed.

2048
02:08:32,268 --> 02:08:34,854
Ready... Get set... Go!

2049
02:08:36,147 --> 02:08:38,065
(Running footsteps)

2050
02:08:40,151 --> 02:08:43,779
(Groan) Merciful heaven.

2051
02:08:45,281 --> 02:08:47,199
What is the meaning of this?

2052
02:08:47,491 --> 02:08:49,368
Aunt Josephine.

2053
02:08:49,660 --> 02:08:51,871
Mother said you weren't coming until tomorrow.

2054
02:08:52,163 --> 02:08:54,874
Is that any reason to try to kill me in my sleep?

2055
02:08:55,166 --> 02:08:59,879
Diana Barry, you are the worst behaved girl | have ever known.

2056
02:09:00,212 --> 02:09:03,633
Your parents will certainly hear about this outrage.

2057
02:09:03,966 --> 02:09:04,966
It's all my fault, ma'am.

2058
02:09:05,009 --> 02:09:06,552
It was my idea to race.

2059
02:09:06,886 --> 02:09:08,206
And we didn't know you were here.

2060
02:09:08,262 --> 02:09:09,639
Honestly, we didn't.

2061
02:09:09,930 --> 02:09:12,090
Please don't tell mother, Aunt Jo we're terribly sorry.

2062
02:09:12,892 --> 02:09:15,019
| most certainly will tell her.

2063
02:09:15,311 --> 02:09:17,897
She'll want to know the reason why I've changed my mind

2064
02:09:18,189 --> 02:09:20,191
about the music lessons | was going to pay for.

2065
02:09:20,608 --> 02:09:24,945
You need a few lessons in behavior more than in music, young lady.

2066
02:09:25,237 --> 02:09:29,033
Now, get out of here and let a poor old woman get some sleep!

2067
02:09:35,206 --> 02:09:38,542
- This is really dreadful, Anne.
- Ohhh!

2068
02:09:38,834 --> 02:09:40,503
I've always wanted music lessons.

2069
02:09:40,795 --> 02:09:42,088
She's the only one In the family

2070
02:09:42,380 --> 02:09:43,740
who's rich enough to pay for them.

2071
02:09:44,590 --> 02:09:47,468
- We'll explain tomorrow.
- Don't worry, Diana.

2072
02:09:47,885 --> 02:09:49,279
She'll probably leave in a big huff

2073
02:09:49,303 --> 02:09:51,013
first thing in the morning.

2074
02:09:51,305 --> 02:09:52,932
But | don't care.

2075
02:09:53,265 --> 02:09:57,061
She's only father's great-aunt. We've never been close.

2076
02:09:57,436 --> 02:10:00,981
It was pretty funny, wasn't it?

2077
02:10:01,273 --> 02:10:02,983
Did you see the look on her face?

2078
02:10:03,275 --> 02:10:05,945
- (Mock gasp)
- (Laughing)

2079
02:10:08,823 --> 02:10:11,659
| won't have you lose your music lessons because of me.

2080
02:10:11,951 --> 02:10:13,661
| just have to have talk with her.

2081
02:10:13,953 --> 02:10:16,747
- Anne, don't.
- She'll eat you alive.

2082
02:10:18,582 --> 02:10:21,419
I've had lots of practice at making apologies before.

2083
02:10:21,711 --> 02:10:24,422
Just leave it to me.

2084
02:10:24,714 --> 02:10:25,714
All right.

2085
02:10:27,049 --> 02:10:34,724
(Pen rasps)

2086
02:10:35,641 --> 02:10:36,976
Ahem.

2087
02:10:38,102 --> 02:10:40,020
Ahem!

2088
02:10:40,479 --> 02:10:41,814
What?

2089
02:10:42,356 --> 02:10:44,734
Come to finish the job?

2090
02:10:45,192 --> 02:10:47,236
Sorry | startled you, ma'am.

2091
02:10:47,737 --> 02:10:48,821
Who are you?

2092
02:10:49,405 --> 02:10:53,117
- Anne of Green Gables.
- And I've come to confess.

2093
02:10:53,409 --> 02:10:55,119
Confess what?

2094
02:10:55,411 --> 02:10:58,122
I'm not interested in the confessions of assassins

2095
02:10:58,414 --> 02:11:00,750
who masquerade as little girls.

2096
02:11:05,838 --> 02:11:07,548
It was all my doing, Miss Barry.

2097
02:11:07,840 --> 02:11:08,942
Diana would never think of sucha thing

2098
02:11:08,966 --> 02:11:10,676
as racing to a bed and jumping on it.

2099
02:11:10,968 --> 02:11:12,678
She's far too ladylike.

2100
02:11:13,429 --> 02:11:16,557
Whereas I'm merely an orphan who doesn't know any better.

2101
02:11:16,849 --> 02:11:18,329
So | think you ought to forgive Diana

2102
02:11:18,434 --> 02:11:20,144
and let her have her music lessons back.

2103
02:11:20,436 --> 02:11:23,147
Oh, you do, do you?

2104
02:11:23,439 --> 02:11:25,274
Yes, Ma'am.

2105
02:11:25,566 --> 02:11:28,152
Do you any idea what it's like to be waken

2106
02:11:28,444 --> 02:11:30,154
from the few hours of precious sleep

2107
02:11:30,446 --> 02:11:33,157
granted an old woman in a strange bed

2108
02:11:33,449 --> 02:11:37,036
by two ferocious wild girls landing on her head.

2109
02:11:37,328 --> 02:11:39,038
| don't know, | can imagine

2110
02:11:39,455 --> 02:11:41,665
it must have been terrifying in the extreme

2111
02:11:41,957 --> 02:11:45,628
and if you'd any imagination, you could put yourself in our place.

2112
02:11:45,961 --> 02:11:48,297
| haven't been in your place for 47 years,

2113
02:11:48,672 --> 02:11:50,674
thank you very much.

2114
02:11:50,966 --> 02:11:54,678
Don't you have any imagination, Miss Barry?

2115
02:11:55,221 --> 02:11:58,933
At my age, imagination is a threat to life.

2116
02:11:59,308 --> 02:12:01,188
Well, we honestly didn't know you were in there.

2117
02:12:01,477 --> 02:12:03,187
And you scared us half to death.

2118
02:12:03,479 --> 02:12:06,190
You should just imagine how exciting it was going to be for me

2119
02:12:06,482 --> 02:12:07,562
to sleep in a spare bedroom

2120
02:12:07,775 --> 02:12:10,194
reserved for important company such as yourself.

2121
02:12:10,486 --> 02:12:12,166
As it was, | had to sleep with Minnie May,

2122
02:12:12,363 --> 02:12:13,683
and you don't know how she kicks.

2123
02:12:13,781 --> 02:12:17,368
Mine was the sleep of the bitterly disappointed, Miss Barry.

2124
02:12:17,660 --> 02:12:20,204
| was forced to lie awake all night

2125
02:12:20,496 --> 02:12:23,082
with the knowledge that | had cost Diana her career

2126
02:12:23,374 --> 02:12:26,001
as a world-famous concert pianist.

2127
02:12:28,379 --> 02:12:33,300
| Suppose your claim to sympathy is as valid as mine.

2128
02:12:34,844 --> 02:12:38,931
Do you know what! Am composing here, Anne of Green Gables?

2129
02:12:39,765 --> 02:12:42,226
I'm sure | don't, Miss Barry.

2130
02:12:42,518 --> 02:12:46,605
It's anote expressing my outrage to Diana's parents.

2131
02:12:48,023 --> 02:12:50,985
Trouble is, | don't feel outraged any more.

2132
02:12:53,153 --> 02:12:56,824
So... what do you suggest?

2133
02:12:57,408 --> 02:13:00,244
Perhaps if Diana apologized,

2134
02:13:00,536 --> 02:13:03,247
which she's too frightened to do at this moment.

2135
02:13:03,539 --> 02:13:05,416
| have a better idea.

2136
02:13:05,708 --> 02:13:11,672
Suppose | reinstate her music lessons in exchange for

2137
02:13:14,425 --> 02:13:19,263
you coming to visit me in Charlottetown on occasion.

2138
02:13:19,555 --> 02:13:21,265
Me, Miss Barry?

2139
02:13:21,557 --> 02:13:24,518
Yes, you, Anne of Green Gables.

2140
02:13:24,810 --> 02:13:27,187
Diana can come along as well.

2141
02:13:27,479 --> 02:13:31,191
You amuse me, and precious little in this world amuses me

2142
02:13:31,483 --> 02:13:33,193
at my age.

2143
02:13:33,569 --> 02:13:35,571
You seem a very interesting old lady to me.

2144
02:13:35,863 --> 02:13:39,283
You're not an old ogre at all, are you?

2145
02:13:39,575 --> 02:13:42,286
| didn't mean that. Excuse me, Miss Barry.

2146
02:13:42,578 --> 02:13:45,289
| most certainly am an old ogre,

2147
02:13:45,581 --> 02:13:48,292
and don't you let on any different

2148
02:13:48,584 --> 02:13:51,295
will you come and see me?

2149
02:13:51,587 --> 02:13:55,299
Then go tell Diana she can be a concert pianist after all.

2150
02:13:55,591 --> 02:13:57,217
Thank you, Miss Barry.

2151
02:13:57,509 --> 02:14:00,220
We appreciate you making up your mind so swiftly.

2152
02:14:00,596 --> 02:14:03,390
Good day, Anne girl.

2153
02:14:07,478 --> 02:14:09,313
(Small laugh)

2154
02:14:09,605 --> 02:14:11,315
You wouldn't think so to look at her,

2155
02:14:11,607 --> 02:14:15,319
but she is definitely a kindred spirit, Diana.

2156
02:14:15,611 --> 02:14:17,780
(Chuckles) Oh...

2157
02:14:19,490 --> 02:14:27,490
(Music)

2158
02:14:34,630 --> 02:14:42,630
(Music)

2159
02:14:46,642 --> 02:14:49,353
(Reading) "...Please find enclosed two silver bangles",

2160
02:14:49,645 --> 02:14:52,356
one for you, and one for the Anne girl.

2161
02:14:52,648 --> 02:14:56,151
| want to become better acquainted with you both.

2162
02:14:56,443 --> 02:14:57,629
If you come to town for a visit,

2163
02:14:57,653 --> 02:15:00,489
| will put you up in my very Sparest of spare rooms.

2164
02:15:00,781 --> 02:15:04,535
Yours very truly, Aunt Josephine Barry.'.

2165
02:15:04,827 --> 02:15:09,456
You know, Diana, kindred Spirits aren't as scarce as | used to think.

2166
02:15:20,551 --> 02:15:21,551
(Clock ticks)

2167
02:15:29,268 --> 02:15:32,229
Anne, do you intend to daydream during the Charlottetown exam?

2168
02:15:32,771 --> 02:15:34,481
Five minutes, class.

2169
02:15:38,902 --> 02:15:41,780
Well, don't worry. In two days, it will all be over.

2170
02:15:42,072 --> 02:15:43,615
This is one of those rare moments

2171
02:15:43,907 --> 02:15:47,870
when not even my imagination can solve my anxiety.

2172
02:16:11,101 --> 02:16:12,978
- Hello, Aunt Jo.
- Diana.

2173
02:16:13,479 --> 02:16:17,983
So you've come to see me at last, you Anne girl.

2174
02:16:18,275 --> 02:16:20,986
Mercy me, you're both so much better-looking

2175
02:16:21,278 --> 02:16:22,988
than you used to be.

2176
02:16:23,280 --> 02:16:25,991
- I'm sure Diana Is.
- My hair is still red.

2177
02:16:26,283 --> 02:16:28,994
Come in, come in, John, take the bags right upstairs.

2178
02:16:29,286 --> 02:16:31,413
Wipe your feet.

2179
02:16:33,624 --> 02:16:35,542
Aunt Jo.

2180
02:16:39,838 --> 02:16:42,841
You must be tired from your trip.

2181
02:16:43,133 --> 02:16:45,469
Nancy will prepare your bath and look after you.

2182
02:16:45,761 --> 02:16:49,473
| suppose you'll want to cram for your exam tonight.

2183
02:16:49,765 --> 02:16:52,476
Miss Stacey made me promise not to open a book

2184
02:16:52,768 --> 02:16:54,186
so | won't get the jitters.

2185
02:16:54,478 --> 02:16:56,198
In that case, we can have a leisurely dinner

2186
02:16:56,480 --> 02:16:58,398
after you've freshened up.

2187
02:16:58,690 --> 02:17:00,400
And following your exam tomorrow,

2188
02:17:00,776 --> 02:17:03,570
I've planned a tremendous Surprise.

2189
02:17:04,655 --> 02:17:07,491
Nancy, tell John that | will have my tea with him

2190
02:17:07,783 --> 02:17:10,577
in the palm room.

2191
02:17:18,252 --> 02:17:20,087
I've never been here before.

2192
02:17:20,379 --> 02:17:22,089
| didn't know she was this rich.

2193
02:17:22,381 --> 02:17:25,092
No wonder she has so little imagination.

2194
02:17:25,384 --> 02:17:27,104
That's the one consolation about being poor.

2195
02:17:27,386 --> 02:17:29,179
You have to dream all this up.

2196
02:17:37,187 --> 02:17:40,023
| wish | could go with you and help you somehow, Anne.

2197
02:17:40,482 --> 02:17:42,651
Don't make me nervous.

2198
02:17:42,943 --> 02:17:44,027
I'm trying to imagine away

2199
02:17:44,319 --> 02:17:49,032
this horrible fluttery feeling around my heart.

2200
02:17:49,324 --> 02:17:52,703
- | have faith in you.
- You'll pass all right.

2201
02:17:53,370 --> 02:17:54,496
|'d rather not pass at all

2202
02:17:54,788 --> 02:17:57,583
than come out somewhere in the middle.

2203
02:17:57,916 --> 02:18:00,419
Matthew, Marilla, Miss Stacey,

2204
02:18:00,711 --> 02:18:04,047
everyone has such great hopes for me.

2205
02:18:04,339 --> 02:18:09,011
(Yawns) It would be such a disgrace if...

2206
02:18:09,970 --> 02:18:12,139
Gilbert came first.

2207
02:18:13,724 --> 02:18:15,604
| suppose I'd settle for beating Gilbert Blythe,

2208
02:18:15,851 --> 02:18:17,561
if | had to.

2209
02:18:17,853 --> 02:18:22,649
Just keep thinking about Gilbert, then.

2210
02:18:23,734 --> 02:18:26,236
(Yawns)

2211
02:18:26,570 --> 02:18:30,365
Yes. Gilbert.

2212
02:18:37,623 --> 02:18:39,374
Please do not touch your papers

2213
02:18:39,666 --> 02:18:41,835
until all the examinations have been distributed

2214
02:18:42,127 --> 02:18:44,546
or they will be discounted completely.

2215
02:18:53,764 --> 02:18:56,683
You may now begin.

2216
02:19:10,072 --> 02:19:11,990
(Bell tolls)

2217
02:19:12,783 --> 02:19:15,035
Oh, for one awful moment

2218
02:19:15,327 --> 02:19:17,037
| felt exactly like | did three years ago,

2219
02:19:17,329 --> 02:19:20,290
when | asked Marilla if | was to stay at Green Gables.

2220
02:19:20,582 --> 02:19:22,960
(Chuckles) You had me worried there for a moment.

2221
02:19:23,418 --> 02:19:25,837
| could see you turning green.

2222
02:19:26,129 --> 02:19:28,131
Oh, but | Knew you'd pull through.

2223
02:19:28,423 --> 02:19:32,344
Well, pass or fail, I'M going to miss you tremendously, Miss Stacey.

2224
02:19:32,636 --> 02:19:36,556
| want to wish you all the luck in the world, Anne Shirley.

2225
02:19:36,848 --> 02:19:40,143
If anyone deserves to be successful, it's you.

2226
02:19:40,435 --> 02:19:43,188
I'll be watching out for you, even from Halifax.

2227
02:19:43,647 --> 02:19:46,149
So you really are going to leave Avonlea.

2228
02:19:46,441 --> 02:19:47,418
Hmm.

2229
02:19:47,442 --> 02:19:49,987
| have my own little set of troubles.

2230
02:19:50,279 --> 02:19:53,156
Oh, nothing to worry about.

2231
02:19:53,448 --> 02:19:58,161
Remember, true friends are always together in spirit.

2232
02:19:58,453 --> 02:20:00,038
Thank you, Miss Stacey,

2233
02:20:00,330 --> 02:20:03,250
for giving us all the chance to make something of ourselves.

2234
02:20:11,591 --> 02:20:14,511
Someone else wants to say good bye.

2235
02:20:15,595 --> 02:20:19,391
| want to remind you of something you once told me.

2236
02:20:19,683 --> 02:20:21,518
Tomorrow is always fresh.

2237
02:20:23,603 --> 02:20:27,316
There's no mistakes tn it.

2238
02:20:43,290 --> 02:20:45,500
| was born for city life.

2239
02:20:49,087 --> 02:20:51,631
And what's your opinion, Anne?

2240
02:20:51,923 --> 02:20:54,634
| hadn't thought seriously about it until now.

2241
02:20:55,010 --> 02:20:56,850
| think | would probably come to the conclusion

2242
02:20:57,012 --> 02:20:58,722
that I'd like it for a while,

2243
02:20:59,014 --> 02:21:02,726
but in the end, I'd still prefer the sound of the wind

2244
02:21:03,018 --> 02:21:04,811
in the firs across the brook

2245
02:21:05,103 --> 02:21:06,730
more than the tinkling of crystal.

2246
02:21:07,022 --> 02:21:09,733
(Laughs)

2247
02:21:10,025 --> 02:21:11,777
(Applause)

2248
02:21:19,910 --> 02:21:23,747
(Singing opera)

2249
02:21:26,917 --> 02:21:34,716
(Singing opera)

2250
02:21:35,300 --> 02:21:37,677
What do you think now, Anne?

2251
02:21:37,969 --> 02:21:40,097
| was wrong.

2252
02:21:40,389 --> 02:21:43,100
| don't see how | could ever return to common life

2253
02:21:43,392 --> 02:21:46,186
after this, Miss Barry.

2254
02:21:48,897 --> 02:21:50,941
(Singing opera)

2255
02:21:55,946 --> 02:21:59,741
Madam Selitzky had a definitive alabaster brow, Diana.

2256
02:22:00,033 --> 02:22:01,660
Did you see Alice Bell there?

2257
02:22:01,952 --> 02:22:03,792
Parading around like she had an alabaster brow?

2258
02:22:04,079 --> 02:22:06,957
Ha, if | had Alice Bell's crooked nose, | wouldn't.

2259
02:22:07,249 --> 02:22:10,794
Oh, no, | shouldn't have said that. That's uncharitable.

2260
02:22:11,086 --> 02:22:13,797
| was comparing it to my own nose, and that is vanity.

2261
02:22:14,089 --> 02:22:15,809
Someone complimented me on my nose long ago,

2262
02:22:16,091 --> 02:22:19,803
and I'm afraid I've thought about it far too much ever since.

2263
02:22:20,095 --> 02:22:24,433
| ought to hire you as my court jester, Anne girl.

2264
02:22:24,766 --> 02:22:26,685
| wasn't meaning to be funny.

2265
02:22:27,102 --> 02:22:30,063
Well, | hope you both enjoyed the matinee.

2266
02:22:30,355 --> 02:22:31,815
Oh, immensely.

2267
02:22:32,107 --> 02:22:34,901
And you, Anne?

2268
02:22:35,986 --> 02:22:37,821
Unforgettably.

2269
02:22:38,113 --> 02:22:42,409
Then you must stay with me when you come back and study.

2270
02:22:42,784 --> 02:22:44,161
Maybe I'll come stay with you too.

2271
02:22:44,453 --> 02:22:45,620
You'd both be welcome.

2272
02:22:46,121 --> 02:22:48,832
| haven't had so much fun SINCE...

2273
02:22:49,124 --> 02:22:51,751
well never mind how long since.

2274
02:22:52,043 --> 02:22:54,754
| thought Marilla Cuthbert was an old fool

2275
02:22:55,088 --> 02:22:58,800
when | heard that she'd adopted a little orphan girl.

2276
02:22:59,134 --> 02:23:01,928
But | see now which of us was the old fool.

2277
02:23:06,016 --> 02:23:08,643
- Good bye, Aunt Jo.
- Good bye, girls.

2278
02:23:08,977 --> 02:23:10,687
Bye, Miss Barry, thank you for everything.

2279
02:23:10,979 --> 02:23:12,689
It was lovely having you here.

2280
02:23:12,981 --> 02:23:14,774
- Bye.
- Bye.

2281
02:23:27,037 --> 02:23:28,917
- Hello, there, Gil.
- You on your way home too?

2282
02:23:29,122 --> 02:23:30,582
Yes, Sir.

2283
02:23:30,874 --> 02:23:32,584
Well, | wish we could offer you a ride.

2284
02:23:33,168 --> 02:23:37,422
Oh, no, that's all right. I'm meeting Moody at the station.

2285
02:23:37,714 --> 02:23:39,074
Anne, | wish you luck on the exam.

2286
02:23:39,174 --> 02:23:41,885
| hope you come in first. You've, you've worked hard.

2287
02:23:42,177 --> 02:23:45,889
Thank you, but I'm sure the first will go to you.

2288
02:23:46,181 --> 02:23:50,977
Well, | guess we'll see, won't we?

2289
02:23:56,066 --> 02:23:57,984
(Bell tolls)

2290
02:24:16,920 --> 02:24:18,129
Ruby, you be Elaine.

2291
02:24:18,421 --> 02:24:20,757
You're the only one who has golden hair.

2292
02:24:21,049 --> 02:24:22,769
| couldn't lie there and pretend | was dead.

2293
02:24:23,051 --> 02:24:25,637
I'd die of fright. Honest.

2294
02:24:25,929 --> 02:24:28,515
- You be Elaine, Anne.
- This is your idea.

2295
02:24:28,848 --> 02:24:30,648
A red-haired person cannot play the lily maid.

2296
02:24:30,934 --> 02:24:32,644
Tennyson would never approve.

2297
02:24:33,019 --> 02:24:35,730
Your complexion"s just as fair as Ruby's.

2298
02:24:36,022 --> 02:24:39,734
And anyway, your hair's darker now than just plain old red.

2299
02:24:40,235 --> 02:24:41,403
Really?

2300
02:24:41,778 --> 02:24:43,488
I'd say it's definitely auburn,

2301
02:24:43,780 --> 02:24:46,324
and that's sort of close to blonde.

2302
02:24:46,783 --> 02:24:49,578
- Well, all right.
- It isn't authentic.

2303
02:25:05,218 --> 02:25:08,388
You can lay the piano cover over me.

2304
02:25:15,145 --> 02:25:18,440
Gosh, she really looks dead.

2305
02:25:18,732 --> 02:25:19,732
I'm frightened.

2306
02:25:19,774 --> 02:25:21,484
Mis. Lynde says acting is a sin.

2307
02:25:21,776 --> 02:25:24,446
- Ruby, keep quiet.
- You're spoiling the effect.

2308
02:25:24,738 --> 02:25:26,048
Besides, this is hundreds of years

2309
02:25:26,072 --> 02:25:28,658
before Mrs. Lynde was born.

2310
02:25:29,284 --> 02:25:30,524
Diana, you arrange all of this.

2311
02:25:30,785 --> 02:25:32,263
It's ridiculous for Elaine to be talking

2312
02:25:32,287 --> 02:25:33,872
when she's supposed to be dead.

2313
02:25:34,164 --> 02:25:36,583
All right, Jane, the flowers.

2314
02:25:43,798 --> 02:25:45,550
Now she's ready.

2315
02:25:45,842 --> 02:25:47,844
Anne, for gosh sakes, smile a little.

2316
02:25:48,136 --> 02:25:52,515
It says here, Elaine lay as though she smiled.

2317
02:25:52,974 --> 02:25:53,974
That's better.

2318
02:25:55,143 --> 02:25:59,939
All right, ladies, let us send her to her watery grave.

2319
02:26:01,191 --> 02:26:03,109
(Kiss)

2320
02:26:04,486 --> 02:26:06,112
(Kiss)

2321
02:26:07,155 --> 02:26:08,823
(Kiss)

2322
02:26:17,832 --> 02:26:20,669
"There she weaves by night and day

2323
02:26:21,336 --> 02:26:25,048
a magic web, with colors gay

2324
02:26:25,340 --> 02:26:27,592
she has heard a whisper Say

2325
02:26:27,884 --> 02:26:30,053
'a curse is on her if she stay'

2326
02:26:30,345 --> 02:26:34,140
to look down on Camelot.

2327
02:26:34,432 --> 02:26:36,142
And at the closing of the day,

2328
02:26:36,434 --> 02:26:39,145
the broad stream bore her far away.

2329
02:26:39,437 --> 02:26:42,065
And as the boat head wound along,

2330
02:26:42,357 --> 02:26:45,068
they heard her singing a last song,

2331
02:26:45,360 --> 02:26:48,154
"the lady of Shallot."

2332
02:26:49,364 --> 02:26:51,157
(Water sloshes, gasps)

2333
02:26:55,245 --> 02:26:57,497
(Splashing)

2334
02:27:11,761 --> 02:27:13,596
She looked so good with the hair!

2335
02:27:13,888 --> 02:27:15,432
(Laughing)

2336
02:27:24,274 --> 02:27:25,900
Anne! She's drowning!

2337
02:27:26,192 --> 02:27:27,819
Anne's drowning.

2338
02:27:28,111 --> 02:27:29,391
We have to go and get some help!

2339
02:27:29,487 --> 02:27:31,197
(Panicked screaming)

2340
02:27:48,298 --> 02:27:50,216
(Sighs)

2341
02:28:04,314 --> 02:28:08,151
Anne Shirley.

2342
02:28:08,443 --> 02:28:11,154
What in heck are you doing?

2343
02:28:11,446 --> 02:28:13,990
Fishing for lake trout.

2344
02:28:14,282 --> 02:28:16,075
For lake trout? Ah.

2345
02:28:19,329 --> 02:28:22,248
(Grunting with effort)

2346
02:28:32,342 --> 02:28:34,260
Ahem.

2347
02:28:35,386 --> 02:28:37,180
Nobody's home.

2348
02:28:37,472 --> 02:28:38,515
It's too late.

2349
02:28:38,848 --> 02:28:40,558
She's drowned and we're murderers.

2350
02:28:41,142 --> 02:28:43,853
Matthew, come on.

2351
02:28:44,479 --> 02:28:45,897
(Sobbing)

2352
02:28:49,359 --> 02:28:52,195
Well, if you must know, | was in Diana's skiff.

2353
02:28:52,487 --> 02:28:56,157
But it sprang a leak, and | had to climb on to the pile or sink.

2354
02:28:56,449 --> 02:28:59,160
Now if you'll be so kind as to row me to the landing.

2355
02:28:59,452 --> 02:29:01,162
Ah, | see.

2356
02:29:01,496 --> 02:29:04,999
Well, then the fact is, I've rescued you.

2357
02:29:06,084 --> 02:29:09,379
Help is on the way, and | was calmly waiting for it.

2358
02:29:21,391 --> 02:29:23,184
You're most welcome.

2359
02:29:23,476 --> 02:29:25,270
I'm grateful for your assistance, Mr. Blythe,

2360
02:29:25,603 --> 02:29:27,230
even though it was not required.

2361
02:29:27,522 --> 02:29:29,524
Now if you'll excuse me, | have to find my friends.

2362
02:29:29,816 --> 02:29:32,235
They are likely overcome with fear for my life.

2363
02:29:32,527 --> 02:29:35,071
Anne, wait, wait a minute.

2364
02:29:35,363 --> 02:29:36,507
| was just down at the post office

2365
02:29:36,531 --> 02:29:39,242
to see if the Queens results had been printed.

2366
02:29:39,534 --> 02:29:41,995
Congratulations on coming first, Gilbert.

2367
02:29:42,287 --> 02:29:44,163
I'm sure you're very proud of you achievements.

2368
02:29:44,539 --> 02:29:46,249
Wait a minute, you ninny.

2369
02:29:46,541 --> 02:29:51,254
We tied for first place, you and |.

2370
02:29:51,546 --> 02:29:53,256
| figured you'd have it for sure.

2371
02:29:53,548 --> 02:29:56,259
We all passed. Our entire class.

2372
02:29:56,551 --> 02:29:59,387
First out of all 200?

2373
02:29:59,679 --> 02:30:01,973
I'm just sorry you had to share with me.

2374
02:30:02,557 --> 02:30:05,143
| never expected to beat you.

2375
02:30:08,438 --> 02:30:11,107
Can't we be friends?

2376
02:30:11,399 --> 02:30:14,736
This childishness has gone on long enough, don't you think?

2377
02:30:15,028 --> 02:30:16,672
The fact that you rescued me unnecessarily

2378
02:30:16,696 --> 02:30:20,450
hardly wipes out past wrongs.

2379
02:30:21,242 --> 02:30:24,454
Look, I'm sorry | ever said anything about your hair.

2380
02:30:24,913 --> 02:30:26,915
You have no idea how sorry.

2381
02:30:27,206 --> 02:30:28,833
But it was so long ago.

2382
02:30:29,125 --> 02:30:30,919
Aren't you ever going to forgive me?

2383
02:30:31,336 --> 02:30:34,047
You hurt my feelings excruciatingly.

2384
02:30:34,422 --> 02:30:38,134
| only said it because I...

2385
02:30:38,426 --> 02:30:41,220
Because | wanted to meet you SO much.

2386
02:30:42,597 --> 02:30:44,700
Why did you turn your back on me at the Christmas Ball?

2387
02:30:44,724 --> 02:30:46,434
Anne, that was over a year ago.

2388
02:30:47,185 --> 02:30:48,895
It was a deliberate humiliation.

2389
02:30:49,187 --> 02:30:51,547
And | knew exactly what you were thinking too, Anne Shirley,

2390
02:30:51,814 --> 02:30:53,691
you and Diana Barry.

2391
02:30:54,192 --> 02:30:57,070
Look, can we be friends now?

2392
02:30:57,362 --> 02:31:01,574
Why don't you figure it out, if you're so clever?

2393
02:31:02,116 --> 02:31:04,535
Anne, wait a minute.

2394
02:31:04,827 --> 02:31:06,788
Everyone will think I've drowned.

2395
02:31:18,508 --> 02:31:19,968
(Sobbing)

2396
02:31:22,512 --> 02:31:25,264
We thought, we thought you were dead.

2397
02:31:25,556 --> 02:31:26,617
- It was all our fault.
- No.

2398
02:31:26,641 --> 02:31:28,351
And Ruby's having hysterics.

2399
02:31:28,643 --> 02:31:31,354
Oh, Anne, how did you ever escape?

2400
02:31:31,646 --> 02:31:33,189
| climbed underneath the bridge.

2401
02:31:33,481 --> 02:31:35,459
And Gilbert Blythe came along and rowed me to shore.

2402
02:31:35,483 --> 02:31:37,068
Oh, how romantic.

2403
02:31:37,360 --> 02:31:39,040
Of course you'll speak to him from now on.

2404
02:31:39,278 --> 02:31:40,655
Of course | will not.

2405
02:31:40,947 --> 02:31:43,300
| don't ever want to hear the word "romance again, Jane Andrews."

2406
02:31:43,324 --> 02:31:44,867
lt was easy enough in Camelot,

2407
02:31:45,159 --> 02:31:47,870
but it certainly isn't appreciated in Avonlea.

2408
02:31:48,663 --> 02:31:51,040
Oh, I'm sorry | frightened everyone So.

2409
02:31:51,332 --> 02:31:53,251
It was all my fault.

2410
02:31:53,543 --> 02:31:55,521
Well, I'm sure that John Barry will be pleased to hear

2411
02:31:55,545 --> 02:31:57,265
that he no longer owns a dory, Anne Shirley.

2412
02:31:57,547 --> 02:32:01,259
- You do beat all, girl.
- When are you going to have any sense?

2413
02:32:01,676 --> 02:32:03,386
| think my prospects are brightening.

2414
02:32:03,678 --> 02:32:05,388
| just saw the pass list for Queens

2415
02:32:05,680 --> 02:32:10,143
for better or for worse, | tied with Gilbert Blythe for first!

2416
02:32:10,435 --> 02:32:13,187
First! Diana: Oh, Anne, you must be sO proud.

2417
02:32:13,479 --> 02:32:15,648
| must say, you've done pretty well for yourself, Anne.

2418
02:32:15,940 --> 02:32:18,401
Well, | guess she has done well, Marilla.

2419
02:32:18,693 --> 02:32:21,404
Far be it from me to be backward when praise is due.

2420
02:32:21,696 --> 02:32:24,490
You're a credit to us all, and we're all proud of you.

2421
02:32:39,589 --> 02:32:42,508
(Hooves clop, cart rattles)

2422
02:32:44,510 --> 02:32:46,220
Whoa!

2423
02:32:46,554 --> 02:32:48,264
Good afternoon, Miss Cuthbert.

2424
02:32:48,556 --> 02:32:50,892
Good afternoon.

2425
02:32:51,184 --> 02:32:53,144
I'm Gilbert Blythe.

2426
02:32:53,436 --> 02:32:57,356
Yes. You've grown into quite a young man.

2427
02:33:00,068 --> 02:33:01,444
So you must be very proud of Anne.

2428
02:33:01,736 --> 02:33:03,776
It's a real challenge keeping up with her at school.

2429
02:33:03,863 --> 02:33:07,450
Yes, Matthew and | are both proud.

2430
02:33:07,742 --> 02:33:11,954
She has the talent to make something of herself.

2431
02:33:12,246 --> 02:33:16,876
But she's still very young, Gilbert.

2432
02:33:20,004 --> 02:33:21,839
Good afternoon.

2433
02:33:22,131 --> 02:33:24,550
Afternoon, ma'am.

2434
02:33:32,642 --> 02:33:35,061
So what are you going to recite, Anne?

2435
02:33:35,353 --> 02:33:36,896
I've decided to give "The highwayman".

2436
02:33:37,188 --> 02:33:38,481
It's very pathetic.

2437
02:33:38,773 --> 02:33:40,493
Laura Spencer is giving a comic recitation.

2438
02:33:40,775 --> 02:33:42,485
But | prefer to make people cry.

2439
02:33:42,777 --> 02:33:44,487
Are you nervous?

2440
02:33:44,779 --> 02:33:47,782
A little bit, even though I've stood out in public so often.

2441
02:33:48,074 --> 02:33:50,409
But | feel very well prepared, and that helps.

2442
02:33:50,701 --> 02:33:52,370
Well, this organdy will look elegant

2443
02:33:52,662 --> 02:33:55,373
under all the electric lights at the white sands hotel.

2444
02:33:55,706 --> 02:33:58,417
Oh, mother tried to convince me to audition for a violin solo.

2445
02:33:58,793 --> 02:34:00,503
Can you imagine?

2446
02:34:00,795 --> 02:34:03,381
| mean, | haven't picked up a bow in three years.

2447
02:34:03,673 --> 02:34:05,753
The Charlottetown hospital is a worthy cause and all,

2448
02:34:05,800 --> 02:34:09,512
but | am not prepared to suffer humiliation for it.

2449
02:34:09,804 --> 02:34:11,097
We're all amateurs.

2450
02:34:11,389 --> 02:34:13,325
They may be very sorry they've asked any of us to do it

2451
02:34:13,349 --> 02:34:14,349
when it's all over.

2452
02:34:14,809 --> 02:34:16,477
Hardly the case with you, Anne.

2453
02:34:16,769 --> 02:34:18,521
After standing first at Queens,

2454
02:34:18,813 --> 02:34:20,606
you can do no wrong In this community.

2455
02:34:20,898 --> 02:34:23,526
Someone ought to tell that to Marilla.

2456
02:34:23,818 --> 02:34:26,320
Well, Mr. Cuthbert is sure proud.

2457
02:34:26,612 --> 02:34:28,531
He plunks down his money awful quick these days

2458
02:34:28,823 --> 02:34:31,534
for anything | tell him is pretty and fashionable.

2459
02:34:31,826 --> 02:34:34,036
Alice Lawson, you devil.

2460
02:34:34,328 --> 02:34:36,539
Don't you go abusing Matthew's generosity to me.

2461
02:34:36,831 --> 02:34:38,541
Ooh, you do have good taste, though.

2462
02:34:38,833 --> 02:34:40,626
I'll see you at the concert.

2463
02:34:52,430 --> 02:34:54,348
Hello, Anne.

2464
02:34:55,975 --> 02:34:57,810
Whoa.

2465
02:34:58,102 --> 02:35:00,605
Can | offer you a ride home?

2466
02:35:00,897 --> 02:35:04,775
You promised we were going to be friends, remember?

2467
02:35:05,067 --> 02:35:06,110
Well, all right, then.

2468
02:35:06,402 --> 02:35:09,906
It is rather awkward with all these.

2469
02:35:12,283 --> 02:35:14,118
It was good of you to stop.

2470
02:35:14,410 --> 02:35:15,953
Seems I'm developing quite a habit

2471
02:35:16,245 --> 02:35:19,207
of getting you out of awkward Situations.

2472
02:35:20,291 --> 02:35:23,127
| thought a lot about what happened at the bridge, Gilbert.

2473
02:35:23,836 --> 02:35:26,130
What I mean to say Is...

2474
02:35:26,422 --> 02:35:29,133
It was very rude of me to just run off like that.

2475
02:35:29,425 --> 02:35:33,137
But | was very overwrought over learning my score, Our score.

2476
02:35:33,429 --> 02:35:35,139
| mean, | wasn't myself.

2477
02:35:35,890 --> 02:35:37,475
That's all right.

2478
02:35:37,767 --> 02:35:40,061
Life's too short to hold grudges anyway.

2479
02:35:40,478 --> 02:35:42,605
It's valiant of you to Say so.

2480
02:35:42,897 --> 02:35:44,607
You'll go far with that kind of attitude.

2481
02:35:44,899 --> 02:35:47,401
Do you know what you're going to study at Queens yet, Anne?

2482
02:35:47,693 --> 02:35:50,613
| intend on taking my teacher's license In one year instead of two.

2483
02:35:50,905 --> 02:35:53,491
At Miss Stacey's suggestion.

2484
02:35:53,783 --> 02:35:55,993
Gee, | always imagined you'd have a career on the stage.

2485
02:35:56,285 --> 02:35:58,496
| think you'd make a swell actress.

2486
02:35:58,871 --> 02:36:01,582
Especially as the lily maid.

2487
02:36:01,916 --> 02:36:03,185
| hear you're giving "The highwayman"

2488
02:36:03,209 --> 02:36:04,627
at the white sands recital.

2489
02:36:04,919 --> 02:36:06,629
My life is an open book, | see.

2490
02:36:06,921 --> 02:36:08,631
Who told you that?

2491
02:36:08,923 --> 02:36:11,133
Well, | have a little confession to make.

2492
02:36:11,467 --> 02:36:13,067
Well, | was just at the Lawson's myself,

2493
02:36:13,135 --> 02:36:14,845
and Alice told me you were walking home.

2494
02:36:15,137 --> 02:36:16,514
Oh.

2495
02:36:16,806 --> 02:36:18,200
I'm gonna try and get you an encore while you're up there.

2496
02:36:18,224 --> 02:36:19,993
So make sure you have a second selection ready.

2497
02:36:20,017 --> 02:36:23,521
Nobody's going to encore me.

2498
02:36:23,854 --> 02:36:24,854
Well, | would,

2499
02:36:24,939 --> 02:36:29,652
Especially if | had the honor of escorting you to the concert.

2500
02:36:29,944 --> 02:36:31,654
Um, | don't know.

2501
02:36:31,946 --> 02:36:33,786
| promised the Barry's I'd go with them, but...

2502
02:36:33,948 --> 02:36:37,326
| think you're old enough to make up your own mind, Anne.

2503
02:36:37,618 --> 02:36:39,662
I've always been old enough to make up my own mind.

2504
02:36:39,954 --> 02:36:41,580
Very well, then, Gilbert.

2505
02:36:41,872 --> 02:36:43,666
I'd be pleased to accept your invitation.

2506
02:36:45,835 --> 02:36:48,337
C-could you let me off at the corner, please?

2507
02:36:48,629 --> 02:36:50,909
I'm going to take a shortcut and show Diana what | bought.

2508
02:37:13,279 --> 02:37:15,865
(Clock ticks)

2509
02:37:22,872 --> 02:37:25,791
Well, Matthew, say something.

2510
02:37:27,710 --> 02:37:29,545
She was holding his hand.

2511
02:37:29,837 --> 02:37:31,547
She'd have to hold his hand

2512
02:37:31,839 --> 02:37:33,758
if he was helping her out of the buggy.

2513
02:37:34,050 --> 02:37:36,552
What was she doing in that buggy?

2514
02:37:36,844 --> 02:37:40,306
Nothing worth all this fussing.

2515
02:37:40,598 --> 02:37:41,718
She's just a child, Matthew.

2516
02:37:41,849 --> 02:37:44,352
She doesn't know what she's doing.

2517
02:37:44,810 --> 02:37:46,729
- Hello, everyone.
- Sorry | was late.

2518
02:37:47,021 --> 02:37:48,314
| stopped over at Diana's,

2519
02:37:48,606 --> 02:37:51,317
and she just loves the material you chose, Matthew.

2520
02:37:51,734 --> 02:37:55,446
Anne Shirley, I've just been informed by a reliable source

2521
02:37:55,780 --> 02:37:58,491
that you were seen at Avonlea crossroads.

2522
02:37:58,783 --> 02:38:00,493
In a buggy with a young man.

2523
02:38:00,785 --> 02:38:03,162
Marilla.

2524
02:38:03,454 --> 02:38:04,694
He only offered me a ride home.

2525
02:38:04,789 --> 02:38:07,124
He was just being friendly.

2526
02:38:07,458 --> 02:38:10,169
Not according to Rachel Lynde.

2527
02:38:10,461 --> 02:38:13,798
- Rachel Lynde?
- Yes, Rachel Lynde saw you.

2528
02:38:14,924 --> 02:38:17,843
Marilla, she ain't done nothing wrong.

2529
02:38:19,553 --> 02:38:22,765
Matthew, remember, In the beginning,

2530
02:38:23,224 --> 02:38:26,727
| told you not to put your Oar in.

2531
02:38:33,943 --> 02:38:35,861
(Knocking)

2532
02:38:39,949 --> 02:38:41,117
(Door creaks open)

2533
02:38:46,455 --> 02:38:51,710
I'm sorry | lost my temper, Anne.

2534
02:39:00,594 --> 02:39:02,096
Marilla, please.

2535
02:39:02,388 --> 02:39:05,015
| never meant anything to come of all this.

2536
02:39:05,307 --> 02:39:08,185
And nothing has as yet.

2537
02:39:12,606 --> 02:39:18,028
Anne, you've changed so much, grown so tall and,

2538
02:39:19,989 --> 02:39:22,908
so stylish.

2539
02:39:26,662 --> 02:39:29,498
You don't belong in Avonlea anymore.

2540
02:39:29,790 --> 02:39:33,669
| get lonely just thinking about it.

2541
02:39:35,004 --> 02:39:37,381
You'll be going off into the world to

2542
02:39:37,673 --> 02:39:40,384
make your way and...

2543
02:39:40,676 --> 02:39:42,428
You don't want to make any ties here

2544
02:39:42,720 --> 02:39:45,931
that you might come to regret later.

2545
02:39:50,519 --> 02:39:55,024
It doesn't matter where | go... or how | change.

2546
02:39:55,316 --> 02:40:00,529
I'll always be your Anne... Anne of Green Gables.

2547
02:40:07,620 --> 02:40:10,539
| want you to give this to him.

2548
02:40:14,043 --> 02:40:15,878
(Reading) "Dear Gilbert",

2549
02:40:16,170 --> 02:40:19,965
| regret that I'll be unable to attend the white sands concert with you.

2550
02:40:20,841 --> 02:40:23,552
Sincerely, Anne Shirley.'.

2551
02:40:24,178 --> 02:40:27,556
Why won't you go with him?

2552
02:40:27,848 --> 02:40:29,642
Plenty of reasons why | won't go.

2553
02:40:30,059 --> 02:40:33,521
| only accepted in the first place because he dared me.

2554
02:40:33,812 --> 02:40:36,398
Well, | think you owe him an explanation.

2555
02:40:39,068 --> 02:40:42,363
Marilla: | hope you don't ruin your dress driving there in the dust.

2556
02:40:42,655 --> 02:40:44,907
It's far too thin for this damp weather.

2557
02:40:45,199 --> 02:40:47,993
I'm sorry | ever agreed to it.

2558
02:41:08,514 --> 02:41:11,016
Thank you, Matthew.

2559
02:41:17,648 --> 02:41:19,751
Well, there's no use in saying anything to you, Matthew,

2560
02:41:19,775 --> 02:41:23,487
but those pearls look absolutely ridiculous.

2561
02:41:23,988 --> 02:41:26,198
| don't know where you get these silly ideas.

2562
02:41:26,490 --> 02:41:29,785
Matthew is proud of the way | look.

2563
02:41:32,913 --> 02:41:34,081
Anne!

2564
02:41:34,373 --> 02:41:37,084
They're here.

2565
02:41:37,418 --> 02:41:40,004
Thank you, Marilla and Matthew.

2566
02:41:40,296 --> 02:41:42,923
| mustn't keep them waiting.

2567
02:41:44,675 --> 02:41:46,555
Now mind you keep your dress clear of the wheel.

2568
02:41:46,677 --> 02:41:47,761
Good luck, Anne!

2569
02:41:48,053 --> 02:41:49,253
I'll be watching for you both.

2570
02:41:54,602 --> 02:41:57,438
(Music)

2571
02:42:02,276 --> 02:42:04,695
Gilbert gave me this in return for your note.

2572
02:42:04,987 --> 02:42:06,697
He's coming to watch you anyway.

2573
02:42:06,989 --> 02:42:09,783
| didn't want Jane or Gabby's brother to see.

2574
02:42:13,162 --> 02:42:15,706
(Reading) "...You have a right to your own opinion.

2575
02:42:15,998 --> 02:42:18,334
It would have been easier if you had told me in person.

2576
02:42:18,792 --> 02:42:21,003
| hope you can still consider me a friend.

2577
02:42:21,295 --> 02:42:23,839
Sincerely, Gilbert Blythe."

2578
02:42:24,173 --> 02:42:26,884
| won't be accused of being a coward, Diana.

2579
02:42:27,301 --> 02:42:29,011
He doesn't understand.

2580
02:42:29,303 --> 02:42:30,343
Tell him I'll soeak to him

2581
02:42:30,471 --> 02:42:32,071
in the first minute | can steal tonight.

2582
02:42:32,306 --> 02:42:34,016
Calm down, Anne.

2583
02:42:34,308 --> 02:42:38,103
I'm so ashamed, | can't go up on that stage. | can't.

2584
02:42:39,813 --> 02:42:42,024
They'll be merciless if | fail.

2585
02:42:42,316 --> 02:42:45,861
You've never failed at anything, Anne Shirley.

2586
02:42:46,153 --> 02:42:47,488
Go on.

2587
02:42:47,780 --> 02:42:50,157
(Applause)

2588
02:42:53,202 --> 02:42:56,121
"Ho, ho, the breakers roared

2589
02:42:57,206 --> 02:43:00,042
at daybreak, on the bleak sea beach,

2590
02:43:00,334 --> 02:43:05,923
a fisherman stood aghast to see the form of a maiden fair,

2591
02:43:06,215 --> 02:43:09,051
lashed close to a drifting mast.

2592
02:43:09,343 --> 02:43:13,055
The salt sea was frozen on her breast,

2593
02:43:13,347 --> 02:43:15,140
the salt tears in her eyes,

2594
02:43:15,432 --> 02:43:18,060
and he saw her hair,

2595
02:43:18,352 --> 02:43:21,063
like the brown seaweed,

2596
02:43:21,355 --> 02:43:24,149
on the billows fall and the rise

2597
02:43:26,235 --> 02:43:29,071
such was the wreck of the hesperus

2598
02:43:29,363 --> 02:43:32,908
in the midnight and the snow...

2599
02:43:33,200 --> 02:43:36,412
God save us all from a death like this,

2600
02:43:36,704 --> 02:43:40,082
"on the reef of Norman's woe."

2601
02:43:40,374 --> 02:43:42,167
(Applause)

2602
02:43:53,846 --> 02:43:57,433
Mrs. Evans has just completed the European tour.

2603
02:43:57,725 --> 02:43:59,268
Oh, she's a prodigious talent.

2604
02:43:59,560 --> 02:44:01,687
| was moved beyond words.

2605
02:44:01,979 --> 02:44:04,690
On behalf of Charlottetown hospital,

2606
02:44:04,982 --> 02:44:09,695
| would like to offer our indebtedness to Mrs. Amelia Evans

2607
02:44:09,987 --> 02:44:13,699
for gracing us with such a Stirring performance.

2608
02:44:13,991 --> 02:44:16,702
In support of today's benefit.

2609
02:44:16,994 --> 02:44:19,872
Oh, thank you.

2610
02:44:20,164 --> 02:44:23,125
(Applause)

2611
02:44:28,839 --> 02:44:32,050
And now, ladies and gentlemen,

2612
02:44:32,342 --> 02:44:37,139
| would like to present one of Avonlea's most celebrated students,

2613
02:44:37,431 --> 02:44:40,142
who achieved the highest standing

2614
02:44:40,434 --> 02:44:45,230
in the recent entrance examinations to Queens Academy.

2615
02:44:46,315 --> 02:44:50,152
Miss Anne Shirley.

2616
02:44:50,444 --> 02:44:53,447
(Applause)

2617
02:45:03,332 --> 02:45:06,710
It will be amusing to see what arises

2618
02:45:07,002 --> 02:45:09,630
from the local amateurs after this.

2619
02:45:36,657 --> 02:45:38,867
"The wind was a torrent of darkness

2620
02:45:39,159 --> 02:45:41,203
among the gusty trees

2621
02:45:41,495 --> 02:45:43,205
the moon was a ghostly galleon

2622
02:45:43,497 --> 02:45:45,874
tossed upon cloudy seas,

2623
02:45:46,166 --> 02:45:48,210
the road was a ribbon of moonlight

2624
02:45:48,502 --> 02:45:49,502
over the purple moor,

2625
02:45:49,670 --> 02:45:53,215
and the highwayman came riding, riding, riding

2626
02:45:53,507 --> 02:45:57,803
the highwayman came riding up to the old inn door

2627
02:45:58,095 --> 02:46:00,222
one kiss, my Bonnie sweetheart,

2628
02:46:00,514 --> 02:46:02,307
I'm after a prize tonight,

2629
02:46:02,599 --> 02:46:04,309
but | shall return with the yellow gold

2630
02:46:04,601 --> 02:46:06,228
before the morning light,

2631
02:46:06,520 --> 02:46:10,232
yet if they press me sharply and harry me through the day,

2632
02:46:10,524 --> 02:46:15,237
look for me by moonlight, watch for me by moonlight,

2633
02:46:15,529 --> 02:46:18,073
I'll come to thee by moonlight,

2634
02:46:18,365 --> 02:46:21,243
though hell should bar the way

2635
02:46:21,535 --> 02:46:22,744
ack he spurred like amadman,

2636
02:46:23,036 --> 02:46:24,413
shouting a curse to the sky

2637
02:46:24,705 --> 02:46:26,248
with the white road smoking behind him

2638
02:46:26,540 --> 02:46:29,251
and his rapier brandished high,

2639
02:46:29,543 --> 02:46:32,254
blood-red were his spurs in the golden noon

2640
02:46:32,546 --> 02:46:34,631
wine red was his velvet coat,

2641
02:46:34,923 --> 02:46:37,259
when they shot him down on the highway

2642
02:46:37,551 --> 02:46:40,262
down like a dog on the highway,

2643
02:46:40,554 --> 02:46:44,266
and he lay in his blood on the highway

2644
02:46:44,558 --> 02:46:47,352
with the bunch of lace at his throat.'

2645
02:46:49,771 --> 02:46:51,690
(Applause)

2646
02:46:57,446 --> 02:47:00,365
- Encore!
- Encore!

2647
02:47:01,450 --> 02:47:04,286
- My dear, you were splendid.
- Go back, go back.

2648
02:47:04,578 --> 02:47:06,788
They're encoring you.

2649
02:47:07,080 --> 02:47:10,375
- | can't go back.
- You can.

2650
02:47:12,461 --> 02:47:14,379
(Applause)

2651
02:47:26,475 --> 02:47:29,186
(Low hum of chatter)

2652
02:47:32,481 --> 02:47:35,400
Ladies, can you excuse me?

2653
02:47:44,076 --> 02:47:46,995
Could you excuse me?

2654
02:47:54,044 --> 02:47:56,880
Anne, | have to admit, | was so proud.

2655
02:47:57,172 --> 02:48:00,592
Your recitation was as magnificent as Mrs. Evans.

2656
02:48:01,343 --> 02:48:03,345
What's all this bother about Anne Shirley?

2657
02:48:03,637 --> 02:48:06,264
You sure couldn't get up there and give a recital like her.

2658
02:48:06,556 --> 02:48:09,356
. asking who that gir! On the platform was with the lovely titian hair.

2659
02:48:09,643 --> 02:48:12,771
Whatever that means.

2660
02:48:13,063 --> 02:48:15,565
Plain red. It's just a fancy way of saying it.

2661
02:48:16,024 --> 02:48:18,026
Well, he thought you were wonderful anyway.

2662
02:48:18,360 --> 02:48:21,863
You'll forgive me for stealing her away again, won't you?

2663
02:48:22,197 --> 02:48:26,994
There are so many people waiting to meet our young, Miss Shirley.

2664
02:48:28,870 --> 02:48:30,630
If you'll excuse me, | don't mean to be rude,

2665
02:48:30,664 --> 02:48:33,125
but there is someone | absolutely must speak with.

2666
02:48:33,500 --> 02:48:34,477
I'll return right away, | promise.

2667
02:48:34,501 --> 02:48:36,086
Very well, dear, but hurry up.

2668
02:48:36,378 --> 02:48:39,297
| have important people waiting.

2669
02:48:48,557 --> 02:48:52,436
Gilbert! Gilbert!

2670
02:49:00,569 --> 02:49:03,405
I'm sorry | didn't get a chance to talk to him, Anne.

2671
02:49:03,697 --> 02:49:06,408
Blame me if you want.

2672
02:49:06,700 --> 02:49:09,411
It's all right, Diana.

2673
02:49:09,703 --> 02:49:12,497
Some things just aren't meant to be.

2674
02:49:14,750 --> 02:49:17,586
Did you see all those diamonds?

2675
02:49:17,878 --> 02:49:21,423
| wish | were rich, and | could spend my whole summer at a hotel,

2676
02:49:21,715 --> 02:49:24,968
eating ice cream and chicken salad.

2677
02:49:25,260 --> 02:49:27,429
You know something, Diana.

2678
02:49:27,721 --> 02:49:29,431
We are rich.

2679
02:49:29,723 --> 02:49:31,433
We have 16 years to our credit.

2680
02:49:31,725 --> 02:49:34,436
And we both have wonderful imaginations.

2681
02:49:34,728 --> 02:49:37,522
We should be as happy as queens.

2682
02:49:39,149 --> 02:49:40,817
Look at that.

2683
02:49:41,109 --> 02:49:42,754
You couldn't enjoy its loveliness any more

2684
02:49:42,778 --> 02:49:45,197
if you had ropes or diamonds.

2685
02:49:45,489 --> 02:49:48,200
| don't know about that.

2686
02:49:48,742 --> 02:49:50,786
I'm content with my string of pearls.

2687
02:49:51,078 --> 02:49:52,718
Matthew gave them to me with as much love

2688
02:49:52,746 --> 02:49:57,459
as ever went into any of those stuffy women's jewels.

2689
02:49:57,751 --> 02:50:02,422
Lam happy, Diana, and nothing is going to hold me back.

2690
02:50:05,634 --> 02:50:08,553
(Train whistle blowing)

2691
02:50:14,059 --> 02:50:15,059
Hurry up, Anne.

2692
02:50:15,185 --> 02:50:18,647
Do you think the train IS going to wait for you?

2693
02:50:21,650 --> 02:50:24,402
No, I'll take it, Matthew.

2694
02:50:24,694 --> 02:50:26,404
It will be easier go quickly by myself.

2695
02:50:26,780 --> 02:50:28,949
Getting emotional over nothing.

2696
02:50:29,241 --> 02:50:33,578
Nothing? You both mean everything to me.

2697
02:50:40,210 --> 02:50:42,504
All this foolishness.

2698
02:50:42,796 --> 02:50:46,174
You might as well kiss him too.

2699
02:50:46,508 --> 02:50:48,301
(Kiss)

2700
02:50:52,681 --> 02:50:56,017
- (Man Shouting)
- All aboard! All aboard!

2701
02:51:06,236 --> 02:51:08,155
(Train whistle blowing)

2702
02:51:11,700 --> 02:51:13,535
I'm afraid for her, Matthew.

2703
02:51:13,827 --> 02:51:15,620
She'll be gone so long.

2704
02:51:17,247 --> 02:51:19,416
She'll get terrible lonesome.

2705
02:51:19,708 --> 02:51:22,085
You mean we'll get terrible lonesome.

2706
02:51:22,377 --> 02:51:25,922
| can't help wishing that she'd stayed a little girl.

2707
02:51:26,298 --> 02:51:29,009
Miss Spencer made a lucky mistake, | guess.

2708
02:51:29,718 --> 02:51:32,429
- Lt wasn't luck.
- It was providence.

2709
02:51:32,721 --> 02:51:35,515
He knew we needed her.

2710
02:51:37,726 --> 02:51:41,646
Even with her queer little ways.

2711
02:51:41,938 --> 02:51:44,441
| loved her for them.

2712
02:51:54,284 --> 02:51:55,785
| like people who make me like them.

2713
02:51:56,077 --> 02:52:00,582
It saves Me so much trouble forcing myself to like them.

2714
02:52:00,874 --> 02:52:02,185
Oh, but I'd be a much happier woman

2715
02:52:02,209 --> 02:52:04,294
if you'd stay at Beechwood with me.

2716
02:52:04,669 --> 02:52:07,214
There's no other place I'd rather be, Miss Barry.

2717
02:52:07,505 --> 02:52:10,217
- | know it's impractical.
- You need to be near the school.

2718
02:52:10,508 --> 02:52:12,177
The lady who runs this boarding house

2719
02:52:12,469 --> 02:52:15,305
is agentlewoman of reduced circumstances.

2720
02:52:16,556 --> 02:52:17,891
You'll be quite safe.

2721
02:52:18,183 --> 02:52:20,560
Here we are, Peter.

2722
02:52:20,852 --> 02:52:22,892
Miss Barry said you have of a creative turn of mind,

2723
02:52:23,104 --> 02:52:26,816
so I've given you my best room, looking out over the street.

2724
02:52:27,108 --> 02:52:29,819
- Thank you, I'm sure.
- Cheer up now.

2725
02:52:30,111 --> 02:52:32,906
I've had dozens of students here and not lost one of them yet.

2726
02:52:36,993 --> 02:52:38,833
If there's anything you want, just let me know.

2727
02:52:38,954 --> 02:52:41,081
Thank you.

2728
02:52:52,801 --> 02:52:55,637
| can't cheer up. | don't want to cheer up.

2729
02:52:55,929 --> 02:52:58,139
I'd rather just be miserable.

2730
02:53:04,229 --> 02:53:06,439
Those of you that have elected to complete the program

2731
02:53:06,731 --> 02:53:08,691
for a teacher's license In one year instead of two

2732
02:53:08,942 --> 02:53:10,652
have a difficult struggle ahead of you.

2733
02:53:10,944 --> 02:53:14,656
But you're here because we know you're capable of doing it

2734
02:53:14,948 --> 02:53:16,926
these first two weeks will be a probationary period

2735
02:53:16,950 --> 02:53:18,790
in which you can decide whether you really want

2736
02:53:18,952 --> 02:53:21,663
to complete the program In a single year.

2737
02:53:21,955 --> 02:53:23,395
In that sense, these first two weeks

2738
02:53:23,623 --> 02:53:25,667
will be the most important you spend at Queens.

2739
02:53:25,959 --> 02:53:28,211
Bear that in mind.

2740
02:53:28,837 --> 02:53:30,630
(Bells toll)

2741
02:53:33,842 --> 02:53:37,679
- Anne Shirley!
- You look positively ill.

2742
02:53:38,847 --> 02:53:39,949
Whenever your nose and eyes get red,

2743
02:53:39,973 --> 02:53:42,684
you just seem red all over.

2744
02:53:42,976 --> 02:53:46,688
Tell me, how are the first year students doing?

2745
02:53:46,980 --> 02:53:48,690
Our french professor's a dream.

2746
02:53:48,982 --> 02:53:50,567
He has the cutest moustache.

2747
02:53:50,859 --> 02:53:51,836
Come for lunch, and I'll tell all.

2748
02:53:51,860 --> 02:53:54,571
I'm meeting Jane and Ruby and some of the others.

2749
02:53:54,863 --> 02:53:56,743
Thank you, but I've other business to attend to.

2750
02:53:56,990 --> 02:53:59,826
Gilbert won't be there, if that's what's worrying you.

2751
02:54:00,118 --> 02:54:02,203
What ever do you mean?

2752
02:54:02,495 --> 02:54:04,205
Gilbert Blythe is a rake.

2753
02:54:04,497 --> 02:54:06,851
And after his insulting behavior at the White Sands concert,

2754
02:54:06,875 --> 02:54:08,585
| have decided to completely ignore him.

2755
02:54:09,002 --> 02:54:13,089
Besides, there are far more dashing young men around here anyway.

2756
02:54:13,381 --> 02:54:17,719
I'm amazed that Gilbert could even insult you.

2757
02:54:18,011 --> 02:54:21,473
Anne! We've been looking all over for you.

2758
02:54:21,765 --> 02:54:22,867
What's second-year class like?

2759
02:54:22,891 --> 02:54:23,891
| don't know anyone.

2760
02:54:24,142 --> 02:54:25,870
| wish you people had decided to go into second.

2761
02:54:25,894 --> 02:54:28,605
Second? I'll be lucky if | pass first.

2762
02:54:28,897 --> 02:54:30,065
| don't care if | don't pass

2763
02:54:30,357 --> 02:54:32,609
my father can afford to send me back.

2764
02:54:33,026 --> 02:54:34,546
You know, Anne, Frank Stockley told me

2765
02:54:34,778 --> 02:54:36,618
that the graduate who receives the highest mark

2766
02:54:36,654 --> 02:54:38,740
in English lit this year wins the Avery scholarship.

2767
02:54:39,032 --> 02:54:41,743
$250 a year for four years.

2768
02:54:42,035 --> 02:54:43,078
Are you sure?

2769
02:54:43,411 --> 02:54:45,014
The board of governors is announcing it tomorrow.

2770
02:54:45,038 --> 02:54:46,748
It will be you for sure, Anne.

2771
02:54:47,040 --> 02:54:48,208
| don't know.

2772
02:54:48,500 --> 02:54:50,019
This is amuch bigger pond we're swimming in now

2773
02:54:50,043 --> 02:54:51,336
than in Avonlea.

2774
02:54:51,628 --> 02:54:53,630
Can you two goody-goodies control yourselves?

2775
02:54:54,047 --> 02:54:55,632
There's a lot more to do around here

2776
02:54:55,924 --> 02:54:57,717
than keeping your nose in the books.

2777
02:55:07,018 --> 02:55:09,771
So you have been here one week

2778
02:55:10,063 --> 02:55:12,023
and already you're planning to take an arts degree

2779
02:55:12,065 --> 02:55:13,775
from Redmond college.

2780
02:55:14,067 --> 02:55:15,787
| fully intend to win that Avery scholarship

2781
02:55:16,069 --> 02:55:17,779
if hard work can do it.

2782
02:55:18,071 --> 02:55:20,782
| never knew a girl with such ambition...

2783
02:55:21,074 --> 02:55:23,785
except perhaps myself.

2784
02:55:24,077 --> 02:55:26,788
But my ambition was for money.

2785
02:55:27,080 --> 02:55:29,457
God knows I've succeeded.

2786
02:55:29,749 --> 02:55:31,793
I've never really considered money.

2787
02:55:32,085 --> 02:55:34,796
Probably just as well.

2788
02:55:35,088 --> 02:55:37,799
Though | can hardly believe I'm saying so.

2789
02:55:38,091 --> 02:55:40,802
Wealth can be very empty

2790
02:55:41,094 --> 02:55:43,805
when you don't have someone to share it with,

2791
02:55:44,097 --> 02:55:48,810
but by the time | realized that, no one would have me.

2792
02:55:49,102 --> 02:55:52,814
Except men who wanted my money more than | did.

2793
02:55:53,106 --> 02:55:54,899
You aren't lonely, though, are you?

2794
02:55:55,191 --> 02:55:58,820
Not with you in town.

2795
02:55:59,112 --> 02:56:03,825
Now tell me, have you made all kinds of interesting friends

2796
02:56:04,117 --> 02:56:05,827
your first week here, then?

2797
02:56:06,119 --> 02:56:07,829
| have a small circle of friends,

2798
02:56:08,121 --> 02:56:10,832
but no bosom friends, mind you.

2799
02:56:11,124 --> 02:56:16,838
And what about young men in that circle of friends?

2800
02:56:17,130 --> 02:56:20,049
I've become too practical for romance, like, Marilla.

2801
02:56:20,341 --> 02:56:22,844
| shall probably end up an old maid.

2802
02:56:23,136 --> 02:56:25,847
(Laughs)

2803
02:56:26,139 --> 02:56:27,366
Miss Barry, | didn't mean that.

2804
02:56:27,390 --> 02:56:29,851
It isn't all bad.

2805
02:56:30,143 --> 02:56:31,863
A married woman can never be as cantankerous

2806
02:56:32,145 --> 02:56:34,856
as |am free to be.

2807
02:56:35,148 --> 02:56:39,861
But it's not a circumstance I'd recommend for you.

2808
02:56:40,153 --> 02:56:44,866
Make a little room in your plans again for romance, Anne girl.

2809
02:56:45,158 --> 02:56:47,535
All the degrees and scholarships in the world

2810
02:56:47,827 --> 02:56:50,955
can't make up for the lack of it.

2811
02:56:56,461 --> 02:56:57,855
May | leave this for Gilbert Blythe?

2812
02:56:57,879 --> 02:56:59,589
You can take it to him yourself, miss.

2813
02:56:59,881 --> 02:57:01,674
He's across the hall.

2814
02:57:03,760 --> 02:57:05,595
Thank you.

2815
02:57:05,887 --> 02:57:07,597
Can't you just picture it, Gilbert,

2816
02:57:07,889 --> 02:57:11,601
Emily Clay, winner of the Avery scholarship. (Chuckles)

2817
02:57:12,185 --> 02:57:13,978
Don't be too sure.

2818
02:57:21,069 --> 02:57:22,987
(Bell tolls)

2819
02:57:33,414 --> 02:57:36,334
(Reading) "Dear Marilla and Matthew"

2820
02:57:38,044 --> 02:57:40,880
it hardly seems possible that the term is almost over.

2821
02:57:41,172 --> 02:57:44,133
I've become so preoccupied with my work,

2822
02:57:44,425 --> 02:57:46,886
I've almost lost track of time,

2823
02:57:47,178 --> 02:57:50,974
but here | am, with exams looming up before me.

2824
02:57:51,266 --> 02:57:55,478
And for the time being they are all there is in the world.

2825
02:57:55,770 --> 02:57:58,982
But, as Rachel Lynde used to Say

2826
02:58:01,067 --> 02:58:04,195
the sun will go on rising and setting

2827
02:58:05,530 --> 02:58:08,950
whether | fail in geometry or not.

2828
02:58:09,242 --> 02:58:12,954
| think I'd rather it didn't go on if | failed.

2829
02:58:13,246 --> 02:58:16,457
| miss you both very, very much.

2830
02:58:16,749 --> 02:58:21,671
Yours lovingly with all my heart... Anne.'.

2831
02:58:21,963 --> 02:58:24,757
She sounds unhappy.

2832
02:58:29,137 --> 02:58:30,471
Oh, cheer up, Anne.

2833
02:58:30,763 --> 02:58:32,483
You have to win at least one of the awards.

2834
02:58:33,266 --> 02:58:35,393
I'm not sure | care anymore.

2835
02:58:35,685 --> 02:58:37,885
That's a fine attitude after all the work you've put in.

2836
02:58:38,104 --> 02:58:39,814
| haven't a hope for the Avery.

2837
02:58:40,106 --> 02:58:42,817
Everyone has practically said that Emily Clay is getting it.

2838
02:58:43,276 --> 02:58:44,986
You'll probably get the gold medal then.

2839
02:58:45,278 --> 02:58:47,118
Well, I'm not gonna look at the bulletin board.

2840
02:58:47,280 --> 02:58:49,449
I'm gonna go straight to the girl's dressing room.

2841
02:58:49,741 --> 02:58:51,826
I'll come find you.

2842
02:58:52,118 --> 02:58:53,661
If | fail, just say so, Jane.

2843
02:58:53,953 --> 02:58:57,999
Don't break it to me gently and don't sympathize.

2844
02:58:58,291 --> 02:59:00,269
- Hurrah, hip-hip, hurrah!
- Hurrah, hip-hip, hurrah!

2845
02:59:00,293 --> 02:59:05,006
Hurrah for Gilbert Blythe! Winner of the gold medal.

2846
02:59:05,298 --> 02:59:07,091
(Cheering)

2847
02:59:15,892 --> 02:59:18,728
Three cheers for Anne Shirley, winner of the Avery.

2848
02:59:19,020 --> 02:59:22,231
- The Avery?
- Hip-hip, hurrah!

2849
02:59:22,857 --> 02:59:27,153
Hurrah for Anne Shirley, winner of the Avery, hurrah.

2850
02:59:27,820 --> 02:59:30,114
(Cheering, applause)

2851
02:59:34,869 --> 02:59:37,121
(Wind gusts, seagulls cry)

2852
02:59:40,541 --> 02:59:44,587
Isn't that breath of mint delicious?

2853
02:59:44,879 --> 02:59:47,048
| can't bear the thought of leaving here again.

2854
02:59:47,340 --> 02:59:49,258
Four long years.

2855
02:59:49,550 --> 02:59:53,054
I'll probably be old and grey when you do come back, Anne.

2856
02:59:53,346 --> 02:59:56,057
More likely married to a dashingly handsome young man.

2857
02:59:56,349 --> 02:59:57,517
And too busy with babies

2858
02:59:57,809 --> 02:59:59,536
to be interested In your former bosom friend.

2859
02:59:59,560 --> 03:00:02,605
Such as who, Moody?

2860
03:00:02,897 --> 03:00:04,899
I'll pray that someone wonderful comes to Avonlea

2861
03:00:05,191 --> 03:00:08,820
and sweeps you off your feet.

2862
03:00:09,112 --> 03:00:12,490
Gilbert's getting the Avonlea school, you know?

2863
03:00:13,032 --> 03:00:15,076
He isn't going to college?

2864
03:00:15,368 --> 03:00:16,470
His father can't afford to send him,

2865
03:00:16,494 --> 03:00:19,247
so he's going to earn his way.

2866
03:00:19,539 --> 03:00:23,084
Did you ever explain to him?

2867
03:00:23,376 --> 03:00:26,087
Some books are better left on the shelf.

2868
03:00:26,379 --> 03:00:28,089
| wish him luck, though.

2869
03:00:28,381 --> 03:00:31,092
He's a determined young man.

2870
03:00:31,384 --> 03:00:35,179
Then as far as you're concerned, he's fair game?

2871
03:00:36,514 --> 03:00:39,350
Why, Diana Barry, if you were interested in Gilbert Blythe,

2872
03:00:39,642 --> 03:00:41,352
why didn't you ever say so?

2873
03:00:41,644 --> 03:00:45,356
Because | thought my bosom friend was in love with him.

2874
03:00:45,648 --> 03:00:49,068
In love with Gilbert Blythe, me?

2875
03:00:49,569 --> 03:00:51,988
Yes, you.

2876
03:00:52,280 --> 03:00:54,198
Gilbert did say that being smart

2877
03:00:54,490 --> 03:00:57,452
was better than being pretty.

2878
03:01:05,585 --> 03:01:06,961
Good night, dear sweet Diana.

2879
03:01:07,253 --> 03:01:09,505
Good night, Anne.

2880
03:01:20,308 --> 03:01:22,226
(Cow moos)

2881
03:01:29,317 --> 03:01:31,235
Agh!

2882
03:01:33,321 --> 03:01:35,615
Matthew, Matthew!

2883
03:01:35,907 --> 03:01:38,659
What Is it?

2884
03:01:38,951 --> 03:01:40,161
(Breathing heavily)

2885
03:01:40,453 --> 03:01:43,247
I'm all right.

2886
03:01:45,333 --> 03:01:47,168
Please, Matthew, you need help.

2887
03:01:47,460 --> 03:01:48,437
I've got to get a doctor.

2888
03:01:48,461 --> 03:01:51,172
| worked hard all my life.

2889
03:01:51,464 --> 03:01:54,801
I'd rather drop in the harness

2890
03:01:58,346 --> 03:02:00,181
| got old.

2891
03:02:00,473 --> 03:02:02,183
| never noticed.

2892
03:02:02,475 --> 03:02:04,185
If I'd been the boy you sent for,

2893
03:02:04,477 --> 03:02:07,271
| could have spared you In SO Many ways.

2894
03:02:07,563 --> 03:02:09,273
| never wanted a boy.

2895
03:02:11,150 --> 03:02:13,736
| only wanted you from... from the first day.

2896
03:02:16,364 --> 03:02:19,283
Don't ever change.

2897
03:02:20,368 --> 03:02:23,204
| love my little girl.

2898
03:02:23,496 --> 03:02:26,290
I'm so proud of my little girl.

2899
03:02:28,209 --> 03:02:31,212
Matthew, don't.

2900
03:02:31,504 --> 03:02:39,504
(Music)

2901
03:02:47,520 --> 03:02:53,901
(Music)

2902
03:02:59,740 --> 03:03:02,451
We have stood here In silent prayer

2903
03:03:02,743 --> 03:03:05,454
at Matthew Cuthbert's grave

2904
03:03:05,746 --> 03:03:09,458
and struggled, each of us, to see the meaning in his life.

2905
03:03:09,750 --> 03:03:13,337
But the mystery of death prevails.

2906
03:03:13,629 --> 03:03:16,841
All we know is that we are troubled in our hearts

2907
03:03:17,133 --> 03:03:21,387
at this evidence that death comes to all of us.

2908
03:03:21,679 --> 03:03:25,725
In the end, all we know is that we loved him.

2909
03:03:26,017 --> 03:03:28,561
And we commend his soul to Jesus.

2910
03:03:45,912 --> 03:03:46,912
(Kiss)

2911
03:04:18,486 --> 03:04:21,864
Miss Cuthbert... Anne...

2912
03:04:24,492 --> 03:04:26,410
I'm very sorry for your loss.

2913
03:04:28,496 --> 03:04:32,291
Thank you, Gilbert Blythe.

2914
03:04:45,429 --> 03:04:48,265
(Sobbing)

2915
03:05:21,549 --> 03:05:24,468
(Door creaks open, Anne sobbing)

2916
03:05:38,649 --> 03:05:41,736
There now, oh, dear.

2917
03:05:43,571 --> 03:05:46,490
It won't bring him back.

2918
03:05:47,575 --> 03:05:51,579
Keep your arms around me, Marilla, for a little while.

2919
03:05:51,871 --> 03:05:54,832
Tears don't hurt like the ache does.

2920
03:05:57,585 --> 03:06:01,422
| know I've been strict with you.

2921
03:06:01,714 --> 03:06:05,509
| don't know what I'd do if you'd never come.

2922
03:06:08,179 --> 03:06:09,899
But you mustn't think that | don't love you

2923
03:06:09,972 --> 03:06:12,183
as much as Matthew did.

2924
03:06:14,602 --> 03:06:17,938
It's never been easy for me to say

2925
03:06:19,607 --> 03:06:23,152
the things from my heart, but...

2926
03:06:26,614 --> 03:06:29,533
You're like my own flesh and blood now.

2927
03:06:32,787 --> 03:06:34,622
(Crying)

2928
03:06:35,748 --> 03:06:37,541
It's not right to cry so.

2929
03:06:39,168 --> 03:06:41,087
God knows best.

2930
03:06:43,297 --> 03:06:48,260
Oh, but he was always such a kind brother to me.

2931
03:06:50,638 --> 03:06:52,973
We have each other now.

2932
03:06:55,643 --> 03:06:57,520
(Sighs)

2933
03:06:58,729 --> 03:07:02,358
Yes, yes, yes.

2934
03:07:03,651 --> 03:07:11,651
(Music)

2935
03:07:19,792 --> 03:07:21,585
(Cart rattles)

2936
03:07:25,506 --> 03:07:27,550
Whoa, whoa.

2937
03:07:29,009 --> 03:07:31,262
'Morning, Miss Cuthbert.

2938
03:07:31,554 --> 03:07:33,514
And how is Green Gables holding up these days?

2939
03:07:33,806 --> 03:07:35,516
Oh, pretty well.

2940
03:07:35,808 --> 03:07:39,186
| haven't seen you around these parts much lately.

2941
03:07:39,520 --> 03:07:43,274
Well, business in Carmody takes all my time.

2942
03:07:43,816 --> 03:07:47,528
You know what | mean.

2943
03:07:47,820 --> 03:07:52,533
(Sighs) This sure is a lovely piece of countryside.

2944
03:07:52,825 --> 03:07:54,535
It is that.

2945
03:07:54,827 --> 03:07:59,540
People in Avonlea say that it's the prettiest acreage on the north shore.

2946
03:07:59,832 --> 03:08:03,878
Matthew kept this place up impeccably.

2947
03:08:04,170 --> 03:08:07,923
You don't want to let it get run down at all,

2948
03:08:08,215 --> 03:08:09,550
decreases the value.

2949
03:08:09,842 --> 03:08:13,554
Now may be a good time to consider selling

2950
03:08:13,846 --> 03:08:16,557
if you want to get the highest value for your property.

2951
03:08:16,849 --> 03:08:21,645
Well, | can't deny that the thought has crossed my mind.

2952
03:08:21,937 --> 03:08:27,193
You could certainly retire on what I'm prepared to offer you for the place.

2953
03:08:27,484 --> 03:08:30,112
Thank you, Mr. Sadler.

2954
03:08:42,750 --> 03:08:45,586
What did Mr. Sadler want?

2955
03:08:45,878 --> 03:08:47,588
He once offered to buy Green Gables,

2956
03:08:47,880 --> 03:08:50,466
and he's still interested.

2957
03:08:50,758 --> 03:08:53,969
Buy Green Gables? Marilla.

2958
03:08:54,261 --> 03:08:56,597
| don't know what else to do.

2959
03:08:56,889 --> 03:08:58,599
My eyesight is getting weaker.

2960
03:08:58,891 --> 03:09:01,602
Dr. Spencer says that if the headaches persist,

2961
03:09:01,894 --> 03:09:04,605
| might lose it completely.

2962
03:09:04,897 --> 03:09:07,608
What if | can't run this place?

2963
03:09:07,900 --> 03:09:10,903
Rachel has kindly offered to let me live with her.

2964
03:09:11,195 --> 03:09:12,613
But you can't sell Green Gables.

2965
03:09:12,905 --> 03:09:14,740
Anne, | would go crazy if there was trouble

2966
03:09:15,032 --> 03:09:17,618
and | was all alone here.

2967
03:09:17,910 --> 03:09:22,623
I'm sorry that you won't have a home to come to on your vacations.

2968
03:09:22,915 --> 03:09:24,625
| never thought I'd live to see the day

2969
03:09:24,917 --> 03:09:28,629
when | would sell this place.

2970
03:09:28,921 --> 03:09:32,091
But we'll survive somehow.

2971
03:09:33,801 --> 03:09:35,636
You won't have to stay here alone.

2972
03:09:35,928 --> 03:09:38,639
I'm not going to Redmond.

2973
03:09:38,931 --> 03:09:40,641
What do you mean?

2974
03:09:40,933 --> 03:09:42,643
I'm not going to take that scholarship.

2975
03:09:42,935 --> 03:09:45,896
I've already decided, but | hadn't told you yet.

2976
03:09:46,188 --> 03:09:48,649
Mr. Barry said he'd run our fields next year.

2977
03:09:48,941 --> 03:09:50,126
And I'm going to take the school at Carmody.

2978
03:09:50,150 --> 03:09:51,150
They need a teacher

2979
03:09:51,402 --> 03:09:53,112
and I'm sure they'll be glad to have me.

2980
03:09:53,404 --> 03:09:55,524
| can drive back and forth until the weather gets bad.

2981
03:09:55,614 --> 03:09:57,092
And then I'll board and come home on the weekends.

2982
03:09:57,116 --> 03:09:59,827
| won't let you sacrifice your education for me.

2983
03:10:00,327 --> 03:10:02,037
| won't allow it, Anne Shirley.

2984
03:10:02,413 --> 03:10:04,123
Lam going to do it.

2985
03:10:04,415 --> 03:10:08,419
I'm 16-years-old and just as stubborn as you are.

2986
03:10:12,381 --> 03:10:14,758
(Laughs) Ohl!

2987
03:10:17,594 --> 03:10:20,431
You blessed girl.

2988
03:10:20,973 --> 03:10:24,435
| know | ought to stick to it and make you go to college.

2989
03:10:24,768 --> 03:10:28,188
But I've learned better than to stand in your way.

2990
03:10:31,859 --> 03:10:35,696
Gilbert Blythe will be teaching too, won't he?

2991
03:10:35,988 --> 03:10:36,989
Yes.

2992
03:10:37,281 --> 03:10:41,702
What a nice-looking young boy he is.

2993
03:10:41,994 --> 03:10:44,705
He looks a lot like his father did at that age.

2994
03:10:44,997 --> 03:10:48,709
We used to be real good friends, he and if.

2995
03:10:49,001 --> 03:10:51,795
People called him my beau.

2996
03:10:53,881 --> 03:10:57,718
And what happened?

2997
03:10:58,010 --> 03:11:01,722
We quarreled, and | wouldn't forgive him when he asked me to.

2998
03:11:02,014 --> 03:11:05,476
| wanted to after a while, but | was stubborn.

2999
03:11:05,768 --> 03:11:07,811
And | wanted to punish him first.

3000
03:11:09,897 --> 03:11:12,358
He never came back.

3001
03:11:12,649 --> 03:11:15,903
|... always felt rather sorry.

3002
03:11:17,905 --> 03:11:23,619
|... sort of wish that I'd forgiven him

3003
03:11:24,661 --> 03:11:26,580
when | had a chance.

3004
03:11:30,501 --> 03:11:32,336
Glad to hear you've come to your senses, Anne.

3005
03:11:32,628 --> 03:11:35,339
Teacher's course one year, and off to Redmond the next.

3006
03:11:35,631 --> 03:11:38,342
| don't believe in women going off to college with the men,

3007
03:11:38,634 --> 03:11:41,929
cramming their heads full of Latin and Greek.

3008
03:11:42,221 --> 03:11:46,100
I'm doing my courses by correspondence, Rachel.

3009
03:11:46,558 --> 03:11:47,786
With all the work you have to do?

3010
03:11:47,810 --> 03:11:49,871
Teaching over at Carmody and looking after Green Gables?

3011
03:11:49,895 --> 03:11:51,522
Marilla, talk some sense to the girl.

3012
03:11:51,814 --> 03:11:53,774
Oh, mind your own business, for once, Rachel,

3013
03:11:54,066 --> 03:11:56,527
and leave her alone.

3014
03:11:56,819 --> 03:11:58,612
Anne thrives on studying.

3015
03:12:04,952 --> 03:12:07,788
Well, Marilla, she'll kill herself,

3016
03:12:08,080 --> 03:12:09,123
that's all there is to it.

3017
03:12:09,415 --> 03:12:11,875
Pride goes before the fall.

3018
03:12:13,961 --> 03:12:21,961
(Music)

3019
03:12:29,101 --> 03:12:37,101
(Music)

3020
03:12:45,117 --> 03:12:49,872
- Whoa, whoa.
- Hello, Anne.

3021
03:12:50,622 --> 03:12:53,417
Taking a short cut, Mr. Blythe?

3022
03:12:57,421 --> 03:13:01,341
Miss Cuthbert said | could find you here.

3023
03:13:05,012 --> 03:13:07,931
Open it.

3024
03:13:11,518 --> 03:13:12,519
Ahem.

3025
03:13:14,396 --> 03:13:16,636
(Reading) "We would be prepared to agree to your proposal

3026
03:13:16,732 --> 03:13:18,442
to engage Miss Shirley

3027
03:13:18,734 --> 03:13:22,446
under contract for one year in the post of teacher

3028
03:13:22,738 --> 03:13:25,449
at Avonlea public school."

3029
03:13:25,741 --> 03:13:27,451
That's your post.

3030
03:13:27,743 --> 03:13:29,995
Well, | took the liberty of speaking

3031
03:13:30,287 --> 03:13:32,873
to the trustees about an exchange.

3032
03:13:33,165 --> 03:13:34,875
I'd be getting Carmody,

3033
03:13:35,167 --> 03:13:37,836
and you can stay at Green Gables.

3034
03:13:39,046 --> 03:13:41,757
| don't know what to Say.

3035
03:13:42,049 --> 03:13:43,884
Don't say anything.

3036
03:13:44,176 --> 03:13:45,886
But you'd have to pay for your board.

3037
03:13:46,178 --> 03:13:48,096
You'd never save enough for college you can't...

3038
03:13:48,388 --> 03:13:49,973
I'll save enough.

3039
03:13:50,516 --> 03:13:53,977
Besides, I'm keeping up my studies by correspondence.

3040
03:13:54,770 --> 03:13:56,522
Soaml.

3041
03:14:01,068 --> 03:14:04,196
Thank you for giving up the school for me, Gilbert.

3042
03:14:04,488 --> 03:14:06,907
It was very good of you.

3043
03:14:07,199 --> 03:14:09,993
| want you to know that | really appreciate it.

3044
03:14:11,078 --> 03:14:14,748
Well, | figure you can give me a hand with my work,

3045
03:14:15,040 --> 03:14:17,918
and we'll call it a fair exchange.

3046
03:14:18,210 --> 03:14:19,795
Aren't you worried I'm liable

3047
03:14:20,087 --> 03:14:23,298
to break another slate over your head?

3048
03:14:23,590 --> 03:14:26,927
I'm more worried | might break one over yours...

3049
03:14:27,219 --> 03:14:30,013
Carrots.

3050
03:14:35,018 --> 03:14:37,020
I'll walk you home.

